WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.730
Tim Ferriss: Dr. Williams.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:08.220
Good to see you, sir.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.620
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, thanks man.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:24.470
Thanks for having me.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:39.980
Tim Ferriss: And I thought we would start
with a personal story, not your personal story,

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.980
but a story of Deirdre Lehman.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:48.050
Could you tell us who this person is, how
it fits into your story?

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.300
But let's begin with just a description of
Deirdre.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.780
Dr. Nolan Williams: Absolutely.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:58.680
So Deirdre is, I think, maybe in her 50s,
60s, female, in Bay Area, who has suffered

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:08.140
from bipolar disorder much of her life, and
pretty successfully treated for the mania

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.780
side of things over the years.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.900
Had a psychiatrist taking care of that part
in Marin, and happened to slip into this pretty

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.670
severe depressive episode a couple of years
back.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.729
Guess it's been, maybe like four or five years
now.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:27.220
And her psychiatrist had actually gone to
see a talk that I gave at this mood disorders

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.369
day, the year before.

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:34.299
And we were talking, it was really early on,
when we were working on a rapid-acting neurostimulation

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:35.299
approach.

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:40.909
The psychiatrist had heard the talk and then
her patient fell into this really bad suicidal

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:41.909
depression.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:46.480
And so she reached out to me to treat her,
and I got on the phone, I'll never forget,

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.670
it was a Wednesday.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.430
And I got on the phone with her psychiatrist
and she was telling me symptomatically how

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.430
bad off she was.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:56.670
And I was like, "I don't think we can treat
her at outpatient.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:57.960
She's way too ill.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.840
I think she needs to go in the inpatient hospital."

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:02.840
So essentially, gave her some information
on how to do that.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.350
So I see her the next morning and she's in
really bad shape.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:07.710
Tim Ferriss: What does that mean?

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.600
Does that mean like she's —
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.630
Tim Ferriss: How did that show up?

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:16.170
Dr. Nolan Williams: When people are at the
level where they definitively need to go into

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:22.430
the hospital, they're not really totally communicative
anymore, and they've got some cognitive issues

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:23.430
sometimes.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.950
So in her case, she couldn't look you in the
eyes.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.830
Look at the ground and she was doing this
rocking thing, which you can see in pretty

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.120
severe depression.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.700
It's kind of this catatonia overlap symptoms.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.790
She's at the very end of the spectrum, one
of the highest severity patients we've ever

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:38.790
treated.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:43.300
So she was like, a score of 50, on the moderate
side of 60, very, very severe, and just rocking

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.610
and not really talking.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:46.610
And the husband was counting everything.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:52.220
And she had bipolar one, so she was hypomanic,
I think or manic two weeks before and then

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:54.550
dropped into this very, very severe depression.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:58.349
So it was her daughter and the husband, and
they're sitting in the room with me and they

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:03.680
want me to treat her, and I say, "Listen,
it's Friday, we go Monday to Friday.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.470
You have to find a way basically to keep her
well from now until Monday.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:09.470
And that means…"

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:10.500
Tim Ferriss: And by well, you mean safe?

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.500
Preventing self harm?

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.290
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.500
So keep her not having a suicide attempt basically
from now until Monday.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.500
She's very suicidal.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:26.020
And I said, "You're going to have to take
every knife.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.890
I don't think any guns, but gun, chemical,
scissors, everything out of the house, all

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:29.890
of it has to go.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:34.319
And you guys have to be on a 24-hour watch
until Monday."

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:39.069
And so Monday morning rolls around and we
bring her in.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:43.340
And the craziest thing, we had an error, a
repair on the motor threshold coil, which

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:50.280
is the coil you use to get calibrated on the
intensity, and it shorted out the device and

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.050
blew the capacitor bank up on the first stimulator.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:55.150
Tim Ferriss: Blew your flux capacitor.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.450
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:57.450
Yeah.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.450
At 7 a.m.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.090
And you can't imagine how stressful that was.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.819
So we had a second machine, and I'll tell
you about this later.

00:04:03.819 --> 00:04:09.830
We were running this trait, hypnotizability
modulation study, and it was over at the scanner,

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.709
so it was pretty far away, and these things
weigh like a hundred pounds.

00:04:12.709 --> 00:04:17.479
So I had to send my team over there, run over
there and grab it, bring it over.

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:20.900
And luckily we were able to get her going
and treat her with a second machine.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:25.590
And she was in really, really bad shape that
morning.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.660
And by five o'clock that afternoon, she was
basically normal.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:34.420
And the next morning she was totally zeroed
out and completely normal.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:35.860
Tim Ferriss: Meaning no suicidality?

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.110
Dr. Nolan Williams: Meaning no depression,
no nothing.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:43.440
She looked like any person walking the street.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.440
Totally normal.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.460
And that was in 24 hours.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.550
We've seen this with bipolar patients quicker.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.710
It'll happen really quick for a bipolar one
patient.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.300
You can get it done sometimes in a day.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.930
Tim Ferriss: Just for clarity, by "Get it
done," and don't worry people listening, we're

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.419
going to define terms and get into all this,
but you're talking about accelerated TMS?

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:01.940
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, we're talking about
accelerated TMS.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:07.990
So our rapid-acting neurostimulation approach,
we're able to get people out of these states

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:13.280
and into normal mood, and in short periods
of time, generally 2.6 days on average for

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.270
major depression patients, but it's quicker
with bipolar we've seen, especially with bipolar

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:18.270
one patients.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.583
So she was totally out of it in 24 hours.

00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:24.090
And I remember it was right around July 4th
or something.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.420
And so the whole team left.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:28.760
And I guess we'll maybe talk about caffeine
later too.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:33.100
So my wife and I were like big Philz Coffee
fans, and so I'll never forget this either.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:38.430
So we go down to the Philz Coffee in Palo
Alto after I saw them.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:39.430
And I'll never forget it.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:43.120
Clark Lehman, her husband, also went to Philz,
didn't know I was going to be there.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.980
And I look over and this guy's just kind of
staring at me, and I was like, "Hey, how are

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:48.980
you?

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:49.980
Good to see you again."

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:55.080
I just saw the guy 10 minutes ago, and he's
like, "I still don't understand what happened."

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:01.389
And it makes sense, to take somebody from
the worst you've ever seen them mood-wise

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:06.710
to normal in such a short period of time was
remarkable for him.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:09.699
And it ended up being that after that period,
they actually went out and really were helpful

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:14.060
with a lot of the philanthropy that led to
all the trials being funded and ultimately

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:19.680
the clearance, and Clark and Deirdre really
were advocates and have continued to be advocates

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.910
for this to kind of get it out into the world.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:26.550
And it was totally based off of that experience
of basically him feeling helpless and going

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.840
from that to feeling like it was all solved.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:34.271
And I think she went maybe a year completely
asymptomatic, ended up needing to get retreated

00:06:34.271 --> 00:06:38.590
again at some point, but gets these little
touch-ups here and there and is able to stay

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:44.030
well ongoing, and, as they tell me, depression's
not her problem anymore.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.030
And so that's good.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:50.680
She's a great illustrative case of what this
can do and I think what the promise of it

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:51.680
can be.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:57.860
Tim Ferriss: What I'd love to talk about next
is not necessarily direct mechanism of action,

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:03.390
but I'd love to hear you just explain a snippet
that I pulled from your conversation with

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:07.090
Andrew Huberman, which was a very good conversation.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:10.940
Specifically it was about, and I'm not going
to use the right terminology here, so bear

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:19.910
with me as a lay person, but the sequencing
or abnormal/pathological sequencing of activation

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:22.009
or activity in different parts of the brain.

00:07:22.009 --> 00:07:23.349
So I don't know if it's the anterior cingulate.

00:07:23.349 --> 00:07:24.550
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yep.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:26.990
Tim Ferriss: I think you know what I'm alluding
to here.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:28.889
Would you mind just explaining that?

00:07:28.889 --> 00:07:33.530
Because it was something that I had never
been exposed to and I found it deeply fascinating.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:38.199
And I'll just also mention this context for
people who are listening to this, that part

00:07:38.199 --> 00:07:43.150
of what is deeply interesting to me about
a number of the different tools and modalities

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:50.680
that you explore in depth, is not just the
speed of action, but the durability of effect.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:56.780
Super, super potent combination and very unusual,
from what I can tell, combination of things.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:57.780
All right.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:02.080
So as far as the sequence of activation goes,
could you explain what I'm referring to?

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.300
Dr. Nolan Williams: We published a paper in
the Proceedings of the National Academy of

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.860
Science, I guess it's been about six months
ago now.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:15.210
So one of the former research track resident
postdocs in the psychiatry program at Stanford,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:20.700
Anish Mitra, who's now junior faculty, was
working with Karl Deisseroth and I during

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.700
that training phase.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:30.020
And Anish had this interest in a specific
way of looking at brain imaging, particularly

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:34.050
this type of brain imaging called resting-state
functional connectivity MRI.

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:38.839
And so resting-state functional connectivity
MRI has been around for a long time.

00:08:38.839 --> 00:08:44.351
The resting state part of it is basically
tell the person sit in the MRI scanner and

00:08:44.351 --> 00:08:46.279
let their mind wander.

00:08:46.279 --> 00:08:49.230
So that's kind of the resting state or the
default mode, however you want to think about

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.230
it.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:55.690
And functional connectivity, what that means
is it's the brain regions that have blood

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:58.769
flow that is time-locked with each other.

00:08:58.769 --> 00:09:04.980
And so essentially these connected brain networks,
the blood flow will go up or go down in those

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.209
areas in a time-locked fashion and blood flow
is —

00:09:08.209 --> 00:09:10.380
Tim Ferriss: By time-locked, you just mean
at the same time?

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:11.850
Dr. Nolan Williams: At the same time.

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:12.850
Tim Ferriss: Correlating.

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:13.850
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, exactly.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:14.850
Yeah.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.800
And so why blood flow?

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.060
Blood flow is a surrogate of electrical activity.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:26.540
It's hard to see electrical activity deep
in the brain.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:32.310
People see it at the surface of the EEG, but
with MRI, you use the blood flow as a surrogate

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:38.420
of the electrical activity, and it makes sense,
if you're using glucose in a brain region

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:45.470
using that network, then you need to have
cerebral auto-regulation, so the blood vessels

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.940
increase and they dilate more blood goes into
that area.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.730
So it's just like a response to increased
activity.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:56.620
And so you have these increases in blood flow
that are supposed to represent electrical

00:09:56.620 --> 00:10:01.810
activity that are in different separate nodes
in the brain, and they come on roughly at

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:02.810
the same time.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:03.810
And we've known that for a while.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:11.470
Anish got very interested in this idea that
the timing of the blood flow is consistently

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:15.049
temporally offset between these nodes.

00:10:15.049 --> 00:10:20.110
So slightly, that people ignored it for a
long time, but he was able through using various

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:27.660
math, able to show that there's a slight offset
of the timing such that one brain region slightly

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:29.339
comes on before the other.

00:10:29.339 --> 00:10:33.050
And that's interesting because that infers
some level of causality.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:36.790
And so instead of the whole network coming
on at the same time, maybe it's just one area

00:10:36.790 --> 00:10:41.670
and it's signaling the whole network on, and
it's so quick that you see it as all like

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.589
this, but really it's more like this, if that
makes sense.

00:10:44.589 --> 00:10:46.720
It's coming on all at the same time.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:50.670
But from this network node kind of turning
this one on, turning that one on or something

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:51.670
like that.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:52.670
Tim Ferriss: The lead domino matters.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:53.670
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's right.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:54.670
The lead domino.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:55.670
Exactly.

00:10:55.670 --> 00:11:00.029
And so in the case of mood, it looks like
the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, the area

00:11:00.029 --> 00:11:06.829
that's involved in control, precedes slightly
the cingulate cortex.

00:11:06.829 --> 00:11:13.940
In our normal healthy control population,
essentially nearly everybody had that directionality.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:22.170
In the depressed cohort, 70 percent of them
had it flipped where the cingulate was temporally

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.760
in front of the dorsolateral, but not everybody.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:29.860
And if you just had that information, then
you wouldn't know what to make of that.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.700
Why is it some people and not others?

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:38.269
But what was interesting is when he looked
at the folks that had it versus the ones that

00:11:38.269 --> 00:11:42.100
didn't, the ones that had it were the ones
that were responsive to our — when we'll

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:47.290
talk about what SAINT is and the rapid-acting
neural stimulation approach in detail, I guess,

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:48.290
later.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:54.279
But the folks that responded to SAINT responded
to this rapid-acting TMS approach were the

00:11:54.279 --> 00:12:00.750
ones that had the biomarker and the ones that
had no change, did not have the biomarker

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.459
and looked like a normal healthy control.

00:12:03.459 --> 00:12:10.300
And the signal on the post scan flipped to
look normal in the folks that responded had

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:14.520
the biomarker, and then their brain changed
after.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:20.990
And so the post-scan looked just like the
pre-scan on the folks that didn't clinically

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:22.500
change in the normal healthy controls.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.449
And so we see this sort of test all the time
in medicine.

00:12:25.449 --> 00:12:31.620
10 people come into the primary care doctor's
office with blurry vision, urinating a lot,

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:33.779
drinking a lot, headache.

00:12:33.779 --> 00:12:38.540
A lot of those folks probably have diabetes,
but not all of them.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:46.019
Some of them have migraine headache and need
glasses and some other things, and it looks

00:12:46.019 --> 00:12:48.100
like the diabetes presentation.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:52.310
But when you go and do the blood sugar, the
blood sugar is normal.

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:55.870
And then the folks that have elevated blood
sugar that look like they have diabetes, you

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.930
go and you give them a diabetic medicine and
then it normalizes.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:02.889
So the blood sugar after looks like the blood
sugar of a normal healthy, and it looks like

00:13:02.889 --> 00:13:07.399
the blood sugar of somebody that symptomatically
presented but didn't have diabetes.

00:13:07.399 --> 00:13:12.730
And so it was nice to see this and we're replicating
this now.

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:17.220
We have money from the National Institutes
of Health to do that, but this idea that we're

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:21.980
able to have a test that would change, and
the same thing that signals that there's an

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:24.029
abnormality is the thing that changes later.

00:13:24.029 --> 00:13:29.600
And that's more rare in psychiatry to be able
to have all of that line up.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:32.380
So we're pretty excited about that and hope
to see it.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:37.820
It does replicate in the larger population
of patients, but as a conceptual idea, it's

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:43.410
an important conceptual idea, this general
idea of being able to use neuroimaging or

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:48.690
whatever it is, EG, whatever it is, to type
different people that are presenting with

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:55.019
similar symptoms and be able to say, "Okay,
you're going to respond to this and not this,

00:13:55.019 --> 00:13:57.209
or vice versa."

00:13:57.209 --> 00:14:02.580
And I think that's part of what we need for
psychiatry, right?

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:08.459
Because people spend just so much time in
their lives trying to find the answer, and

00:14:08.459 --> 00:14:09.459
we don't really have any tests.

00:14:09.459 --> 00:14:10.459
Tim Ferriss: Trial and error.

00:14:10.459 --> 00:14:11.459
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it's a lot of trial
and error.

00:14:11.459 --> 00:14:16.139
Tim Ferriss: It's like pharmaceutical ready,
fire, aim, in a sense.

00:14:16.139 --> 00:14:20.570
And this raises, I guess just a meta observation/question.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:26.149
Perhaps you could just discuss this briefly,
which is, it seems to me like there have been,

00:14:26.149 --> 00:14:29.660
and this is part of the impetus for us having
this conversation, part of the impetus for

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:36.630
me in the last, let's call it half year, paying
a lot of attention to accelerated TMS, which

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:44.449
is there have been these dominant paradigms
in certain types of, let's call it psychiatric

00:14:44.449 --> 00:14:47.600
treatments, for things like depression — treatment-resistant
depression.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:52.910
And it seems like a very dominant paradigm
for a period of perhaps several decades has

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:58.329
been these chemical imbalance theory of psychiatric
disorders.

00:14:58.329 --> 00:15:02.680
So you have a serotonin issue, therefore we're
going to treat it in these following ways.

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:06.080
You have such and such an issue, so we're
going to give you SNRIs.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:11.139
And then like you said, it's a lot of trial
and error to figure out what works.

00:15:11.139 --> 00:15:15.790
And even if something works, it may often
only work for a period of time.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:20.120
So it seems to me, and I want you to absolutely
fact check and correct everything that I'm

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.800
saying, but that part of the reason that the
research you're doing and that others are

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:30.310
engaging in is so fascinating is that it presents
an alternative paradigm through which you

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:32.470
could look at certain disorders, right?

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:34.389
Like, oh, wait a second.

00:15:34.389 --> 00:15:39.980
Well, maybe this person's car, when you turn
the ignition, is just tripping things in the

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.480
wrong order.

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:46.340
And if that's causal, we could try to address
that and then maybe that addresses what we

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:48.680
might've otherwise perceived as a chemical
disorder.

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:52.570
I have a lot of follow-up questions, but is
that a helpful way to think about this?

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:54.020
Or what would you add to that?

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:57.320
Dr. Nolan Williams: I think there's been,
I would argue three eras in psychiatry, what

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:03.259
friends of mine have called psychiatry 1.0,
2.0, 3.0, and the first era was this era in

00:16:03.259 --> 00:16:04.259
and around Freud.

00:16:04.259 --> 00:16:11.590
And this idea that it was a content issue
and a life experience issue, which is partially

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:12.590
true.

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:14.090
It's not that that's not true, it's just not
complete.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:20.490
And so then the solution is a content solution,
a initially psychoanalysis all the way through

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:23.370
to modern forms of psychotherapy.

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:27.690
The limitations of that led us to psychiatry
2.0, right?

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:32.450
This idea that we serendipitously found the
first antipsychotics, first antidepressants,

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:40.220
and we were able to deinstitutionalize primarily
schizophrenia patients out of inpatient asylum

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:46.040
stays with these drugs, which kind of flew
in the face of this being a content issue.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:47.889
Tim Ferriss: What was the first antipsychotic?

00:16:47.889 --> 00:16:48.889
Dr. Nolan Williams: Thorazine.

00:16:48.889 --> 00:16:49.889
Tim Ferriss: I was going to say Thorazine.

00:16:49.889 --> 00:16:50.889
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:16:50.889 --> 00:16:52.829
Tim Ferriss: How was that — I don't want
to take you off track.

00:16:52.829 --> 00:16:54.579
So keep track of where we are.

00:16:54.579 --> 00:16:55.579
We're at 2.0.

00:16:55.579 --> 00:16:56.759
How was it serendipitously discovered?

00:16:56.759 --> 00:16:58.203
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, I think, if I remember
correctly, I think it was they were trying

00:16:58.203 --> 00:17:02.310
to — it was like an antibiotic or something
like that, and they were trying to develop

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:08.230
that drug out for something completely unrelated
and happened to give it to some patients with

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:09.230
schizophrenia.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:10.230
And they had a dramatic improvement.

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:13.090
Tim Ferriss: I would love to read a book that
is just a collection of case studies like

00:17:13.090 --> 00:17:14.090
this, right?

00:17:14.090 --> 00:17:15.090
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:17.660
Tim Ferriss: It's like sildenafil, Viagra,
it's like for angina or whatever.

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:18.660
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's right.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.610
Tim Ferriss: And then the male patients are
like, "Why aren't the male patients sending

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:21.610
their meds back?

00:17:21.610 --> 00:17:22.610
Oh, wait a second."

00:17:22.610 --> 00:17:23.610
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's right.

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:24.610
Tim Ferriss: Here we go.

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:25.610
Fascinating.

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:26.610
All right, so right.

00:17:26.610 --> 00:17:33.679
So Thorazine and the serendipitous discovery
of — oh, wait a fucking second.

00:17:33.679 --> 00:17:39.960
This seems to not necessarily negate, but
certainly render incomplete this pre-existing

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:40.960
paradigm.

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:41.960
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's right.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:42.960
Tim Ferriss: And then where does it go from
there?

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:43.960
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:45.480
So then I think, to your point, there was
this accumulate kind of accumulation of assumptions

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:51.250
around, well, if we're moving all of these
chemicals around in the brain, then it must

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:54.460
be that there's a deficiency or an imbalance
or whatever.

00:17:54.460 --> 00:18:01.140
And that I think led us to recent history
where there's quite a bit of prescribing of

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:04.000
oral antidepressants and all that stuff.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:09.360
And the third era, this circuit era that I
think we're in now, and I'd argue we were

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:14.640
entering in 10 years ago, but I think we're
pretty squarely at the beginning of now, flies

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:16.140
in the face of that.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:21.210
If I can take a patient as severe as Deirdre
Lehman, get her out of it in a very quick

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:27.640
timeframe and looking normal and holding that
for a long time, and there was no chemical

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:33.880
exchange, there's nothing that went into her
system, then it gets you into this newer way

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:34.880
of thinking about it.

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:36.380
It's a circuit problem.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:43.920
The useful thing about this framing, one,
it's seemingly consistently true in the sense

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:50.309
that through all the various modalities seeing
these differences, but more importantly, it

00:18:50.309 --> 00:18:54.679
lets you integrate past ideas into that concept.

00:18:54.679 --> 00:19:00.000
Drugs act on circuits, therapy acts on circuits,
but focal neuromodulation is a really direct

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:01.909
way of acting on those same circuits.

00:19:01.909 --> 00:19:07.740
And so from a patient standpoint, I think
it's very empowering because we're not saying

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:12.110
to the patient there's something inherently
missing or too much for you in the sense that

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:19.720
you're constrained to having to take these
exogenous chemicals to stay well, but rather

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:23.800
there's a circuit, there's a miswiring, misfiring
sort of problem.

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:29.679
And if we can reroute that information, then
you can feel well.

00:19:29.679 --> 00:19:33.470
And I think that there's a level of empowerment
that comes with that.

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:39.460
One of the things that patients always tell
me after they get well with some of our stimulation

00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:44.780
approaches is that they kind of look at it
and say, "Well, I may get depressed again,

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:49.320
but I don't think I'll ever get suicidal at
that same level again, because I know that

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:52.780
I've got a way of getting out of it and it's
my own volition to choose to do that, and

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:54.670
it's something I can tolerate and I feel normal."

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:58.510
Tim Ferriss: So I'd like to highlight that
last part because, not that I'm the world's

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:04.400
foremost expert in suicidal ideation, but
as someone who came very close to offing himself

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:13.100
in college and really just by a series of
lucky events ended up not fulfilling that,

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:14.100
it's the hopelessness.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:17.570
For me and for a lot of people, it's the feeling
that nothing can fix this.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:18.570
I am broken.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:20.240
I'm permanently broken.

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:25.070
There is no option other than trying to silence
this voice in my head.

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:28.840
And the only way I can think of doing that
is by ending my life.

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:36.440
But once you see, once you experience, even
more so, something that alleviates that, especially

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:40.909
with any type of durability, doesn't need
to be forever, but some type of durability,

00:20:40.909 --> 00:20:51.059
and especially if it's rapid-acting, then
you feel like you have a plan B, and that

00:20:51.059 --> 00:20:52.059
is incredibly empowering.

00:20:52.059 --> 00:20:55.150
Let me ask you a few questions.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:21:02.630
The first is this type of neuromodulation,
is that synonymous with a term that I came

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:07.150
across, I don't know who coined it, it's a
nice term, electroceuticals, or are those

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:08.150
different?

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:10.320
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, I think it's a broad
— yeah, it's part of that broader term of

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:11.320
electroceuticals.

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:12.320
Yeah.

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:15.230
And so what we had done, I don't know if this
is a good time to get into it.

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:16.230
Tim Ferriss: Let's do it.

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:17.230
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:21.340
What we had done with what we called SAINT,
or Stanford Accelerated Intelligent Neuromodulation

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:28.059
Therapy, is that we came up with a way of
reorganizing conventional TMS, which had been

00:21:28.059 --> 00:21:32.850
around for some time, reorganizing it in time
and in space.

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:39.799
And so with conventional RTMS, it had developed
in the mid-'80s, first used as a therapeutic

00:21:39.799 --> 00:21:46.290
within clinical trials by my mentor Mark George
when he was at NIH in the mid-'90s and approved

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:55.280
by the FDA in the mid to late 2000s, it utilized
average skull positions to find an average

00:21:55.280 --> 00:22:00.260
spot to stimulate, which at the time, given
the technology that was available, that was

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:01.260
the right call.

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:02.260
Right.

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:04.000
Tim Ferriss: Can I pause for one second just
to give some additional context?

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:05.000
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:06.000
Tim Ferriss: What does TMS stand for?

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.160
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, so transcranial
magnetic stimulation.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:09.700
So it's this —
Tim Ferriss: And it was originally developed

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:10.700
for what?

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:14.240
Dr. Nolan Williams: As a motor probe by Tony
Barker in the UK.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:17.929
And the idea is this idea of Faraday's law.

00:22:17.929 --> 00:22:23.580
So Faraday's law is this idea that you pulse
a magnet, you pulse a magnet, you can generate

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:25.970
current and electrically conducting substances.

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:29.799
So if I take an electromagnet, if I take a
TMS machine to the beach and I try to pulse

00:22:29.799 --> 00:22:35.970
the sand, nothing's going to happen because
sand is not, as you know, electrically conducting

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:36.970
at all.

00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:37.970
It's an insulator.

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:45.550
And so if you put a TMS coil or any electromagnet
next to a wire, a copper wire, or speaker

00:22:45.550 --> 00:22:50.080
wire or whatever, you can generate current
in that wire.

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:58.860
If you put the coil on the head, it will bypass
the skin, scalp, skull, and induce current

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:03.960
in the electrically conducting substance in
the brain, the kind of brain tissue.

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:10.120
And so you're able to selectively turn on
cortical neurons without really interacting

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:12.490
with much of the rest of the head.

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:18.650
People do feel something because of the nerves
in the scalp, but your brain can't feel anything.

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:20.590
So that's like scalp nerves.

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:23.850
And so if you, as they did in the '80s, just
kind of send a single pulse, it doesn't really

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.409
change the brain, but you can probe the brain.

00:23:26.409 --> 00:23:31.450
So I could take that coil from the mid-'80s
and I could put it over my hand representation,

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:35.799
make my thumb move, I can put it over my wrist
representation in my brain, I can make my

00:23:35.799 --> 00:23:36.799
wrist move.

00:23:36.799 --> 00:23:43.630
And it's organized in this stereotyped way
such that essentially the head face area is

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:49.360
closer to the ear and you can march up to
the midline of the skulls, such that when

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:53.250
you get to the midline, you're able to actually
move the foot and the leg.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:55.980
If you have a certain kind of coil, you can
do that.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:24:00.380
And so you can actually probe the entire motor
system and make all of it move without having

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:01.630
any volition to it.

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:04.080
Tim Ferriss: Question: what is the value of
this probe?

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:07.730
Dr. Nolan Williams: So the value of a probe
itself is just as they thought —

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:09.120
Tim Ferriss: Figuring out the mapping.

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:11.560
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it's a mapping exercise
initially.

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:12.560
Where is everything?

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:16.360
Is it the same in everybody, is it consistent?

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.400
And they wanted to have a way of doing that
non-invasively.

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:26.450
Penrose and others had been doing this invasively
as neurosurgeons for a hundred years, I guess

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:27.840
50 years at that point.

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:34.530
And so they wanted to be able to emulate what
the surgeons could do in epilepsy patients,

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:37.080
they're doing epilepsy surgery non-invasively.

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:42.299
And then what folks realized over the next
10 years is we can send a signal into the

00:24:42.299 --> 00:24:49.020
brain that's like Morse code and basically
send this signal to change the excitability

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:50.020
of the brain.

00:24:50.020 --> 00:24:55.910
And we can measure it if we do it in motor
cortex by how much the thumb moves with a

00:24:55.910 --> 00:24:57.149
set amplitude out of the machine.

00:24:57.149 --> 00:25:02.210
So if I get X movement where I can make from
stimulating here, I can make the thumb move

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:08.309
X amount, and then I send this Morse code
signal onto the brain to tell it to tone down

00:25:08.309 --> 00:25:13.659
or to kind of be less excitable, and then
I send that same intensity back in.

00:25:13.659 --> 00:25:15.840
The thumb will move half as much, right?

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:18.090
And so you've toned down cortical excitability.

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:22.961
If, instead, I get this measurement of X,
and then instead of putting in what we call

00:25:22.961 --> 00:25:29.620
inhibitory or de-potentiating stimulation,
we put in excitatory potentiating stimulation

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:35.530
into the brain, and we do that and then we
measure again, it'll be 2X.

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:41.799
So we knew by the mid-'90s that we could actually
move around how excitable the brain was in

00:25:41.799 --> 00:25:47.490
these normal, healthy, control volunteer motor
cortices, and so the aha moment for Mark and

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:54.299
his team was this idea that depression at
that time on PET scans, on spec scans, was

00:25:54.299 --> 00:25:56.289
this kind of hypoactivity, hypometabolism.

00:25:56.289 --> 00:25:57.750
Tim Ferriss: Meaning lower?

00:25:57.750 --> 00:25:59.559
Dr. Nolan Williams: Lower, yeah.

00:25:59.559 --> 00:26:00.649
Yeah, sorry, yeah.

00:26:00.649 --> 00:26:06.860
Lower activity, lower metabolism of the prefrontal
cortex, right, where the prefrontal cortex

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:13.080
just isn't as active, isn't as robust as it
is in normal healthy controls.

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:19.220
And so, he had this idea, "Well, could we
use this excitatory stimulation to drive up

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:20.230
activity?"

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:24.080
And so that was the aha moment in the kind
of first version of this.

00:26:24.080 --> 00:26:25.899
But they were super careful.

00:26:25.899 --> 00:26:31.440
There'd been some seizures in the early days
from trying to figure out how all this works,

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:35.500
and so they wanted to do a stimulation approach
that wasn't going to have much in the way

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:36.500
of risk.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:43.240
And so, they had this once a day, very extended
protocol, and because of the mid-'90s, it

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:47.500
was very hard to get cheap brain scans on
patients.

00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:53.640
And so this idea that they were going to have
to use average coordinates to target it, and

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:59.690
so they get average skull measurements, and
then they would do a low dose sort of approach

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:04.210
once a day and do that out for weeks, and
then it'd extended out to months.

00:27:04.210 --> 00:27:11.550
And so, the original TMS trials and the original
approval was this less efficient version that

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:19.110
basically utilized kind of a low efficiency
signal into the brain, and used kind of average

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:20.899
coordinates to place the coil.

00:27:20.899 --> 00:27:26.070
Tim Ferriss: So, average coordinates meaning,
just by clumsy analogy, "We can't take an

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:31.690
X-ray of you, but we do have a hundred other
X-rays that seem to indicate roughly this

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:32.820
is where you have your fracture.

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:34.120
So we're going to aim at those coordinates."

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:35.120
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's right.

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:36.120
Yeah, that's right.

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:41.039
Tim Ferriss: And the once-a-day protocol,
I've heard the number 36 from somewhere, but

00:27:41.039 --> 00:27:43.470
is it somewhere in, generally, like the 30
to 40 session range?

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:44.840
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:49.990
Tim Ferriss: So we're looking at, let's just
call it for a placeholder, 30 to 40 sessions,

00:27:49.990 --> 00:27:50.990
one per day.

00:27:50.990 --> 00:27:54.890
And to perhaps not necessarily conjure an
image, but remove an image from the minds

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:55.890
of some listeners.

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:58.860
They may be thinking, "This is A Beautiful
Mind.

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:03.850
This is somebody chomping down on a wallet
or a piece of wood, getting electroconvulsive

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:04.850
therapy."

00:28:04.850 --> 00:28:05.850
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yep, yep.

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:06.850
Tim Ferriss: These are not the same thing.

00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:07.850
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it's actually the
opposite, right?

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:11.549
So the goal of electroconvulsive therapy,
or ECT, which has been around for 100 years,

00:28:11.549 --> 00:28:14.010
is to produce a therapeutic seizure.

00:28:14.010 --> 00:28:17.400
It's effective, it's associated with, and
some people with autobiographical memory troubles,

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:23.030
it's pretty underutilized as a treatment in
psychiatry because of these issues, because

00:28:23.030 --> 00:28:28.020
of particularly One Flew Over the Cuckoo's
Nest and that story, and that movie culturally

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:31.470
has actually had an impact on ECT.

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:36.330
And it's one of these things that's kind of,
for a lot of patients, that last ditch resort

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:39.700
because of the concerns around the side effects.

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:41.900
TMS is different, right?

00:28:41.900 --> 00:28:45.750
You're not trying to induce a seizure.

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:50.590
In fact, you're not trying to have any sort
of change in cognition.

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:55.250
I always tell patients it's really boring,
and so we have Netflix running in the background

00:28:55.250 --> 00:28:58.460
and people have their Netflix movie that they
keep watching every day.

00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:03.010
And it's basically just, once folks get used
to it, it's just part of a routine where they're

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:07.900
sitting there and watching their show or whatever
it is and the stimulators turning their brain

00:29:07.900 --> 00:29:08.900
on.

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:09.900
And, yeah.

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:11.809
Unlike ECT, there's no anesthesia, there's
really no need for anything.

00:29:11.809 --> 00:29:14.100
You can do this in an outpatient setting.

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:19.550
But the old forms of TMS are extremely slow.

00:29:19.550 --> 00:29:22.070
You can't use that in a psychiatric emergency.

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:26.090
And so this is something that happens over
a couple of months, and it's tricky for some

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:27.090
people.

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:33.559
If you're a working person and you have to
do TMS during, conventional TMS during business

00:29:33.559 --> 00:29:36.740
hours, and you basically have to tell your
boss or sneak out of the office or whatever

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:41.820
it is and go do an hour and a half during
the middle of the day, during standard bankers

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:45.950
hours to be able to do this, and it's hard
to do that over a couple of months.

00:29:45.950 --> 00:29:49.149
And it doesn't address these kind of high
acuity emergencies.

00:29:49.149 --> 00:29:53.711
And so we got very interested in this idea
of can we, like I said earlier, reorganize

00:29:53.711 --> 00:29:56.840
the stimulation in time and space?

00:29:56.840 --> 00:29:59.830
Space being, can we personalize it to each
person's brain?

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:02.059
I can talk about that in greater detail.

00:30:02.059 --> 00:30:06.909
And then, can we compress these six-week courses,
in this case, into a single day?

00:30:06.909 --> 00:30:10.980
So that's why Deirdre was able to get well
in a day, is we gave her a whole six-week

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:11.980
course in a day.

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:13.370
Tim Ferriss: You gave her how many sessions?

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:15.510
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's 10 triple dose
sessions.

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:19.649
And so it's like 30 total equivalent sessions
in a day.

00:30:19.649 --> 00:30:25.850
And so the FDA cleared 600 pulses of ITBS
once a day, five days a week, for six weeks

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:27.210
in 2018.

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:29.190
And so we give —
Tim Ferriss: That was for a major depressive

00:30:29.190 --> 00:30:30.190
disorder?

00:30:30.190 --> 00:30:31.190
Dr. Nolan Williams: That was major depressive
disorder.

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:35.890
And so we give a triple dose every session,
so 1,800 pulses.

00:30:35.890 --> 00:30:41.820
And so, after 10 sessions, you've got the
equivalent of 30 sessions worth in that first

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:44.330
day, and you do that for five days.

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:49.210
And so, people are getting the dose equivalent
of seven and a half months worth of TMS in

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:50.210
five days.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:54.801
And what we found is, surprising to us that
it was this linear, is that you just pick

00:30:54.801 --> 00:30:57.929
up remitters as you progress through the days.

00:30:57.929 --> 00:30:59.240
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by remitters?

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:03.929
Dr. Nolan Williams: People who completely
lose all of their depressive symptoms to a

00:31:03.929 --> 00:31:06.220
level that is within the normal range.

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:09.519
So, they're rating at the same level as somebody
who's not depressed.

00:31:09.519 --> 00:31:14.760
And we can get people there on an average
of 2.6 days, and we're able to do that by

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:20.309
personalizing the target and then being able
to deliver treatment into that target, a lot

00:31:20.309 --> 00:31:22.690
of treatment over a short period of time.

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:28.080
And so what's useful about that is somebody
can be in a really pretty bad state on Monday

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:33.000
morning, they can take a week off a vacation
and may end up being on the inpatient unit

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:39.150
or whatever it is, but they can go out, get
this done, and get back to work in a timeframe

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:40.529
that's actually reasonable.

00:31:40.529 --> 00:31:42.270
And that was really our goal.

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:49.029
How do we get people acutely out of these
high acuity settings and into a state of wellness

00:31:49.029 --> 00:31:53.110
quick enough that it doesn't make a major
impact on their lives?

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:54.820
Tim Ferriss: May I add one thing to that?

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:55.820
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:57.950
Tim Ferriss: Which is,
Tim Ferriss: I've had a number of friends,

00:31:57.950 --> 00:32:03.070
and I've certainly heard of many cases where
people with certain professions, airline pilots,

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:10.169
firefighters, police officers, there are many
more, will not report any type of mental health

00:32:10.169 --> 00:32:14.600
issue because they will be suspended, in a
lot of cases, or relieved of duty.

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:17.700
They'll be forced to take time off.

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:21.990
And in a case like this where you have a compressed
protocol, there's the possibility of taking

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:28.000
PTO or taking a week off and doing this treatment,
whereas like you said, doing it during banker's

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:31.830
hours over months is going to be highly visible,
right?

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:32.830
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yes.

00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:35.980
Tim Ferriss: And until the entire system changes,
which is going to take time if it happens

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:40.240
at all, this would be an incredibly attractive
alternative protocol —

00:32:40.240 --> 00:32:41.240
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:42.240
Tim Ferriss: — if it were to work.

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:43.240
So please continue.

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:44.240
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, absolutely.

00:32:44.240 --> 00:32:47.510
Yeah, and that's the way that we thought about
it, right, is can we take folks who need to

00:32:47.510 --> 00:32:48.510
get well quickly.

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:54.000
I mean, we had people in conventional TMS
courses where they started, they're in really

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:56.510
bad shape, they lose their job halfway through.

00:32:56.510 --> 00:33:00.320
Maybe they get better at the end of it, but
it took so long.

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:04.450
And so it's one of these things, if we can
compress that up and get them well in a short

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:08.559
period of time and get them out of it, it
gets people back to their lives and it has

00:33:08.559 --> 00:33:09.620
a low impact on it.

00:33:09.620 --> 00:33:12.870
Tim Ferriss: I know I'm interrupting a lot,
but the threading of this I think is important.

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:17.019
So we can sort of foreshadow something that
is going to be on the minds of a lot of people,

00:33:17.019 --> 00:33:19.130
which is, what are the downsides, what are
the risks?

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:24.580
So my question is, was it easy to get the
sort of ethics board approval to compress

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:26.530
all of this into a much shorter timeframe?

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:31.429
Dr. Nolan Williams: I was able to make a compelling
case to the IRB and then eventually to the

00:33:31.429 --> 00:33:34.419
FDA around safety.

00:33:34.419 --> 00:33:40.490
We give 90 percent of the resting motor threshold
that's depth-corrected for atrophy.

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:46.519
So if it's an older patient, they have more
prefrontal atrophy, then you can increase

00:33:46.519 --> 00:33:50.000
the intensity based off of that difference
in depth with the motor cortex.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:54.370
But essentially, what their brain is getting
is 90 percent of the resting motor threshold,

00:33:54.370 --> 00:34:07.720
which is kind of a calibrated number that's
based off of these motor, these TMS induced

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:11.710
motor movements.

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:30.010
It gives us a sense of how to dose the stimulation.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:31.010
Tim Ferriss: May I translate that briefly?

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:32.010
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:33.010
Tim Ferriss: I'm sorry to interrupt.

00:34:33.010 --> 00:34:34.010
So I'll show a bit of my hand here, which
is I've undergone two separate weeks with

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:35.010
different hardware, different protocols, because
I'd never want to talk about things like this

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:36.010
unless I've put myself in the spaceship and
been the monkey shot into space.

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:37.010
Which doesn't mean at this point I am a proselytizer
100 percent for the treatment, but I find

00:34:37.010 --> 00:34:38.010
it very compelling, at least for investigation.

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:39.909
So, to just explain since I recently went
through this, each morning, or certainly on

00:34:39.909 --> 00:34:43.940
the first morning, but on multiple mornings
in my case, they will test your motor threshold.

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:44.940
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yep.

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:45.940
Tim Ferriss: Right?

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:49.690
And that could be a finger or a hand, it could
be a foot, and they'll watch movement really

00:34:49.690 --> 00:34:50.690
closely.

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:54.460
And then based on that, determine the sort
of dose that will be delivered throughout

00:34:54.460 --> 00:34:56.109
the day during these sessions.

00:34:56.109 --> 00:34:59.840
And in my case, it was once an hour for 10
hours a day.

00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:05.670
Dr. Nolan Williams: So it's just a way of
getting the intensity that you need.

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:14.930
And so we know that 90 percent, meaning sub
motor threshold, there's never been a TMS-related

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:19.839
seizure for that kind of sub motor threshold
intensity.

00:35:19.839 --> 00:35:25.770
Even giving multiple sessions a day or whatever
it is, there's never been an event like that.

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:32.100
It's always been at a higher kind of intensity
of the stimulation in the moment, if that

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:33.100
makes sense.

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:36.910
So the amount of magnetic field that's being
put out in proximity to the brain.

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:41.420
And so it seems to be much more related to
that than it does the amount of pulses that

00:35:41.420 --> 00:35:43.089
you receive in a day.

00:35:43.089 --> 00:35:48.810
And so I was able to lay that out to the various
regulatory bodies and show the evidence that

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:51.830
that threshold is really the threshold where
risk goes up.

00:35:51.830 --> 00:35:52.830
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:56.849
It's sort of the magnitude of the strength
of the pulse, not the frequency or the density.

00:35:56.849 --> 00:35:58.650
Dr. Nolan Williams: Exactly.

00:35:58.650 --> 00:36:03.060
It's more about the intensity in this case,
and less about the density or the amount.

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:09.040
So, laying that out, it was acceptable, and
various parties deemed it to be non-significant

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:10.520
risk, and they let us proceed.

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:15.810
Now this is FDA breakthrough status, FDA cleared,
and all the way through all of the regulatory

00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:16.810
processes.

00:36:16.810 --> 00:36:20.100
And so it's kind of an on-label approach these
days.

00:36:20.100 --> 00:36:24.190
But back then, I had to make a lot of those
arguments.

00:36:24.190 --> 00:36:27.720
And knock on wood, we still haven't seen any
seizure.

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:33.390
Tim Ferriss: How many people would you say
at this point have gone through well-designed,

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:34.390
accelerated TMS?

00:36:34.390 --> 00:36:39.560
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's definitely, I'd say
more than four or 500 between trials.

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:44.270
We have trials where they're ongoing and I
don't know what the clinical outcome of those

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:46.830
trials are because I'm blinded to them, but
I do know that AEs, right, we can know what,

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:47.830
and we don't have any serious adverse events
in the sense we haven't had anybody have a

00:36:47.830 --> 00:36:48.830
seizure yet.

00:36:48.830 --> 00:36:49.830
So that's good.

00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:50.830
But what you do see is headache.

00:36:50.830 --> 00:36:52.680
People will have a headache.

00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:58.790
It's usually from the, not everybody, but
a fair percentage of people.

00:36:58.790 --> 00:37:03.869
And it's mainly related to the coil-activating
skull nerve, scalp nerves, and basically kind

00:37:03.869 --> 00:37:06.290
of turning the facial musculature on.

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:08.270
And that actually goes away over time.

00:37:08.270 --> 00:37:12.430
So, in the long-term, you can actually see
a reduction in migraine headaches and you

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:18.770
can see a reduction in pain, all that sort
of thing, And people actually have an antinociceptive

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:19.770
effect.

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:20.770
Tim Ferriss: Anti, excuse me?

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:21.770
Dr. Nolan Williams: Anti-pain effect.

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:22.770
Tim Ferriss: Ah, pain.

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:23.770
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:37:23.770 --> 00:37:24.770
Tim Ferriss: What was the word you used?

00:37:24.770 --> 00:37:25.770
Dr. Nolan Williams: Nociceptive?

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:26.770
Tim Ferriss: New one.

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:27.770
All right.

00:37:27.770 --> 00:37:28.770
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:29.770
Yeah.

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:30.770
And so, you can actually have an anti-pain
effect, and the reason why we think that happens

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:31.859
is because you actually —
Tim Ferriss: Anti-pain, meaning it could help

00:37:31.859 --> 00:37:33.330
with chronic pain or something like this?

00:37:33.330 --> 00:37:35.740
Dr. Nolan Williams: — at least acute experimental
pain.

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:37.790
There are some chronic pain studies.

00:37:37.790 --> 00:37:44.150
You can see that you release endogenous opioids
from stimulation because you can actually

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:47.180
use opioid blockers and block the anti-pain
effects of TMS.

00:37:47.180 --> 00:37:48.180
Tim Ferriss: Oh, wow.

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:51.369
Dr. Nolan Williams: So, there's this whole
kind of pharmaco stimulation thing that people

00:37:51.369 --> 00:37:52.369
are looking into.

00:37:52.369 --> 00:37:56.440
A buddy of mine, Joe Taylor, who's at Harvard
now, did these studies when he was an MD/PhD

00:37:56.440 --> 00:38:00.910
and he was able to actually show that there
is this release and that you can kind of block

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:03.930
it with co-administration of opiate-blocking
drugs.

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:08.510
But yeah, that's the idea is that there is
some acute potential headache risk, and people

00:38:08.510 --> 00:38:10.200
can get a little fatigued from this.

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:15.790
It's like fatigue, more like you ran a marathon
instead of depression related fatigue, just

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:17.290
from kind of being there all week.

00:38:17.290 --> 00:38:21.160
Tim Ferriss: Look, N-of-1 here, but seems,
I spent a lot of time talking to folks as

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:22.339
kind of my job.

00:38:22.339 --> 00:38:24.310
My experience was, headache for sure.

00:38:24.310 --> 00:38:32.150
I was hesitant to take any type of Tylenol
or medication for that just because, and this

00:38:32.150 --> 00:38:34.579
is, again, as a naive lay person.

00:38:34.579 --> 00:38:38.320
But I was like, well, if you do take NSAIDs,
if you're doing, say, weight training that

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:40.490
can inhibit some of the adaptive response.

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:43.730
I'm not sure we fully understand what the
hell's going on here, so I'm just going to

00:38:43.730 --> 00:38:44.730
deal with the headache.

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:45.730
It was tolerable.

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:46.730
I mean, you could feel it.

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:51.500
The fatigue was the fatigue that you might
feel if you were cramming for your final exams

00:38:51.500 --> 00:38:52.820
every day.

00:38:52.820 --> 00:39:00.460
It was not the fatigue of apathy and anhedonia,
the difficulty or impossibility of finding

00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:02.880
joy in the things you'd normally find joy
with.

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:05.980
That type of fatigue is, characteristically,
it was very different.

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:10.930
It's more like, yeah, you just crammed for
20 hours to try to nail your finals because

00:39:10.930 --> 00:39:12.710
you didn't prepare, and you're going to do
that every day.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:14.060
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's the feeling.

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:15.390
Tim Ferriss: Question for you, then.

00:39:15.390 --> 00:39:16.470
We're going to get to the results.

00:39:16.470 --> 00:39:22.250
I want you to discuss the results of SAINT,
but I think before we do that, it's important

00:39:22.250 --> 00:39:25.420
to maybe describe this patient population.

00:39:25.420 --> 00:39:30.050
Is it fair to say that you're seeing, it'd
be an exaggeration maybe to say the worst

00:39:30.050 --> 00:39:33.680
of the worst, but you're dealing with patients
who have had multiple failed interventions.

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:38.030
So it seems like that's important to kind
of keep in mind if that is true.

00:39:38.030 --> 00:39:39.030
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:39:39.030 --> 00:39:43.180
And so, in our randomized control trial, people
had an average of nine failed, nine years

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:44.290
of the current episode.

00:39:44.290 --> 00:39:48.820
So, they were depressed for nine years on
average, straight before they came into our

00:39:48.820 --> 00:39:53.950
trial, at five plus or minus two med failures,
and they had a lifetime load of depression

00:39:53.950 --> 00:39:55.180
of about 25 years.

00:39:55.180 --> 00:39:58.750
So, these were folks who've been depressed
with multiple episodes or very long episodes,

00:39:58.750 --> 00:40:02.290
and they tried a lot of meds to get out of
those episodes.

00:40:02.290 --> 00:40:04.500
These are not mild cases, right?

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:07.230
These are folks who've kind of been through
it for a long time.

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:13.099
And interestingly, going back to the study
that we talked about earlier with the flipping

00:40:13.099 --> 00:40:17.119
of the signal, that flipping of the signal
was correlated with higher MADRS scores.

00:40:17.119 --> 00:40:19.619
And so, the more —
Tim Ferriss: MADRS is the assessment?

00:40:19.619 --> 00:40:21.119
Dr. Nolan Williams: I'm sorry, yeah.

00:40:21.119 --> 00:40:22.119
Higher depression scores.

00:40:22.119 --> 00:40:28.450
And so, the higher your depression rating,
the higher likelihood you are to have that

00:40:28.450 --> 00:40:32.340
signal from the data that we collected.

00:40:32.340 --> 00:40:33.960
And so, and that's what we've seen, right?

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:34.960
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:38.010
And this switching, just to tie it back to
what we were talking about, is that sort of

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:42.869
having the wrong lead domino, or if you have
a car where you're trying to start the diesel

00:40:42.869 --> 00:40:44.329
car without heating the coil first?

00:40:44.329 --> 00:40:45.640
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's right.

00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:46.740
Tim Ferriss: Type of situation.

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:47.740
Yeah.

00:40:47.740 --> 00:40:48.740
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, yeah.

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:49.740
So, it's that directionality.

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:50.740
And so, that's what we've seen.

00:40:50.740 --> 00:40:57.770
Folks that are at that kind of more in-stage
sort of depression seemed to be more responsive

00:40:57.770 --> 00:40:58.770
to this.

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:02.950
And I think that's because, in those cases,
you're really correcting this pretty dense

00:41:02.950 --> 00:41:06.960
brain signaling problem if that PNAS work
replicates.

00:41:06.960 --> 00:41:12.270
And what we've also seen is folks that do
really well with dorsal later at least are

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:19.770
folks who have a pretty impaired attention,
and so they actually score up on the concentration

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:21.380
item of the depression scales.

00:41:21.380 --> 00:41:26.871
And so that was something we observed earlier
on, that if you're saying you can't read a

00:41:26.871 --> 00:41:33.910
book anymore, you can't really follow a recipe
book to cook your favorite meal or whatever

00:41:33.910 --> 00:41:38.260
because you just can't attend to it, then
your likelihood of getting better from this

00:41:38.260 --> 00:41:40.170
is higher.

00:41:40.170 --> 00:41:45.810
Whereas folks that are more on the kind of
obsessive/depressive side of things that don't

00:41:45.810 --> 00:41:50.030
complain as much about cognitive problems
and concentration problems, but more about

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:54.530
they're just ruminating and kind of obsessing
on things, we've found that inhibiting the

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:58.680
right frontal pole, orbital frontal cortex,
so stimulating here, and we have OCD trials

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:01.770
to do that, is pretty effective at shutting
that down.

00:42:01.770 --> 00:42:07.010
So it's more of a shutting down than a turning
up sort of intervention.

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:12.370
So, some of it's actually where the illness
intersects with the brain anatomy intersects

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:19.780
with the symptomatic presentation to try to
derive the best spot to stimulate for those

00:42:19.780 --> 00:42:20.780
folks.

00:42:20.780 --> 00:42:24.200
And we have some early studies now where we're
trying to use brain imaging to actually sort

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:30.170
folks into buckets neurally, to figure out
which target makes sense for their symptomatic

00:42:30.170 --> 00:42:31.170
presentation.

00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:32.579
Tim Ferriss: Based on their personalized fMRI
scans?

00:42:32.579 --> 00:42:33.599
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:42:33.599 --> 00:42:34.599
Yeah, yeah.

00:42:34.599 --> 00:42:38.069
Tim Ferriss: So I think this would be a good
point, and we'll mention this again at the

00:42:38.069 --> 00:42:46.099
end, but maybe you could mention any open
trials or if people would like to consider

00:42:46.099 --> 00:42:49.720
enrolling or they know someone who might want
to become subject in the study.

00:42:49.720 --> 00:42:50.829
Is there anything you'd like to mention?

00:42:50.829 --> 00:42:55.260
Dr. Nolan Williams: So we have a number of
trials that are ongoing at the Stanford Brain

00:42:55.260 --> 00:42:57.910
Stimulation Lab, so it's bsl.stanford.edu/clinical-trials.

00:42:57.910 --> 00:43:02.591
And you can go on the website and then there's
a screening portal and people can go on and

00:43:02.591 --> 00:43:04.030
fill out their information.

00:43:04.030 --> 00:43:10.780
And essentially, we have trials for anhedonic
depression, we have trials for standard severe

00:43:10.780 --> 00:43:15.830
treatment-resistant depression, obsessive
compulsive disorder, borderline personality

00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:20.020
disorder, patients who also have depression,
bipolar depression, and other pilots, some

00:43:20.020 --> 00:43:28.590
addiction work that my collaborators are working
on, and so folks have that range of symptomatology.

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:31.050
Happy to have folks come through and screen.

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:33.010
And it's all free, which is nice.

00:43:33.010 --> 00:43:35.869
So it's all basically funded through these
trials.

00:43:35.869 --> 00:43:40.540
And yeah, we're excited to bring people in
and see if it makes sense for them to work

00:43:40.540 --> 00:43:44.400
with us on this, and it's a couple of week
commitment.

00:43:44.400 --> 00:43:49.650
Tim Ferriss: What did the results, or what
have the results looked like with SAINT or

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:55.010
slight permutations of that, and how do they
compare to, let's just say more conventional

00:43:55.010 --> 00:43:57.250
treatments for these same conditions?

00:43:57.250 --> 00:44:02.280
Dr. Nolan Williams: So in our original pilot
study, 90 percent of people experienced remission

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:03.280
at the post.

00:44:03.280 --> 00:44:04.280
Tim Ferriss: 90 percent?

00:44:04.280 --> 00:44:05.280
Dr. Nolan Williams: 90 percent, yep.

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:13.270
In the randomized control trial, it was 79
percent of people transited through remission

00:44:13.270 --> 00:44:14.619
at some point in the four-week follow-up.

00:44:14.619 --> 00:44:16.940
What's interesting is it's not all at the
same time point.

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:20.180
So if you look at time point by time point,
it's in the 50 to 60 percent range.

00:44:20.180 --> 00:44:23.280
And the reason for that is because there's
this, colleagues of mine at Cornell, Conor

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:27.991
Liston and others, have replicated this finding
that there's a subpopulation of patients that

00:44:27.991 --> 00:44:30.950
actually has a slower time to remission.

00:44:30.950 --> 00:44:32.829
And we've seen those too.

00:44:32.829 --> 00:44:36.530
Folks will lose their suicidal ideation, will
actually peak their antidepressant effect

00:44:36.530 --> 00:44:37.530
at one month.

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:41.930
It's usually in older adults, and that's what
we've seen is basically it's only in 50-year-olds

00:44:41.930 --> 00:44:42.930
and above.

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:45.230
We haven't seen that —
Tim Ferriss: That sort of delayed remission?

00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:47.550
Dr. Nolan Williams: — that delayed remission,
yeah.

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:54.190
But if you're putting together 22- to 80-year-olds,
you're going to have some of these folks.

00:44:54.190 --> 00:44:57.890
But what's interesting is TMS is also a probe
of the system.

00:44:57.890 --> 00:45:02.070
If you kind of ignore the kind of normal rules
of how to define remission, and you just ask

00:45:02.070 --> 00:45:07.700
the question of who crosses through, 79 percent
of our active group crossed through, 13 percent

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:10.020
of our control group crossed through.

00:45:10.020 --> 00:45:14.610
And that tells you something, I think, about
the neuroanatomy, that probably something

00:45:14.610 --> 00:45:18.880
in the 70 percent of folks have this dorsal
lateral cingulate problem.

00:45:18.880 --> 00:45:24.770
And then that was a version of that thinking
was seen in our PNAS signaling paper too,

00:45:24.770 --> 00:45:28.140
about 70 percent of those folks had the flip
in the signal.

00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:36.000
And so my suspicion is that there's a subpopulation
of people who don't have that, who have some

00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:37.500
other diagnosis.

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:43.400
It probably looks a lot like depression, but
it's probably a different neuroanatomy.

00:45:43.400 --> 00:45:46.690
And we've seen this sort of phenomenon happen
in medicine before.

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:50.099
We used to cluster all the Parkinsonism together.

00:45:50.099 --> 00:45:56.109
So, there's lots of reasons that Parkinsonism,
some of those reasons are idiopathic Parkinson's

00:45:56.109 --> 00:45:58.180
disease in the kind of classic sense, the
way —

00:45:58.180 --> 00:45:59.180
Tim Ferriss: What does idiopathic mean?

00:45:59.180 --> 00:46:04.190
Dr. Nolan Williams: Just that it's kind of
this spontaneous happening of Parkinson's

00:46:04.190 --> 00:46:05.190
disease.

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:10.079
That's the core Parkinson's syndrome that
we think about in Michael J. Fox and others.

00:46:10.079 --> 00:46:11.079
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:46:11.079 --> 00:46:15.290
Dr. Nolan Williams: And then you've got other
things that look like it, progressive supranuclear

00:46:15.290 --> 00:46:16.290
palsy, Lewy body dementia, drug-related Parkinsonism.

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:22.670
And so, we used to kind of lump —
Tim Ferriss: Parallel to your diabetes presentation

00:46:22.670 --> 00:46:23.670
earlier?

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:24.670
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:29.250
So, we forever lumped all these folks together,
and it really took the UK Brain Bank and being

00:46:29.250 --> 00:46:34.650
able to link, in that case for Parkinson's,
link Parkinson's pathology to symptomatic

00:46:34.650 --> 00:46:35.650
presentation.

00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:36.650
Tim Ferriss: What is the UK Brain Bank?

00:46:36.650 --> 00:46:37.650
I've never heard of this.

00:46:37.650 --> 00:46:38.650
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:46:38.650 --> 00:46:41.050
So, it's like a brain — I mean, there's
lots of brain banks or brain donation entities.

00:46:41.050 --> 00:46:44.430
Tim Ferriss: Physical brains, or are we talking
about scans?

00:46:44.430 --> 00:46:46.900
Dr. Nolan Williams: Physical brains after
death, post-mortem donations.

00:46:46.900 --> 00:46:49.190
So, there's a lot of ways you can donate.

00:46:49.190 --> 00:46:53.290
If you have a neuropsych disease, you can
donate your brain to science.

00:46:53.290 --> 00:46:57.530
And so, a lot of Parkinson's patients donated
their brains to this brain bank to try to

00:46:57.530 --> 00:46:58.610
understand what it is.

00:46:58.610 --> 00:47:05.300
And what they found was, with a deep clinical
phenotyping before death, right?

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:10.660
And so then folks, you had this kind of deep
phenotyping, and then after death, they were

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:12.160
able to —
Tim Ferriss: Sorry, what do you mean by deep

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:13.160
phenotyping?

00:47:13.160 --> 00:47:18.430
Dr. Nolan Williams: — like a deep, kind
understanding of the symptomatology, right?

00:47:18.430 --> 00:47:25.650
So basically, does this person's tremor, did
their tremor happen on one side or both sides

00:47:25.650 --> 00:47:26.650
at the same time?

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:28.140
Tim Ferriss: How do things present?

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:29.240
Dr. Nolan Williams: How do things present?

00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:30.610
Tim Ferriss: In an observable way?

00:47:30.610 --> 00:47:31.610
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's right, exactly.

00:47:31.610 --> 00:47:34.410
Tim Ferriss: In the same way, just folks,
so you have genotype, phenotype, right?

00:47:34.410 --> 00:47:36.070
So, this would be a similar idea.

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:37.070
Okay.

00:47:37.070 --> 00:47:38.070
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:47:38.070 --> 00:47:39.070
Yeah.

00:47:39.070 --> 00:47:43.349
So basically, what they found was, clinically,
people that have the kind of standard Parkinson's

00:47:43.349 --> 00:47:49.380
disease typically tend to have a presentation
of unilateral onset illness, right?

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:54.520
Where one side of the body or the other is
much worse from a Parkinsonian standpoint

00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:55.520
than the other.

00:47:55.520 --> 00:48:01.470
And it's actually very common for people with
Parkinson's to present where, if it's a leg

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:06.450
onset Parkinson's or foot's dragging, or tremor
just in one hand, right?

00:48:06.450 --> 00:48:09.190
And that's actually what you would expect
from normal Parkinson's.

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:14.339
If it's really symmetric, it's less likely
to be that and it's more likely to be something

00:48:14.339 --> 00:48:15.339
else.

00:48:15.339 --> 00:48:18.920
And they figured that out from essentially
the brain biology and forming that, being

00:48:18.920 --> 00:48:25.290
able to link up substantia nigra problems
they found on autopsy with symptoms.

00:48:25.290 --> 00:48:29.240
And so I think we're at the beginning of an
era in psychiatry to be able to do the same

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:34.320
sort of thing, to take what's lumped together
as depression or anxiety or whatnot, and to

00:48:34.320 --> 00:48:39.920
be able to parse it into a lot of different,
what we call biotypes, or people think about

00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:47.510
as endophenotypes or whatever these kind specific
kind of flavors of that symptomatology.

00:48:47.510 --> 00:48:54.670
Some linked with a very specific brain physiology
or brain functional neuroanatomy, such that

00:48:54.670 --> 00:48:59.740
you can say that this depression type one
needs this treatment.

00:48:59.740 --> 00:49:03.640
Depression type two needs this treatment,
and so on and so forth.

00:49:03.640 --> 00:49:10.310
And so that's really, from my vantage point,
the future, right, is being able to really,

00:49:10.310 --> 00:49:14.980
at an N-of-1 level, at a single-patient level,
be able to figure out something about their

00:49:14.980 --> 00:49:20.329
brain and then use that to help to prescribe
what the next step is for them.

00:49:20.329 --> 00:49:25.630
And if we can do that, we can also cut time,
right, because we can go straight to the effective

00:49:25.630 --> 00:49:28.330
treatment for people and cut all the time
around diagnosis.

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:30.030
I mean, there's —
Tim Ferriss: And it's not just time, it's

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:31.030
risk, right?

00:49:31.030 --> 00:49:32.130
In a lot of these cases.

00:49:32.130 --> 00:49:33.130
Dr. Nolan Williams: Absolutely.

00:49:33.130 --> 00:49:35.990
It's time, it's risk from potentially the
wrong treatment.

00:49:35.990 --> 00:49:37.260
It's also risk from waiting.

00:49:37.260 --> 00:49:39.630
Tim Ferriss: It's cost, potentially, also,
right?

00:49:39.630 --> 00:49:41.260
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's also cost.

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:45.280
Yeah, So, as you know, right, there's a period
of time where the diagnosis is uncertain,

00:49:45.280 --> 00:49:50.960
and then there's a period of time where the
treatment is uncertain, right?

00:49:50.960 --> 00:49:56.710
So, for bipolar depression, the average length
of time it takes to diagnose somebody with

00:49:56.710 --> 00:50:03.390
bipolar disorder from a depressive episode
onset, so they come in with depression and

00:50:03.390 --> 00:50:08.190
depression's their primary presentation, seven
years until you get them.

00:50:08.190 --> 00:50:09.190
Tim Ferriss: Geez.

00:50:09.190 --> 00:50:10.190
Dr. Nolan Williams: Right?

00:50:10.190 --> 00:50:11.190
Tim Ferriss: It's wild.

00:50:11.190 --> 00:50:12.190
Dr. Nolan Williams: Right?

00:50:12.190 --> 00:50:14.030
And then, that's just to get to that point.

00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:16.910
Now you're on the right realm of medications
or whatever.

00:50:16.910 --> 00:50:22.410
Now you're talking about, in year seven, you're
having to spend whatever it is, like three,

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:25.630
five, seven more years trying to find a solution.

00:50:25.630 --> 00:50:33.550
So you could go from being 25 years old and
having this be your first depressive episode,

00:50:33.550 --> 00:50:37.480
and you're still trying to figure out what
you're going to do about it at 40, and you

00:50:37.480 --> 00:50:38.480
see these patients.

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:39.480
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:42.470
I'm just imagining you're reaching into a
dark closet and there's what you think is

00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:46.930
a screw head, and you're just trying different
tools, but you're not allowed to touch the

00:50:46.930 --> 00:50:47.930
screw head.

00:50:47.930 --> 00:50:48.930
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's it.

00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:53.950
Tim Ferriss: Versus let's flip on the light,
take a photograph, and then go find the appropriate

00:50:53.950 --> 00:50:54.950
tool for the job.

00:50:54.950 --> 00:50:59.059
Just a couple of follow-ups, the numbers that
you're mentioning, the remission rates, the

00:50:59.059 --> 00:51:03.680
people who pass through, so to speak, how
does that compare to frontline conventional

00:51:03.680 --> 00:51:04.680
treatments right now?

00:51:04.680 --> 00:51:07.170
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah so I mean, oral antidepressants,
so there's a study called STAR*D, it's been

00:51:07.170 --> 00:51:13.670
an interesting year for STAR*D in the sense
that some people have reanalyzed that data

00:51:13.670 --> 00:51:18.690
and there's open questions about what the
percentages are as far as what percentage

00:51:18.690 --> 00:51:22.690
of people make it to a remitted state after
going through this algorithm.

00:51:22.690 --> 00:51:28.710
But, essentially, STAR*D started with a pretty
low side effect burden, oral antidepressants,

00:51:28.710 --> 00:51:34.950
citalopram, and then transited through higher
risks like SNRIs and psychotherapy and lots

00:51:34.950 --> 00:51:35.950
of other.

00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:37.849
Tim Ferriss: What's the trade name for escitalopram?

00:51:37.849 --> 00:51:39.200
Do you know what people would know it as?

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:40.230
Dr. Nolan Williams: Lexapro.

00:51:40.230 --> 00:51:41.230
Tim Ferriss: Lexapro.

00:51:41.230 --> 00:51:42.230
Got it.

00:51:42.230 --> 00:51:43.230
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:44.230
And citalopram, Celexa.

00:51:44.230 --> 00:51:48.050
So those drugs, and then you go to SNRIs,
like Effexor, venlafaxine, and then into the

00:51:48.050 --> 00:51:49.180
tricyclics and monoamine oxidase inhibitors.

00:51:49.180 --> 00:51:52.910
And so people would go through this algorithm
and then they'd pop out on the other end after

00:51:52.910 --> 00:51:55.339
five or six or seven different treatments.

00:51:55.339 --> 00:51:59.990
And then they'd ask the question of, after
people go through all of this, what's their

00:51:59.990 --> 00:52:00.990
remission rate?

00:52:00.990 --> 00:52:06.690
So that number's kind of been called into
question, but essentially when you get into

00:52:06.690 --> 00:52:11.599
med four, which is roughly where insurance
used to start to pay for TM, conventional

00:52:11.599 --> 00:52:18.740
TMS, conventional TMS would beat that pretty
well, almost double, compared to you're talking

00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:21.549
about lithium or thyroid hormone, things like
that.

00:52:21.549 --> 00:52:25.430
When you start getting into that step, and
conventional TMS would beat that.

00:52:25.430 --> 00:52:30.650
When you get to those next even more severe
steps, you start to lose efficacy with conventional

00:52:30.650 --> 00:52:31.650
TMS.

00:52:31.650 --> 00:52:36.980
So you go from a remission rate of around
30 percent with conventional TMS down to about

00:52:36.980 --> 00:52:41.400
16 percent when you get into the higher treatment
resistance levels.

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:46.981
That's the levels that we ran our trials on,
which really that where people normally get

00:52:46.981 --> 00:52:50.030
about 16 percent remission with conventional
TMS.

00:52:50.030 --> 00:52:51.290
That's where we were seeing those numbers.

00:52:51.290 --> 00:52:53.900
Tim Ferriss: Could you just repeat those numbers
where you're seeing what numbers?

00:52:53.900 --> 00:52:54.900
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:58.810
So in our open-label trial, 90 percent in
our randomized control trial, depending upon

00:52:58.810 --> 00:53:05.000
how you look at it's in the 50, 60 percent
at each time point or 79 percent if you're

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:07.250
asking did people transit through remission
at any point.

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:08.250
Tim Ferriss: Versus 16 percent.

00:53:08.250 --> 00:53:09.250
Dr. Nolan Williams: Versus 16 percent.

00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:10.290
Tim Ferriss: And this wasn't a head-to-head.

00:53:10.290 --> 00:53:11.569
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's not a head-to-head
thing.

00:53:11.569 --> 00:53:15.380
Tim Ferriss: But just so people have some
means of comparison.

00:53:15.380 --> 00:53:18.670
And in terms of pharmaceutical interventions
at that point?

00:53:18.670 --> 00:53:19.670
Dr. Nolan Williams: Very low.

00:53:19.670 --> 00:53:23.770
I mean, you're talking about sub-ten percent
efficacy.

00:53:23.770 --> 00:53:28.860
This is around where electroconvulsive therapy
is thought about, this kind of therapeutic

00:53:28.860 --> 00:53:30.030
seizure thing we were talking about earlier.

00:53:30.030 --> 00:53:36.700
That's got about a 48 percent remission rate,
but it's not fast either, and you definitely

00:53:36.700 --> 00:53:38.740
can't work the day you get it.

00:53:38.740 --> 00:53:43.619
And there's this autobiographical memory thing,
so you're talking about a conventional TMS,

00:53:43.619 --> 00:53:49.790
like a one- to two-month commitment, and then
quite a bit of risk for side effects there.

00:53:49.790 --> 00:53:56.080
You've got ketamine, which produces about
a 30 percent spot remission rate with a single

00:53:56.080 --> 00:54:02.780
infusion, and that goes up as you administer
more ketamine treatments with the IV ketamine

00:54:02.780 --> 00:54:03.780
forms.

00:54:03.780 --> 00:54:10.440
But as you go from a single infusion to what
they did in this New England Journal paper

00:54:10.440 --> 00:54:16.099
that was published a couple of months ago,
like six treatments over a couple of weeks,

00:54:16.099 --> 00:54:18.580
it starts to accumulate more time.

00:54:18.580 --> 00:54:23.329
And so we're able to, from my standpoint,
we're able to get the most bang for your buck

00:54:23.329 --> 00:54:28.900
the quickest and with the least kind of interruption,
and I think people's ability to do stuff during

00:54:28.900 --> 00:54:29.900
that time.

00:54:29.900 --> 00:54:33.790
But there are other things, ketamine, psilocybin,
and other psychedelic drugs that I think you've

00:54:33.790 --> 00:54:40.420
thought about and talked about on this show
before that are also coming over the next

00:54:40.420 --> 00:54:41.420
couple of years.

00:54:41.420 --> 00:54:43.730
Tim Ferriss: A few follow up comments and
questions.

00:54:43.730 --> 00:54:48.819
You mentioned a number of things, borderline
personality disorder, bipolar as two examples.

00:54:48.819 --> 00:54:53.130
Part of the reason that I'm so interested
in neuromodulation right now, accelerated

00:54:53.130 --> 00:54:57.070
TMS in particular, although I'm also interested
in other things that I know less about like

00:54:57.070 --> 00:55:02.030
focused ultrasound and so on, which we might
get into if we have time, is that many of

00:55:02.030 --> 00:55:07.240
the conditions that would be screened out
there would be exclusionary criteria in, say,

00:55:07.240 --> 00:55:10.200
a psychedelic-assisted psychotherapy trial
—

00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:11.200
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:55:11.200 --> 00:55:15.670
Tim Ferriss: — are eligible for this type
of treatment.

00:55:15.670 --> 00:55:16.670
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yep.

00:55:16.670 --> 00:55:17.670
Tim Ferriss: Which is incredibly interesting.

00:55:17.670 --> 00:55:21.950
There are people also who might have something
that is more common, extreme hypertension,

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:29.280
some type of ocular issue where, physiologically,
psilocybin shouldn't technically pose a risk,

00:55:29.280 --> 00:55:33.740
but if they have a lot of panic, rapid heart
rate, etc, there might be complications, right?

00:55:33.740 --> 00:55:35.270
Elderly patients, etc.

00:55:35.270 --> 00:55:39.960
So part of the reason this entire realm of
treatment possibilities with neuromodulation

00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:45.410
is interesting is because these tools could
be available to people who would not be good

00:55:45.410 --> 00:55:48.280
candidates for some of these other things
that you're mentioning.

00:55:48.280 --> 00:55:52.000
What I'd love to ask you about, because this
has been one of the questions that has just

00:55:52.000 --> 00:55:54.109
stuck in my head, is the topic of delayed
remission.

00:55:54.109 --> 00:55:55.130
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:55:55.130 --> 00:55:59.480
Tim Ferriss: Having these patients, I can't
remember the number, I was looking at some

00:55:59.480 --> 00:56:02.780
of the charts, maybe it was three or four
people out of N-of-16, I can't recall.

00:56:02.780 --> 00:56:03.990
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's right.

00:56:03.990 --> 00:56:04.990
Tim Ferriss: Okay, there we go.

00:56:04.990 --> 00:56:06.880
Who had this remission at week four.

00:56:06.880 --> 00:56:07.880
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:56:07.880 --> 00:56:08.880
Tim Ferriss: Or something like that.

00:56:08.880 --> 00:56:13.740
How do you explain this and why is it older
patients or does it seem to be older patients,

00:56:13.740 --> 00:56:17.030
and how do you relate it to a few things?

00:56:17.030 --> 00:56:23.640
So, in the case of, say, SSRIs, some folks
also have this, a lot of people, delayed onset

00:56:23.640 --> 00:56:24.640
of benefits.

00:56:24.640 --> 00:56:25.640
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

00:56:25.640 --> 00:56:29.220
Tim Ferriss: And then you have ketamine, this
is going to be a bit of a sloppy question,

00:56:29.220 --> 00:56:30.869
but I'm sure you can clean it up for me.

00:56:30.869 --> 00:56:36.520
Then you have ketamine, which is very rapid-acting,
and I have heard, I do not have the credibility

00:56:36.520 --> 00:56:39.650
or the means of parsing all of this.

00:56:39.650 --> 00:56:45.460
Okay, well, ketamine acts directly on NMDA
receptors.

00:56:45.460 --> 00:56:51.250
Seems like maybe SSRIs do that, but in a very
indirect way, and that explains the delayed

00:56:51.250 --> 00:56:53.059
benefits in some patients.

00:56:53.059 --> 00:56:54.059
Don't know if that's true or not.

00:56:54.059 --> 00:56:55.220
Don't know if I'm even wording that correctly.

00:56:55.220 --> 00:57:00.030
How do you tie this to or not the delayed
remission?

00:57:00.030 --> 00:57:02.030
Because I'm just like, how does that even
work, right?

00:57:02.030 --> 00:57:07.490
I'm like, okay, if you put a drug in someone's
head and it triggers a cascade or maybe it

00:57:07.490 --> 00:57:16.010
triggers some type of exceptional neuroplasticity
that then shows the fruits of that at weeks,

00:57:16.010 --> 00:57:17.010
whatever, two, three, four.

00:57:17.010 --> 00:57:18.870
But I just cannot figure out, I can't come
up with, I haven't been able to figure out

00:57:18.870 --> 00:57:22.900
a plausible mechanism for the accelerated
TMS and that delayed remission.

00:57:22.900 --> 00:57:24.020
How do you think about that?

00:57:24.020 --> 00:57:25.079
Even if it's speculation.

00:57:25.079 --> 00:57:29.760
Dr. Nolan Williams: And it would be speculation,
but I think, so we've only seen in older adults,

00:57:29.760 --> 00:57:33.030
we know that the brain plasticity in older
adults goes down as a generality.

00:57:33.030 --> 00:57:36.660
And there are lots of metrics about why folks
think that.

00:57:36.660 --> 00:57:40.420
As you talked about earlier, it's cramming
for a test, you're actually sending a memory

00:57:40.420 --> 00:57:41.500
signal into the brain.

00:57:41.500 --> 00:57:45.360
So the stimulation pattern you're sending
into the brain, this kind of Morse code is

00:57:45.360 --> 00:57:51.820
really a "Turn on, stay on, remember to stay
on" memory signal that's going into the brain.

00:57:51.820 --> 00:57:56.360
You're just basically taking the hippocampus,
the part of the brain that's involved in memory

00:57:56.360 --> 00:58:01.430
and that signaling that comes out of there,
and you're playing that back through the prefrontal

00:58:01.430 --> 00:58:07.250
cortex in a way to try to tell the prefrontal
cortex to "Turn on, stay on, and remember

00:58:07.250 --> 00:58:08.250
to stay on."

00:58:08.250 --> 00:58:15.579
I think part of what's going on is, because
that older brain is a little less likely to

00:58:15.579 --> 00:58:22.049
have flexibility/plasticity, it takes some
time for the signal to fully lay its tracks

00:58:22.049 --> 00:58:23.430
into the brain.

00:58:23.430 --> 00:58:32.650
We don't have any sort of biology to back
that up yet, but what we're doing right now,

00:58:32.650 --> 00:58:35.840
we haven't analyzed this data yet, is we're
actually scanning people every single day

00:58:35.840 --> 00:58:38.980
and we're scanning people multiple times out
to the month.

00:58:38.980 --> 00:58:40.940
NIH funded one —
Tim Ferriss: By scanning you mean fMRI?

00:58:40.940 --> 00:58:41.940
Dr. Nolan Williams: fMRI scanning.

00:58:41.940 --> 00:58:42.940
Yeah, yeah.

00:58:42.940 --> 00:58:44.790
So we're actually getting 10 scans spread
out over —

00:58:44.790 --> 00:58:46.859
Tim Ferriss: It's a lot of coffin time.

00:58:46.859 --> 00:58:48.430
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's a lot.

00:58:48.430 --> 00:58:49.430
Yeah, yeah.

00:58:49.430 --> 00:58:51.990
People have been very cool about this.

00:58:51.990 --> 00:58:53.579
And so we've been able to with that, we think
that we're going to be able to potentially

00:58:53.579 --> 00:58:55.280
spell some of this out.

00:58:55.280 --> 00:58:59.530
Why are these delayed remitters happening?

00:58:59.530 --> 00:59:06.480
But my suspicion is that it's probably a plasticity
related issue.

00:59:06.480 --> 00:59:13.119
Interestingly, ketamine and TMS may have more
in common with each other than one would initially

00:59:13.119 --> 00:59:14.190
think.

00:59:14.190 --> 00:59:19.260
A lot of the TMS effects are probably, in
part, glutamate-related, as is ketamine.

00:59:19.260 --> 00:59:24.500
And then as we talked about earlier, there's
an endogenous opioid release from TMS.

00:59:24.500 --> 00:59:27.900
We've done some work with opioid-related mechanisms
in ketamine.

00:59:27.900 --> 00:59:36.930
There's probably a confluence of not one neurotransmitter
system, but an orchestra of neurotransmitter

00:59:36.930 --> 00:59:40.600
systems that are being affected across these
interventions.

00:59:40.600 --> 00:59:45.000
It's my suspicion that that's probably what
needs to happen in order for these treatments

00:59:45.000 --> 00:59:46.000
to be effective.

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:55.210
And our old views on this kind of chemical
imbalance sort of 1990s view of one neurotransmitter,

00:59:55.210 --> 01:00:00.650
one neurotransmitter receptor sort of problem
in the brain is way too simplistic and that

01:00:00.650 --> 01:00:05.280
it's probably a lot more complicated, as one
would imagine, a lot more complicated than

01:00:05.280 --> 01:00:06.280
that.

01:00:06.280 --> 01:00:11.010
Tim Ferriss: Outside of accelerated TMS, if
you're looking out, say, over the next five

01:00:11.010 --> 01:00:18.010
years for rapid-acting, potentially durable
antidepressant effects, what other tools or

01:00:18.010 --> 01:00:21.170
molecules or treatments are most interesting
to you?

01:00:21.170 --> 01:00:25.470
Dr. Nolan Williams: We have a paper coming
out soon in Nature Medicine on looking at

01:00:25.470 --> 01:00:31.119
at ibogaine as a potential treatment for,
in this case for military traumatic brain

01:00:31.119 --> 01:00:37.730
injury, but a lot of these folks had depression,
generalized anxiety disorder, and PTSD.

01:00:37.730 --> 01:00:44.620
It was an interesting, that was another interesting
story, so I was approached back in 2018 by

01:00:44.620 --> 01:00:49.150
a professor, a senior professor at Stanford
who was tapped by some folks to kind of find

01:00:49.150 --> 01:00:54.520
somebody who'd be willing to partner with
a non-profit called VETS at the time who were

01:00:54.520 --> 01:00:59.099
sending, and I think you've interacted with
Amber and Marcus Capone a little bit.

01:00:59.099 --> 01:01:03.859
Tim Ferriss: I have, yeah, I interviewed Rick
Perry and Rick Doblin two years ago at their

01:01:03.859 --> 01:01:04.859
Veterans Day fundraiser.

01:01:04.859 --> 01:01:06.890
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, I was there, I remember.

01:01:06.890 --> 01:01:08.460
That was an interesting one, yeah.

01:01:08.460 --> 01:01:13.230
We partnered up with them and I had to again,
go to the Stanford IRB and ask them for another

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:16.340
edgy — and you have to give the Stanford
IRB credit.

01:01:16.340 --> 01:01:17.710
Tim Ferriss: Could you just spell out IRB?

01:01:17.710 --> 01:01:18.799
Dr. Nolan Williams: Oh, sorry.

01:01:18.799 --> 01:01:20.000
Institutional Review Board.

01:01:20.000 --> 01:01:24.960
It's the entity that reviews all research
protocols at institutions.

01:01:24.960 --> 01:01:29.410
This is the kind of governing body that's
in place that's been around for about a hundred

01:01:29.410 --> 01:01:35.020
years that essentially is a non-conflicted,
uninvolved group of senior professors that

01:01:35.020 --> 01:01:40.281
look at your proposed research and then determine
if it's ethical and safe and answering the

01:01:40.281 --> 01:01:41.490
questions that you think you're going to answer.

01:01:41.490 --> 01:01:49.390
And so just going to them to talk through
doing accelerated TMS and saying we were able

01:01:49.390 --> 01:01:54.680
to talk to them about doing a study where
people would come to Stanford, knowing in

01:01:54.680 --> 01:02:01.809
the IRB, knowingly agree to them, then going
down to Mexico to take an illegal-in-the-US

01:02:01.809 --> 01:02:09.230
root bark extract that's been utilized for
millennia in the country of Gabon and related

01:02:09.230 --> 01:02:12.059
areas in central West Africa.

01:02:12.059 --> 01:02:17.650
As you can imagine, this was not a quick turnaround,
nor should it have been for the IRB in the

01:02:17.650 --> 01:02:20.670
sense that I had to convince them this was
science worth doing.

01:02:20.670 --> 01:02:24.869
Tim Ferriss: At that time though, is it fair
to say you're the TMS guy, or was it in the

01:02:24.869 --> 01:02:31.190
air that you also had an interest in exploring
something like an iboga or an ibogaine?

01:02:31.190 --> 01:02:35.720
Dr. Nolan Williams: People knew that I was
very open-minded to things.

01:02:35.720 --> 01:02:37.289
I'm a pragmatist, right?

01:02:37.289 --> 01:02:40.549
I mean, for me, the patient's the most important
thing.

01:02:40.549 --> 01:02:46.000
I have this view of psychiatry that it's going
to look like in-patient cardiology in 20 years

01:02:46.000 --> 01:02:49.539
where we're going to use drugs, we're going
to use devices, we're going to be able to

01:02:49.539 --> 01:02:52.339
figure out what the best thing is for that
patient.

01:02:52.339 --> 01:02:57.450
To your point, some of these things are good
for different problems.

01:02:57.450 --> 01:03:00.680
I think that was known that I was open to
that.

01:03:00.680 --> 01:03:07.930
We'd just been running this ketamine mechanism
trial, so I was tapped to look at this and

01:03:07.930 --> 01:03:09.829
a couple other people too.

01:03:09.829 --> 01:03:12.440
I found out later they'd gone to, like, a
lot of people.

01:03:12.440 --> 01:03:17.640
Apparently, I was the only person that was
willing to do this trial.

01:03:17.640 --> 01:03:19.599
I felt kind of special back then, and then
later realized —

01:03:19.599 --> 01:03:22.333
Tim Ferriss: "Oh, wait a second, I was on
The Bachelorette and I didn't even realize."

01:03:22.333 --> 01:03:25.039
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, I was on The Bachelorette
and I didn't realize it.

01:03:25.039 --> 01:03:28.270
Essentially, yeah, and The Bachelorette was
a tricky one.

01:03:28.270 --> 01:03:31.400
We ended up reluctantly agreeing to this.

01:03:31.400 --> 01:03:37.359
Admittedly I almost pulled out a couple of
times because ibogaine has this street knowledge,

01:03:37.359 --> 01:03:40.089
and academic knowledge, that it has a death
risk.

01:03:40.089 --> 01:03:45.700
Right, it has a one, roughly one-in-300 risk
of death from Torsade de pointes.

01:03:45.700 --> 01:03:49.779
This arrhythmia, fatal arrhythmia that can
happen with certain cardiac-acting drugs,

01:03:49.779 --> 01:03:54.540
and it works through this, what they call
HRG potassium channel.

01:03:54.540 --> 01:03:59.420
Other things do this or like FDA cleared like
cancer drugs and arrhythmia drugs, that'll

01:03:59.420 --> 01:04:03.599
do it too, so it's not —
In context, it's like something that happens

01:04:03.599 --> 01:04:06.230
with lots of different drugs that we use.

01:04:06.230 --> 01:04:11.190
But I think there was somewhat of a stigma
and a bias around particularly an addiction

01:04:11.190 --> 01:04:12.190
drug.

01:04:12.190 --> 01:04:18.299
Folks had been trying to get this through
the FDA and NIH and whatnot in the '90s, Howard

01:04:18.299 --> 01:04:21.500
Lotsof and others, and it was a very complicated
drug.

01:04:21.500 --> 01:04:23.869
And I'd known about it since residency.

01:04:23.869 --> 01:04:24.980
I'd read about it.

01:04:24.980 --> 01:04:29.970
I thought that it was like, "Wow, this is
the most promising anti-addiction drug on

01:04:29.970 --> 01:04:30.970
the planet."

01:04:30.970 --> 01:04:35.470
But I'd thought it was completely unstudiable,
and I kept doing my other stuff.

01:04:35.470 --> 01:04:38.780
Tim Ferriss: At that point, how did you come
across ibogaine?

01:04:38.780 --> 01:04:40.020
Where did you find it?

01:04:40.020 --> 01:04:42.109
Dr. Nolan Williams: In 2012, 2010, you know,
I think —

01:04:42.109 --> 01:04:44.789
Tim Ferriss: Would this have been early Deborah
Mash stuff or?

01:04:44.789 --> 01:04:45.900
Dr. Nolan Williams: No, no.

01:04:45.900 --> 01:04:48.960
I come across this book Breaking Open the
Head by Daniel Pinchbeck.

01:04:48.960 --> 01:04:49.960
Tim Ferriss: Pinchbeck.

01:04:49.960 --> 01:04:50.960
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:04:50.960 --> 01:04:51.960
Yeah.

01:04:51.960 --> 01:04:53.630
He was a big, I think he wrote for The New
Yorker or something, and I was stuck.

01:04:53.630 --> 01:04:59.450
I was going kite surfing in South America
and I was stuck in the San Salvador airport

01:04:59.450 --> 01:05:03.599
and there was five English-speaking books
or something.

01:05:03.599 --> 01:05:06.150
I picked this book up in the airport.

01:05:06.150 --> 01:05:07.339
Tim Ferriss: What?

01:05:07.339 --> 01:05:08.529
That was in the San Salvador airport?

01:05:08.529 --> 01:05:09.529
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, yeah.

01:05:09.529 --> 01:05:10.529
Yeah.

01:05:10.529 --> 01:05:11.529
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

01:05:11.529 --> 01:05:12.529
Dr. Nolan Williams: Randomly, right?

01:05:12.529 --> 01:05:15.430
I got a hold of this book and read it waiting
on my flight, which was like eight hours delayed

01:05:15.430 --> 01:05:16.430
to get to Peru.

01:05:16.430 --> 01:05:18.590
And so essentially, yeah, he just lays the
whole thing out.

01:05:18.590 --> 01:05:20.020
It was very interesting.

01:05:20.020 --> 01:05:24.840
He talks about his own personal experience
of it, and then I read a lot about it.

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:30.980
And there was some work that folks like Ken
Alper and others had published on the case

01:05:30.980 --> 01:05:33.809
report level outcomes, but then also the cardiac
problems.

01:05:33.809 --> 01:05:36.030
So then I figured it was kind of unstudiable.

01:05:36.030 --> 01:05:40.760
And then, yeah, in 2018 I was approached and
was asked to do this trial.

01:05:40.760 --> 01:05:46.099
And so we went to the IRB, spent a year back
and forth, and right when they were about

01:05:46.099 --> 01:05:47.990
to, they approved it, COVID hit.

01:05:47.990 --> 01:05:53.760
So we were actually supposed to start when
COVID happened, and it paused the whole thing.

01:05:53.760 --> 01:05:58.430
It took a while to get it back online, but
it was actually the quickest recruiting study

01:05:58.430 --> 01:05:59.430
I've ever run.

01:05:59.430 --> 01:06:00.960
We got 30 people done in eight months.

01:06:00.960 --> 01:06:06.170
Tim Ferriss: Now is that because of the category
of potential death of despair?

01:06:06.170 --> 01:06:08.039
I mean, is it because of the patient population?

01:06:08.039 --> 01:06:09.210
Were you dealing with addicts?

01:06:09.210 --> 01:06:13.089
Dr. Nolan Williams: So the patient population
was, and that was the unique part about this,

01:06:13.089 --> 01:06:18.970
it was veterans with traumatic brain injury,
some of which were alcohol use disorders,

01:06:18.970 --> 01:06:26.140
so we used to call alcoholism, but more than
a third, I think 13 or 14 people out of the

01:06:26.140 --> 01:06:27.140
30.

01:06:27.140 --> 01:06:31.210
But everybody had TBI, a lot of them, most
of them had depression, most of them had PTSD.

01:06:31.210 --> 01:06:38.020
We were running this trial, I put my best
neuropsychology postdoc on it and a couple

01:06:38.020 --> 01:06:42.920
other superstars, and it was running in the
background and I was like, we'll get this

01:06:42.920 --> 01:06:47.000
into a pretty good journal or whatever, thinking
we'd see some people get better, some not,

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:48.000
whatever.

01:06:48.000 --> 01:06:53.859
I remember I was asked to give a talk about
it at a very prestigious university and asked

01:06:53.859 --> 01:06:58.170
the postdocs, we just wrapped up the immediate
post and asked the postdocs to put the data

01:06:58.170 --> 01:07:00.650
together and we have data to present for them.

01:07:00.650 --> 01:07:04.450
They showed me the clinical outcomes, and
I was completely blown away.

01:07:04.450 --> 01:07:09.569
It was way better than I think we anticipated,
very consistent improvements in basically

01:07:09.569 --> 01:07:10.569
everybody.

01:07:10.569 --> 01:07:15.059
Some people would lose it before the month,
but most people held it and they're holding

01:07:15.059 --> 01:07:16.400
it out to a year now.

01:07:16.400 --> 01:07:17.400
I was floored.

01:07:17.400 --> 01:07:21.740
I actually told them to delete all the code
and start the analysis over.

01:07:21.740 --> 01:07:25.980
They had to have done something wrong in the
math, which is always fun.

01:07:25.980 --> 01:07:31.460
The postdoc always, they kind of say yes,
and look at you, "You just ruined my weekend."

01:07:31.460 --> 01:07:36.220
And so we did it again and it was the same
exact findings.

01:07:36.220 --> 01:07:40.520
So I was wrong, they were right, they had
done it the first time, essentially really,

01:07:40.520 --> 01:07:41.520
really striking.

01:07:41.520 --> 01:07:45.580
And like I said, by the time this will come
out, Nature Medicine will have published this.

01:07:45.580 --> 01:07:46.859
Tim Ferriss: Somewhat prestigious.

01:07:46.859 --> 01:07:48.130
Dr. Nolan Williams: Somewhat!

01:07:48.130 --> 01:07:50.160
Top five biomedical journals in the world.

01:07:50.160 --> 01:07:54.000
Tim Ferriss: It's like being nominated for
best picture at the Academy Awards, I mean,

01:07:54.000 --> 01:07:55.000
scientifically speaking, right?

01:07:55.000 --> 01:07:56.000
It's up there.

01:07:56.000 --> 01:07:57.319
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's up there.

01:07:57.319 --> 01:07:58.390
It's a nice deal.

01:07:58.390 --> 01:08:03.380
It was very surprising that, for me, that
they were going to be open to publishing an

01:08:03.380 --> 01:08:08.652
open-label paper, which historically you're
going to publish in that kind of a journal,

01:08:08.652 --> 01:08:09.880
like a randomized trial.

01:08:09.880 --> 01:08:14.510
Tim Ferriss: Just for clarity's sake, for
folks listening, so open-label means no placebo

01:08:14.510 --> 01:08:15.510
control, right?

01:08:15.510 --> 01:08:16.510
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:08:16.510 --> 01:08:19.100
Tim Ferriss: And also to just rewind for a
second, I want to mention to people that for

01:08:19.100 --> 01:08:22.020
accelerated TMS, how do you know it's not
placebo effect?

01:08:22.020 --> 01:08:25.319
You mentioned the control group, but that's
a sham treatment.

01:08:25.319 --> 01:08:28.299
You're basically, people feel like they're
getting a treatment, but they're not actually

01:08:28.299 --> 01:08:30.120
getting the proper treatment.

01:08:30.120 --> 01:08:32.730
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, in that case, you
want to ask a blind guess.

01:08:32.730 --> 01:08:36.190
And that's actually been a big — so I was
going to get into here a big problem with

01:08:36.190 --> 01:08:39.902
a lot of the psychedelic trials and why there's
a lot of criticism for a lot of the psychedelic

01:08:39.902 --> 01:08:44.930
trials is that they kind of know that the
blind guess is going to be highly skewed.

01:08:44.930 --> 01:08:48.600
So there was one trial where they did psilocybin
for alcohol use disorder.

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:54.509
I think it was like 99 percent correct blind
guess rating, so the P value was highly significant.

01:08:54.509 --> 01:08:59.259
In our same studies, to my great surprise,
our blind guess was chance.

01:08:59.259 --> 01:09:00.790
Tim Ferriss: Could you explain what do you
mean by blind guess?

01:09:00.790 --> 01:09:04.609
Dr. Nolan Williams: Mr. Smith, who —
Tim Ferriss: These are the experimenters.

01:09:04.609 --> 01:09:05.810
Trying to guess who is in which group.

01:09:05.810 --> 01:09:07.859
Dr. Nolan Williams: This is the patients trying
to guess what they got.

01:09:07.859 --> 01:09:08.859
Tim Ferriss: I see.

01:09:08.859 --> 01:09:09.859
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:09:09.859 --> 01:09:13.480
"So, Mr. Smith, you just wrapped up your treatment,
which treatment did you get: active or sham,

01:09:13.480 --> 01:09:14.480
what's your confidence…"

01:09:14.480 --> 01:09:15.529
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:09:15.529 --> 01:09:17.650
Dr. Nolan Williams: "...in what you got?

01:09:17.650 --> 01:09:23.230
And so what we found, which is really interesting
for SAINT, was that I didn't know what people

01:09:23.230 --> 01:09:25.469
got, but I talked to some of these people
and I heard some of this.

01:09:25.469 --> 01:09:27.100
So it was really interesting.

01:09:27.100 --> 01:09:32.410
Patients who'd gotten totally better and they'd
say things like, "Yeah, I just got lucky with

01:09:32.410 --> 01:09:35.080
placebo this time," they ended up getting
active.

01:09:35.080 --> 01:09:38.870
And then the folks that didn't get any better,
that they got sham, would say things like,

01:09:38.870 --> 01:09:42.429
"I'm not even good enough to respond to the
active treatments."

01:09:42.429 --> 01:09:47.660
So it was confusing enough for them where
they were making the wrong guess 50 percent

01:09:47.660 --> 01:09:51.469
of the time, which is what you're looking
for, is about 50 percent error rate.

01:09:51.469 --> 01:09:52.600
And it's a coin toss.

01:09:52.600 --> 01:09:58.020
Tim Ferriss: Sidebar on that, I know this
is very kind of staccato the way that I'm

01:09:58.020 --> 01:10:03.180
trying to hold this conversation, but having
gone through two weeks, different hardware,

01:10:03.180 --> 01:10:07.489
different practitioners, etc., and having
had a lot of conversations with technicians

01:10:07.489 --> 01:10:11.480
and so on, it also seems like for some people
it takes a while for their narrative to catch

01:10:11.480 --> 01:10:14.450
up with the hardware upgrade.

01:10:14.450 --> 01:10:19.780
In the sense that they say, "Well, maybe I
got lucky," or "Maybe I don't really feel

01:10:19.780 --> 01:10:24.620
that much," and yet their assessments are
improving and/or their significant others

01:10:24.620 --> 01:10:25.890
see dramatic changes, right?

01:10:25.890 --> 01:10:26.890
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's true.

01:10:26.890 --> 01:10:32.350
That's not specifically a TMS effect, or a
SAINT effect, or accelerated TMS effect.

01:10:32.350 --> 01:10:35.620
That's true, I think, for a lot of treatments
you see that, I mean, I've had calls in since

01:10:35.620 --> 01:10:39.830
our data's been out on ibogaine where I've
had people call me and say, "Hey, Mr. or Mrs.

01:10:39.830 --> 01:10:43.239
so-and-so, they look amazing."

01:10:43.239 --> 01:10:45.260
And they're not, they don't think they're
any better.

01:10:45.260 --> 01:10:48.160
And so I think you can see it across the board,
psychedelics, neuro mod.

01:10:48.160 --> 01:10:53.659
There's a certain problem called alexithymia
in about 25 percent of people with treatment-resistant

01:10:53.659 --> 01:10:54.659
depression.

01:10:54.659 --> 01:10:55.659
Tim Ferriss: Alexythymia.

01:10:55.659 --> 01:10:59.320
Dr. Nolan Williams: And so "A" meaning without,
"Lex" meaning the ability to describe, "Thymia"

01:10:59.320 --> 01:11:00.320
mood.

01:11:00.320 --> 01:11:05.409
And so they can't really describe their mood,
and so they have an inability to accurately

01:11:05.409 --> 01:11:06.409
rate their mood.

01:11:06.409 --> 01:11:08.219
And the way you know that is —
Tim Ferriss: Alexithymia.

01:11:08.219 --> 01:11:12.449
I'm going to use that at a fancy cocktail
party, so, "Tim, how are you doing?"

01:11:12.449 --> 01:11:13.560
"Sorry, got alexithymia" "Oh, sorry.

01:11:13.560 --> 01:11:18.630
You may better know that as 'Difficulty to
describe mood.'"

01:11:18.630 --> 01:11:21.600
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's all right.

01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:24.170
Sorry, I'll try to back off on the medical
jargon.

01:11:24.170 --> 01:11:25.440
Tim Ferriss: No, I like it.

01:11:25.440 --> 01:11:26.440
I'm learning all sorts of words.

01:11:26.440 --> 01:11:28.330
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it's an interesting
term and it's an interesting phenomenon.

01:11:28.330 --> 01:11:30.120
You see this in psychiatric conditions.

01:11:30.120 --> 01:11:34.640
People just have a, if you ask them very specific
questions, "Well, when's the last time you've

01:11:34.640 --> 01:11:37.020
been sad or thought about anything negative?"

01:11:37.020 --> 01:11:39.020
"Oh, it's been a week or two."

01:11:39.020 --> 01:11:40.020
"Are you depressed?"

01:11:40.020 --> 01:11:41.020
"Yes."

01:11:41.020 --> 01:11:42.020
"Well, why do you know that?"

01:11:42.020 --> 01:11:43.020
"I don't know."

01:11:43.020 --> 01:11:44.020
They just don't know.

01:11:44.020 --> 01:11:46.160
Tim Ferriss: It's also like a self-reporting
problem too, I think in some cases, right?

01:11:46.160 --> 01:11:48.500
It's like if you ask someone, "How many calories
did you eat yesterday?"

01:11:48.500 --> 01:11:52.469
Most people would be off by 30 to 60 percent
or something, right?

01:11:52.469 --> 01:11:53.630
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's right.

01:11:53.630 --> 01:11:58.290
I think what, the remedy for that is that
you have a clinician rater, and the clinician

01:11:58.290 --> 01:12:04.841
rater asks these really, really pointed, very
detailed questions, and then the patient's

01:12:04.841 --> 01:12:11.510
able to answer, and then it's a formula to
calculate what that mood rating is, in that

01:12:11.510 --> 01:12:12.630
case.

01:12:12.630 --> 01:12:17.500
Sometimes it's just off from what their perception
is, but when their kind of meta perception

01:12:17.500 --> 01:12:18.500
of the whole thing.

01:12:18.500 --> 01:12:22.820
But when you get down to the brass tacks,
they're answering right, and so we've seen

01:12:22.820 --> 01:12:28.449
that and that's been true across these problems
and there's ways of constructing trials to

01:12:28.449 --> 01:12:29.449
deal with that.

01:12:29.449 --> 01:12:31.850
But yeah, we've seen that with psychedelics
as well.

01:12:31.850 --> 01:12:36.230
So we went after military TBI and yeah, we
were just really surprised.

01:12:36.230 --> 01:12:40.230
And one of the biggest —
Tim Ferriss: And these were all special operations?

01:12:40.230 --> 01:12:44.520
Dr. Nolan Williams: They were basically all
Army Rangers, Navy SEAL, former Army Rangers,

01:12:44.520 --> 01:12:45.730
former Navy SEALs.

01:12:45.730 --> 01:12:50.000
There's been a big cohort of these folks that
have gone down, I mean, some people in Congress

01:12:50.000 --> 01:12:53.739
have gone down and done this and have spoken
about it publicly.

01:12:53.739 --> 01:12:58.360
I mean, it's this National Defense Authorization
Act, the NDA, I think has just went through

01:12:58.360 --> 01:13:03.070
the Senate and the House both approved it
yesterday and it's going to Biden.

01:13:03.070 --> 01:13:08.219
So to earmark money for ibogaine for trials,
which is pretty cool.

01:13:08.219 --> 01:13:24.132
The military community and some of the government
is pretty aware that this is a possibility.

01:13:24.132 --> 01:13:25.132
There's been a lot of advocacy that Amber
and Marcus have been involved in.

01:13:25.132 --> 01:13:26.132
My hope is that coming out of a journal like
Nature Medicine that really kind of validates

01:13:26.132 --> 01:13:27.860
what we were seeing and puts some context
to what we observed and what we found in our

01:13:27.860 --> 01:13:32.430
study, will help to spur more funding and
more focus.

01:13:32.430 --> 01:13:40.250
I think the veteran psychedelic angle is an
important one for a lot of ethical moral reasons

01:13:40.250 --> 01:13:43.280
and a lot of —
Tim Ferriss: Stuff like a political immunity

01:13:43.280 --> 01:13:44.280
bracelet, right?

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:45.280
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's right.

01:13:45.280 --> 01:13:46.280
That one.

01:13:46.280 --> 01:13:52.360
Tim Ferriss: It's a very important subpopulation
and it's very fortunate that it aligns with

01:13:52.360 --> 01:13:56.200
driving forward the research around these
therapeutic tools.

01:13:56.200 --> 01:13:58.440
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it was super cool
to work with those guys.

01:13:58.440 --> 01:14:02.350
Tim Ferriss: And this was a monotherapy in
the sense that it was just ibogaine.

01:14:02.350 --> 01:14:07.199
I know that at least in many places in Mexico,
they sometimes combine it.

01:14:07.199 --> 01:14:11.600
Well, they don't combine it, but they sequence
it with 5-MeO-DMT at the tail end.

01:14:11.600 --> 01:14:13.219
Was this ibogaine alone?

01:14:13.219 --> 01:14:18.780
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it was ibogaine
alone, and then we had folks come back later

01:14:18.780 --> 01:14:21.710
to do the 5-MeO outside of this month follow-up
period.

01:14:21.710 --> 01:14:26.199
But the data that we're publishing in Nature
Medicine is just the ibogaine effect.

01:14:26.199 --> 01:14:30.469
It was tricky to divorce those two together
because that's what's been going on, as you

01:14:30.469 --> 01:14:32.469
know, in Mexico quite a bit.

01:14:32.469 --> 01:14:37.639
All the acute out to the one-month effects
are all ibogaine, direct ibogaine effects,

01:14:37.639 --> 01:14:39.660
and it's super cool.

01:14:39.660 --> 01:14:46.639
And the thing that I found really interesting
about this drug is that it produces what I

01:14:46.639 --> 01:14:51.070
think is probably the most stereotyped trip,
if you want to call it, or the psychological

01:14:51.070 --> 01:14:55.270
phenomenon that happens alongside the drug
effects.

01:14:55.270 --> 01:15:02.600
People will describe this earlier life, autobiographical
replaying of emotionally salient memories

01:15:02.600 --> 01:15:06.389
that are kind of epoched in time.

01:15:06.389 --> 01:15:09.389
Tim Ferriss: Some people call it life review,
right?

01:15:09.389 --> 01:15:11.190
Dr. Nolan Williams: Life review or slideshow.

01:15:11.190 --> 01:15:12.400
Yeah, exactly.

01:15:12.400 --> 01:15:15.920
It's interesting, everybody's kind of got
a different version of what the slideshow

01:15:15.920 --> 01:15:18.739
ends up playing out to be like for them.

01:15:18.739 --> 01:15:22.770
Some people would say, I found myself in this
room and it was on my TV from childhood, and

01:15:22.770 --> 01:15:26.191
all of a sudden it was playing all these things,
or I found myself in a hall of mirrors and

01:15:26.191 --> 01:15:27.191
it was playing all these things.

01:15:27.191 --> 01:15:33.949
So kind of like the context can be very different,
the mind seems to shape that, but the actual

01:15:33.949 --> 01:15:36.320
replay seems to be pretty stereotyped.

01:15:36.320 --> 01:15:41.040
Tim Ferriss: Stereotyped, meaning like it's
a pattern that repeats or it is just like

01:15:41.040 --> 01:15:42.040
a common characteristic.

01:15:42.040 --> 01:15:45.239
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's a common characteristic,
and it's closed-eye only.

01:15:45.239 --> 01:15:48.600
It's not open eyes, it's not for a lot of
people.

01:15:48.600 --> 01:15:55.650
The ibogaine experience is kind of really
around this part, this kind of replay part

01:15:55.650 --> 01:15:56.650
that happens.

01:15:56.650 --> 01:16:03.690
And from what I have heard, there's kind of
a cathartic re-evaluation of these memories,

01:16:03.690 --> 01:16:09.731
and interestingly, an ability to not only
have insights into your own reasons, which

01:16:09.731 --> 01:16:16.989
may have been good or bad for feeling or behaving
or doing whatever it was, but also reasons

01:16:16.989 --> 01:16:22.530
for they have some insight and the reasons
for the other, which, to me, is the hardest

01:16:22.530 --> 01:16:24.699
thing to explain.

01:16:24.699 --> 01:16:26.211
It's almost like you knew it the whole time.

01:16:26.211 --> 01:16:27.211
Tim Ferriss: Can you say it one more time?

01:16:27.211 --> 01:16:28.211
Say the last part again?

01:16:28.211 --> 01:16:33.449
Dr. Nolan Williams: When people have this
slideshow or life review, they seem to have

01:16:33.449 --> 01:16:37.340
it as a third-party person in the experience.

01:16:37.340 --> 01:16:42.200
What is this Christmas Carol, Scrooge, or
whatever, when they go back, they go back

01:16:42.200 --> 01:16:44.570
in life and you see —
Tim Ferriss: You become the observer of your

01:16:44.570 --> 01:16:45.650
own past experience.

01:16:45.650 --> 01:16:46.650
Dr. Nolan Williams: Right?

01:16:46.650 --> 01:16:47.650
It's kind of like that.

01:16:47.650 --> 01:16:49.159
Scrooge goes back and sees himself as a kid.

01:16:49.159 --> 01:16:53.040
He sees himself as when he broke up with his
significant, all that stuff.

01:16:53.040 --> 01:16:56.840
It's almost like that, like you're having
a similar sort of thing where I've used the

01:16:56.840 --> 01:17:02.070
analogy of Tom Cruise in Minority Report,
where you're able to go back and see these

01:17:02.070 --> 01:17:03.070
events.

01:17:03.070 --> 01:17:07.690
And what's so fascinating, and why I've said,
and I continue to say I think this is the

01:17:07.690 --> 01:17:12.610
most sophisticated drug on the planet, is
that I think that there's nothing else that

01:17:12.610 --> 01:17:17.210
can seem to do this, right, where you have
these experiences where you're able to hold

01:17:17.210 --> 01:17:22.830
this neutral place, and you're able to have
this sense of where you were and why you did

01:17:22.830 --> 01:17:23.830
what you did.

01:17:23.830 --> 01:17:30.550
And you have this sense of the other, and
you're able to, I don't know, intuit, empathize,

01:17:30.550 --> 01:17:37.050
I don't know what that is, you somehow stored
it at the time and you weren't able to fully

01:17:37.050 --> 01:17:40.340
access it and you're able to access it, whatever
that is.

01:17:40.340 --> 01:17:47.430
Being able to forgive or to forget or to understand
this person's position as well as your own,

01:17:47.430 --> 01:17:54.840
and then seemingly unlock the lock on both
sides and then kind of dissolve the problem.

01:17:54.840 --> 01:17:58.050
And people will say, they kind of would work
through all of this.

01:17:58.050 --> 01:18:03.679
And there's one veteran would say, "Well,
I went through all this military stuff, and

01:18:03.679 --> 01:18:08.350
at the back of the room it was my early childhood
trauma."

01:18:08.350 --> 01:18:14.230
And so this idea that, at the core of it,
for a lot of this ends up being a childhood

01:18:14.230 --> 01:18:15.940
trauma thing that's kind of buried below all
of it.

01:18:15.940 --> 01:18:21.550
And really being able to actually both access
that in a way that you can understand that,

01:18:21.550 --> 01:18:28.160
in many cases, the traumatizer was themselves
traumatized and that it's a pattern of trauma

01:18:28.160 --> 01:18:37.720
and the ability to kind of resolve it by understanding
and this kind of more meta empathic viewpoint.

01:18:37.720 --> 01:18:43.760
And so that's where I think the tool's really
going to be important is this ability to have

01:18:43.760 --> 01:18:46.560
what seems to be a pretty profound or atrophic
effect.

01:18:46.560 --> 01:18:51.110
We were able to see disability improvements
from traumatic brain injury, but also this

01:18:51.110 --> 01:18:59.770
pretty pronounced cathartic reevaluation of
past life problematic memories.

01:18:59.770 --> 01:19:00.770
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:19:00.770 --> 01:19:05.900
As I'm wont to do, let me hop in with just
a few comments that we can bounce around if

01:19:05.900 --> 01:19:07.990
we want and then a whole bunch of questions.

01:19:07.990 --> 01:19:15.909
So the first comment is with the special operations
vets who are friends of mine, what I've observed,

01:19:15.909 --> 01:19:19.920
maybe similar to what you're describing, that
is, part of what has contributed to them being

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:27.670
extremely high performers in these high-stress
environments is their ability to tightly compartmentalize,

01:19:27.670 --> 01:19:31.890
which they developed when they were traumatized
as kids.

01:19:31.890 --> 01:19:35.320
Super high overlap, incredibly high overlap.

01:19:35.320 --> 01:19:38.760
Of course, there are many other factors that
contribute to them being the one person out

01:19:38.760 --> 01:19:46.170
of 10,000 who doesn't get washed out of training,
being that unbreakable in a sense.

01:19:46.170 --> 01:19:51.250
But with those friends, and look, it's a tiny
sample size, but of those friends, I wouldn't

01:19:51.250 --> 01:19:56.469
say any of them would claim to have PTSD or
moral injury, like a feeling of having done

01:19:56.469 --> 01:19:57.890
the wrong thing.

01:19:57.890 --> 01:20:02.940
But the TBI, and this is where I want to lead
into a question, which is not to negate the

01:20:02.940 --> 01:20:06.881
fact that a lot of people could have, of course,
meet the diagnoses for complex PTSD and so

01:20:06.881 --> 01:20:08.350
on with the TBI.

01:20:08.350 --> 01:20:12.560
What makes ibogaine different from some of
these other psychedelics?

01:20:12.560 --> 01:20:17.760
And I want to say one maybe place to explore
would be glial cells.

01:20:17.760 --> 01:20:19.600
Am I making this up?

01:20:19.600 --> 01:20:21.530
Dr. Nolan Williams: Glial-derived neurotrophic
factor, yeah.

01:20:21.530 --> 01:20:22.530
Tim Ferriss: Glial-derived neurotrophic factor.

01:20:22.530 --> 01:20:23.530
There we go.

01:20:23.530 --> 01:20:28.150
What makes it different in terms of mechanisms
of action, therapeutic effect, compared to

01:20:28.150 --> 01:20:32.530
some molecules people might be more familiar
with — psilocybin or otherwise?

01:20:32.530 --> 01:20:37.710
Dr. Nolan Williams: The classic psychedelics,
like psilocybin, as you know, primarily affect

01:20:37.710 --> 01:20:39.110
the 5-HT2A receptors.

01:20:39.110 --> 01:20:45.610
So there are 5-HT2A agonist, they produce
these kind of classic psychedelic experiences

01:20:45.610 --> 01:20:51.771
from largely from that receptor you can selectively
knock out 5-HT2A receptors, you can knock

01:20:51.771 --> 01:20:52.780
out the psychedelic effect.

01:20:52.780 --> 01:20:58.620
So we've largely thought about this class
of psychedelics in that way.

01:20:58.620 --> 01:21:03.480
Ibogaine is a known neurogen, so it produces
a dreamlike state.

01:21:03.480 --> 01:21:06.350
Some people call it an atypical psychedelic.

01:21:06.350 --> 01:21:10.940
We've elected to use this neurogen term because
I think it more accurately reflects what's

01:21:10.940 --> 01:21:15.880
going on in the trip and in the way of perceiving
what it is.

01:21:15.880 --> 01:21:20.770
It does have some 5-HT2A action, but that's
probably the minority of the effect.

01:21:20.770 --> 01:21:25.420
It affects a broad range of other receptor
systems.

01:21:25.420 --> 01:21:28.050
So salvia for instance, is a kappa agonist,
right?

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:34.270
And so there's kappa mu effects, there's NMDA
effects with ibogaine, there's SERT effects,

01:21:34.270 --> 01:21:35.290
so serotonin effects.

01:21:35.290 --> 01:21:41.040
And then there's this upregulation of BDNF,
brain-derived neurotrophic factor, and GDNF,

01:21:41.040 --> 01:21:45.850
glial-derived neurotrophic factor, and both
of those are profound neurotrophic factors

01:21:45.850 --> 01:21:48.710
for plasticity in the brain.

01:21:48.710 --> 01:21:54.130
The problem is this has been, psychedelics
were thought of as relatively obscure 15,

01:21:54.130 --> 01:21:55.500
20 years ago to study.

01:21:55.500 --> 01:21:58.980
Obviously that's changed now, but ibogaine
is extremely obscure.

01:21:58.980 --> 01:22:05.100
So there were a handful of studies that were
performed and published in good journals over

01:22:05.100 --> 01:22:08.030
time, but it's very limited in the data that
we have so far.

01:22:08.030 --> 01:22:11.450
So it's hard to give you some sort of definitive
answer of what's going on in the brain.

01:22:11.450 --> 01:22:15.270
And so what I can tell you is that it was
a paper published, I think 15 or 20 years

01:22:15.270 --> 01:22:18.200
ago, where they took mice and they got them
to self-administer alcohol.

01:22:18.200 --> 01:22:23.040
So that's kind of a way of an addiction model
or whatever, an alcohol self-administration

01:22:23.040 --> 01:22:28.900
or sucrose self-administration, sugar self-administration,
it's like a way of getting, you can put cocaine

01:22:28.900 --> 01:22:30.960
in the water and get mice to —
Tim Ferriss: Modeling addiction.

01:22:30.960 --> 01:22:31.980
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, exactly.

01:22:31.980 --> 01:22:36.630
And so if you give a mouse ibogaine, you can
get them to stop self-administering alcohol.

01:22:36.630 --> 01:22:41.429
It's interesting, we saw that in humans that
stopped drinking as well in our study, or

01:22:41.429 --> 01:22:45.880
in this case, you could actually drill a small
hole in the mouse's brain, you can inject

01:22:45.880 --> 01:22:50.801
GDNF directly into the ventral tegmental area,
the area that produces dopamine for more of

01:22:50.801 --> 01:22:52.650
the pleasure-seeking part of the brain.

01:22:52.650 --> 01:22:58.540
And it emulated the same effect as the ibogaine,
so this GDNF effect in the dopamine system

01:22:58.540 --> 01:23:01.610
at least, and GDNF is thought to regulate
dopamine neurons.

01:23:01.610 --> 01:23:05.330
And so I think that that's probably at least
a pretty strong part of it and what makes

01:23:05.330 --> 01:23:06.330
it so unique.

01:23:06.330 --> 01:23:09.400
But we don't, I always tell people when I'm
talking about this ibogaine stuff, if we gave

01:23:09.400 --> 01:23:18.340
one of the big pharma companies a hundred
billion dollars and said, "Don't just re-synthesize

01:23:18.340 --> 01:23:22.000
ibogaine, but make a drug that works like
ibogaine," or, I think even some of the classic

01:23:22.000 --> 01:23:25.690
psychedelics, but really specifically ibogaine,
I think they'd have a hard time doing it because

01:23:25.690 --> 01:23:29.080
we don't have the neuroscience to understand
what's going on there.

01:23:29.080 --> 01:23:32.190
And I think it's because it's not a one receptor.

01:23:32.190 --> 01:23:35.150
Tim Ferriss: It's not super clean in that
way.

01:23:35.150 --> 01:23:36.150
It's like promiscuous.

01:23:36.150 --> 01:23:38.290
It's like —
Dr. Nolan Williams: People use that term "dirty

01:23:38.290 --> 01:23:40.620
drug," I think it's —
Tim Ferriss: It has the wrong connotation,

01:23:40.620 --> 01:23:41.620
but yeah.

01:23:41.620 --> 01:23:42.620
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:23:42.620 --> 01:23:43.620
Tim Ferriss: LSD is pretty kind of promiscuous.

01:23:43.620 --> 01:23:44.960
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, I think promiscuous,
I like to think about —

01:23:44.960 --> 01:23:45.960
Tim Ferriss: Complex?

01:23:45.960 --> 01:23:46.960
Sophisticated?

01:23:46.960 --> 01:23:47.960
Dr. Nolan Williams: I like to think about
it like a symphony.

01:23:47.960 --> 01:23:48.960
Tim Ferriss: There we go.

01:23:48.960 --> 01:23:51.220
Dr. Nolan Williams: You're interacting with
these neurotransmitter systems in proportions

01:23:51.220 --> 01:23:55.320
in such a way that it produces this effect.

01:23:55.320 --> 01:24:01.639
My suspicion is that it's probably more sophisticated
than we want to attribute to, I mean maybe

01:24:01.639 --> 01:24:05.250
not you, but the scientific community wants
to attribute to nature being able to pull

01:24:05.250 --> 01:24:06.250
off.

01:24:06.250 --> 01:24:11.620
But it's this idea that maybe somehow we have
this drug that just happens to work the way

01:24:11.620 --> 01:24:17.400
it works because it's able to interact with
these systems in pretty important ratios or

01:24:17.400 --> 01:24:20.500
pretty important kind of simultaneous effects.

01:24:20.500 --> 01:24:22.260
And that's really what's driving it.

01:24:22.260 --> 01:24:24.540
And that's the part I'm saying it's going
to be hard to reproduce.

01:24:24.540 --> 01:24:30.060
I mean obviously Sasha Shulgin and others
were able to take/emulate similar sort of

01:24:30.060 --> 01:24:37.420
5-HT2A effects, but I don't even think he
was able to produce an ibogaine-like multi-receptor

01:24:37.420 --> 01:24:38.820
sort of symphony like this.

01:24:38.820 --> 01:24:41.730
There hasn't really been another drug like
it in this way.

01:24:41.730 --> 01:24:48.550
And so trying to think about what that is
and really how to study it's going to take

01:24:48.550 --> 01:24:54.590
a new wave of neuroscience tools to be able
to capture all the effects in real time.

01:24:54.590 --> 01:24:59.380
Tim Ferriss: I have a symphony of follow-up
questions.

01:24:59.380 --> 01:25:03.580
And so I'll give, you can choose, this is
dealer's choice, you're the dealer.

01:25:03.580 --> 01:25:08.429
So we could go with and we will get to all
these, but improving the safety profile of

01:25:08.429 --> 01:25:12.550
ibogaine, which I should also say I've had
people reach out to me, which is always — can

01:25:12.550 --> 01:25:17.730
be very uncomfortable for me, but a friend
of a very close friend, and in this particular

01:25:17.730 --> 01:25:26.530
case, someone's sister was a heroin addict
who is now homeless, acting as a prostitute,

01:25:26.530 --> 01:25:29.510
living under an overpass.

01:25:29.510 --> 01:25:34.200
And the reason I bring up all that level of
detail is that for a lot of interventions

01:25:34.200 --> 01:25:39.719
in this case, ibogaine at that point, the
cardiac risk or some of the risks were known.

01:25:39.719 --> 01:25:41.520
And there's a question of is this risky?

01:25:41.520 --> 01:25:43.890
And the follow-up is compared to what?

01:25:43.890 --> 01:25:47.760
And in this particular conversation it was,
well, she could die tomorrow from an OD or

01:25:47.760 --> 01:25:49.260
this and the other thing.

01:25:49.260 --> 01:25:51.830
So I'm just kind of setting the table with
that.

01:25:51.830 --> 01:25:55.340
But improving the safety profile is one question.

01:25:55.340 --> 01:26:00.900
Another, because you mentioned the cessation
of, or the minimizing of the AUD, so people

01:26:00.900 --> 01:26:02.690
stopping drinking.

01:26:02.690 --> 01:26:07.560
We were also talking before recording people
stopping drinking caffeine I guess, or coffee.

01:26:07.560 --> 01:26:09.369
Dr. Nolan Williams: We saw that.

01:26:09.369 --> 01:26:10.369
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:26:10.369 --> 01:26:11.369
Unexpected presumably.

01:26:11.369 --> 01:26:14.670
And so my question there, which I sort of
seeded because I mentioned I would want to

01:26:14.670 --> 01:26:19.260
talk about this, it's like any overlap in
terms of mechanism around how does this compare

01:26:19.260 --> 01:26:24.400
to something like Semaglutide or an Ozempic
or some of the newer gen, I think Mounjaro,

01:26:24.400 --> 01:26:25.400
maybe I'm getting that wrong.

01:26:25.400 --> 01:26:29.360
And then the last one, and I've been wanting
to ask somebody about this.

01:26:29.360 --> 01:26:36.679
So you mentioned 5-HT2A, it seems like some
psychedelics exert effects also on 5-HT2B

01:26:36.679 --> 01:26:38.199
receptors.

01:26:38.199 --> 01:26:45.190
And if I'm not mistaken, there's some data
to suggest that that can — continued stimulation,

01:26:45.190 --> 01:26:51.140
agonism of that B can lead to some type of
cardiac complication as well, some type of

01:26:51.140 --> 01:26:53.730
ventricular hypertrophy —
Dr. Nolan Williams: Heart valves.

01:26:53.730 --> 01:26:54.840
Tim Ferriss: Valves.

01:26:54.840 --> 01:26:55.840
Exactly.

01:26:55.840 --> 01:27:00.719
So the question there is do you think that
microdosing, for instance, which has become

01:27:00.719 --> 01:27:06.710
all the rage, could that potentially have
long-term negative cardiac effects?

01:27:06.710 --> 01:27:07.710
Dr. Nolan Williams: And not to derail too
much, yeah, exactly.

01:27:07.710 --> 01:27:11.820
The FDA clearance, the FDA guidance document
for psychedelics that they released actually

01:27:11.820 --> 01:27:17.190
on the last day of the psychedelic science
meeting, which is really interesting, specifically

01:27:17.190 --> 01:27:21.820
has a section about this issue of cardiac
valvular problems, particularly from these

01:27:21.820 --> 01:27:23.480
more classic psychedelics.

01:27:23.480 --> 01:27:26.720
The problem with ibogaine is different.

01:27:26.720 --> 01:27:30.170
The problem with ibogaine is that it interacts
with hERG potassium channel.

01:27:30.170 --> 01:27:37.770
There are a number of groups, folks at Columbia,
folks at UC Davis who have looked at this

01:27:37.770 --> 01:27:44.050
and their solution is to modify the molecule
and to affect it in such a way that it no

01:27:44.050 --> 01:27:45.380
longer interacts with hERG.

01:27:45.380 --> 01:27:48.340
Tim Ferriss: Do you mean noribogaine or something
like that?

01:27:48.340 --> 01:27:50.650
Or is it more of a —
Dr. Nolan Williams: Noribogaine, the primary

01:27:50.650 --> 01:27:56.290
metabolite of ibogaine that goes after 2D6
is a normal part of the ibogaine process,

01:27:56.290 --> 01:27:57.610
has a similar cardiac risk profile.

01:27:57.610 --> 01:27:58.610
Tim Ferriss: Oh, it does.

01:27:58.610 --> 01:28:03.040
Dr. Nolan Williams: So it's drugs that are,
I think they call them like tabernanthe logs

01:28:03.040 --> 01:28:10.239
or something, but, iboga logs or something,
I think is what was coined at Davis, but essentially

01:28:10.239 --> 01:28:17.500
these are drugs where they try to engineer
off that interaction, which may or may not

01:28:17.500 --> 01:28:18.500
have an effect on its brain effects.

01:28:18.500 --> 01:28:20.650
I mean generally, and so I want to answer
the question I'm trying to ask, and the question

01:28:20.650 --> 01:28:27.140
I'm trying to ask is, does ibogaine help with
certain human illness by modifying the molecule?

01:28:27.140 --> 01:28:28.989
It's not ibogaine anymore, it's something
else.

01:28:28.989 --> 01:28:32.190
And then you're asking the question, is this
something else help — the view is maybe

01:28:32.190 --> 01:28:34.900
it's close enough to ibogaine to do something
similar.

01:28:34.900 --> 01:28:40.280
And I've taken a different stance on this
where I've basically taken the frame of how

01:28:40.280 --> 01:28:47.580
do you preserve the molecule and really lean
in on the cardiac risk?

01:28:47.580 --> 01:28:52.280
Tim Ferriss: How do you put an airbag in the
car instead of redesigning the whole car?

01:28:52.280 --> 01:28:53.280
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:28:53.280 --> 01:28:56.480
Instead of making it 3an airborne shuttle
or something, I don't know, whatever.

01:28:56.480 --> 01:29:01.210
So it's putting an airbag in with a high likelihood
of saving the person.

01:29:01.210 --> 01:29:07.920
And so we talk about this in the article,
but essentially all the patients that were

01:29:07.920 --> 01:29:14.270
treated received torsades, like IV magnesium
at the start.

01:29:14.270 --> 01:29:16.929
Tim Ferriss: What type of magnesium?

01:29:16.929 --> 01:29:18.250
Dr. Nolan Williams: So it's like magnesium
sulfate.

01:29:18.250 --> 01:29:20.750
So it's basically just like IV bag magnesium.

01:29:20.750 --> 01:29:25.270
And so what's really interesting about this
arrhythmia torsades that everybody's worried

01:29:25.270 --> 01:29:29.320
about is that the treatment for it per the
American Heart Association guidelines is to

01:29:29.320 --> 01:29:33.489
give IV magnesium, which is incredibly safe.

01:29:33.489 --> 01:29:39.210
And I've given many times for various things
in the ER and you can just give it to people

01:29:39.210 --> 01:29:42.610
and you'll eventually urinate it out or whatever.

01:29:42.610 --> 01:29:50.020
And so you can give it, get people through
this risk period, and then they may be slightly

01:29:50.020 --> 01:29:53.429
hyper magnesiumic or a little high on their
magnesium in their blood and they'll go out

01:29:53.429 --> 01:29:54.940
and everything's all good.

01:29:54.940 --> 01:29:59.690
And there've been about a thousand operators
that have gone down to Mexico so far.

01:29:59.690 --> 01:30:02.601
And to my knowledge, and we looked into this
pretty significantly, there hasn't been a

01:30:02.601 --> 01:30:06.150
single case of torsades.

01:30:06.150 --> 01:30:08.659
Conversely, the New Zealand study that was
published a couple of years ago, they had

01:30:08.659 --> 01:30:10.440
a death out of 10 people.

01:30:10.440 --> 01:30:16.050
And so we are not doing head-to-head, I can't
tell you for sure what the deal is there,

01:30:16.050 --> 01:30:22.290
but my suspicion is that if you give, what
is the treatment for the problem you're worried

01:30:22.290 --> 01:30:28.150
about before you give the thing that can cause
that problem, then it's much more likely that

01:30:28.150 --> 01:30:31.350
you can knock out the risk or significantly
reduce the risk.

01:30:31.350 --> 01:30:34.520
It's also about doing it in a monitored bed
setting.

01:30:34.520 --> 01:30:41.300
So Tikosyn, T-I-K-O-S-Y-N, is a drug that
is approved for atrial fibrillation and it's

01:30:41.300 --> 01:30:46.030
an antiarrhythmic that can be pro-arrhythmic
in the same way as ibogaine and actually has

01:30:46.030 --> 01:30:48.590
more risk than ibogaine.

01:30:48.590 --> 01:30:53.280
The rates of torsades are higher with Tikosyn,
it's approved, and it's totally safe if you

01:30:53.280 --> 01:30:56.440
do it in a monitored cardiac bed.

01:30:56.440 --> 01:31:01.179
And so I think that we have to, if we're,
and my suspicion is if we're going to do a

01:31:01.179 --> 01:31:04.969
study in the US with ibogaine, we're just
going to need to do it in a monitored cardiac

01:31:04.969 --> 01:31:07.780
bed with cardiologists involved.

01:31:07.780 --> 01:31:10.160
And I think if you do that, you're good.

01:31:10.160 --> 01:31:17.300
I think the trick is between now and when
in theory one could see an approval eventually

01:31:17.300 --> 01:31:23.909
from the FDA for this, you're going to have
to think about, well, not just can this place

01:31:23.909 --> 01:31:30.420
in wherever Mexico, wherever it is, provide
a good pure form of ibogaine, but what's the

01:31:30.420 --> 01:31:32.570
wraparound risk reduction there?

01:31:32.570 --> 01:31:35.610
And I think that's what people have to think
about as they think about that, taking on

01:31:35.610 --> 01:31:36.889
that level of risk.

01:31:36.889 --> 01:31:38.949
It's not trivial risk, though.

01:31:38.949 --> 01:31:40.110
It's a real risk.

01:31:40.110 --> 01:31:45.929
I think the reason why the veterans that we
studied were so interested in going is because,

01:31:45.929 --> 01:31:49.270
as you point out, there's a lot of risks from
not doing things too.

01:31:49.270 --> 01:31:51.630
Tim Ferriss: There's also a lot of social
proof in that community now.

01:31:51.630 --> 01:31:52.630
Dr. Nolan Williams: Oh, yeah.

01:31:52.630 --> 01:31:57.260
Tim Ferriss: And a friend will go down with
their friend to sit in the same room and it's

01:31:57.260 --> 01:31:58.260
a tight group, I mean.

01:31:58.260 --> 01:32:01.199
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's a super tight group
and it's pretty cool.

01:32:01.199 --> 01:32:06.610
And I think it's, the reason why I did the
trial ultimately was because my view was there's

01:32:06.610 --> 01:32:13.180
no way that all these very high performing
special operators are all going down to Mexico

01:32:13.180 --> 01:32:20.000
and taking this thing, which is not recreational
at all, and spending a week down there dealing

01:32:20.000 --> 01:32:25.760
with this and then they're not really getting
benefit, they're getting some sort of pure

01:32:25.760 --> 01:32:26.760
placebo effect.

01:32:26.760 --> 01:32:31.400
It just seemed unlikely to me given how treatment-resistant
and how many things that these folks had done,

01:32:31.400 --> 01:32:34.070
how much time they'd spent in the VA hospital
and all that good stuff.

01:32:34.070 --> 01:32:35.940
And so that's why we did it.

01:32:35.940 --> 01:32:42.070
And what we found was enough of a consistent
finding and this reversal of disability such

01:32:42.070 --> 01:32:46.929
that the, well, I can't tell you this works
because I don't have that level of evidence

01:32:46.929 --> 01:32:50.340
on it, that the signal that this could work
is really high.

01:32:50.340 --> 01:32:55.940
It's the highest of any kind of brain-acting
drug I've been involved in looking at.

01:32:55.940 --> 01:32:56.940
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:32:56.940 --> 01:33:01.080
Let's talk about the alcohol and the caffeine.

01:33:01.080 --> 01:33:07.510
So mechanism of action, I mean, you sort of
mentioned the GDNF in the animal models, the

01:33:07.510 --> 01:33:10.960
direct kind of injection.

01:33:10.960 --> 01:33:17.290
How do you think about this and does it in
any way tie into what we're seeing with some

01:33:17.290 --> 01:33:24.330
of these drugs that are, I guess designed
for type two diabetics, the semaglutide and

01:33:24.330 --> 01:33:29.290
Ozempic-like drugs, but they're having such
an effect on various types of cravings that

01:33:29.290 --> 01:33:35.230
large box retailers are having to revise,
sort of in a panic, their sales projections

01:33:35.230 --> 01:33:38.070
and inventory planning around snack foods
and stuff.

01:33:38.070 --> 01:33:41.790
I mean, it's wild to see the societal ripple
effects.

01:33:41.790 --> 01:33:45.480
Dr. Nolan Williams: Walmart apparently has
sold less food.

01:33:45.480 --> 01:33:50.699
Tim Ferriss: And I have friends who have been
obese effectively their entire lives, never

01:33:50.699 --> 01:33:56.260
had an exercise habit, and now they're doing
pull-ups for the first time, which is again,

01:33:56.260 --> 01:34:00.760
not an endorsement because I don't understand,
and maybe nobody really quite understands

01:34:00.760 --> 01:34:03.860
exactly how these things work, but I certainly
don't, so I'm not yet there.

01:34:03.860 --> 01:34:08.119
But how do you think about this, I mean, are
there any overlaps with these?

01:34:08.119 --> 01:34:11.070
Are they completely different mechanisms just
presenting similarly?

01:34:11.070 --> 01:34:13.380
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's a great question.

01:34:13.380 --> 01:34:17.750
There's no evidence that there's a direct
overlap in mechanism just because I think

01:34:17.750 --> 01:34:23.780
the kind of CNS kind of psych addictions side
of things, people are still in the like, "Is

01:34:23.780 --> 01:34:24.780
this doing something yet?"

01:34:24.780 --> 01:34:27.469
It looks like it's doing something there,
but not in any deeper than that.

01:34:27.469 --> 01:34:31.290
I think we'll learn about that on the Ozempic
drug side of things.

01:34:31.290 --> 01:34:36.820
And then ibogaine has been relatively underexplored
from basic science, science and technology.

01:34:36.820 --> 01:34:43.550
And my suspicion is, is that is probably similar
enough and maybe some of the same mechanisms

01:34:43.550 --> 01:34:49.350
are being enacted because what you're finding
is a similar phenomenon across both instances.

01:34:49.350 --> 01:34:54.270
We thought these were diabetes drugs and then
there was a significant weight loss observed,

01:34:54.270 --> 01:34:58.110
and then we thought these were weight loss
drugs and then everybody's quitting all their

01:34:58.110 --> 01:35:00.250
other habits.

01:35:00.250 --> 01:35:06.540
And placebo works by a phenomenon called expectancy.

01:35:06.540 --> 01:35:11.389
And as you can expect from the term, it's
expecting something.

01:35:11.389 --> 01:35:16.449
So you're primed to think that this thing's
going to help for this reason.

01:35:16.449 --> 01:35:25.030
I'm always really clued in when you have really
obscure off target, off non-expectancy phenomenon

01:35:25.030 --> 01:35:26.320
happening.

01:35:26.320 --> 01:35:31.360
So in our ibogaine trial, as you mentioned
a minute ago, we had basically everybody quit

01:35:31.360 --> 01:35:37.260
drinking coffee for a period of time and none
of them really went into it as most of us

01:35:37.260 --> 01:35:42.389
don't, our coffee habit is maybe a concern.

01:35:42.389 --> 01:35:46.380
It looks like coffee is protective against
Parkinson's and some other things, so it's

01:35:46.380 --> 01:35:51.510
kind of a mixed bag about that, but it's generally
the thing we're the least worried about trying

01:35:51.510 --> 01:35:58.290
to deal with, as a generality people are much
more focused on dealing with being overweight

01:35:58.290 --> 01:36:03.310
or focused on their alcohol use problem, or
in this case, PTSD and depression and TBI

01:36:03.310 --> 01:36:05.730
and all these other things that folks were
worried about.

01:36:05.730 --> 01:36:12.800
And so when we started seeing consistent reports
of people stopping their caffeine intake,

01:36:12.800 --> 01:36:14.210
it was really a signal for me.

01:36:14.210 --> 01:36:15.880
I was like, you know what?

01:36:15.880 --> 01:36:16.880
That's really interesting.

01:36:16.880 --> 01:36:20.969
I mean, there's probably a bigger systemic
change that's happening.

01:36:20.969 --> 01:36:28.500
And what people will tell you, phenomenologically
about this, is it puts a pause in between

01:36:28.500 --> 01:36:30.199
stimulus and response.

01:36:30.199 --> 01:36:33.720
A phenomenal, and I can't prove this, this
is just what everybody seems to come back

01:36:33.720 --> 01:36:40.030
and say it puts a period of time between when
you normally have a trigger to do something

01:36:40.030 --> 01:36:45.010
and go do it and it was a habitual action
and really kind of gets into this question

01:36:45.010 --> 01:36:49.530
of free will and all this stuff that people
think about, where there's a moment where

01:36:49.530 --> 01:36:57.210
instead of making the habitual action, the
person finds themselves in a purely unbiased

01:36:57.210 --> 01:36:58.210
choice phenomenon.

01:36:58.210 --> 01:37:03.949
And what people who have opiate-use disorder
would say in those scenarios when they would

01:37:03.949 --> 01:37:08.130
relapse after ibogaine is they'd say, "You
know what?

01:37:08.130 --> 01:37:09.480
I had something about it.

01:37:09.480 --> 01:37:10.480
I didn't crave it.

01:37:10.480 --> 01:37:15.119
I just wanted to do it or something wanted
to remember," or whatever, but it wasn't this

01:37:15.119 --> 01:37:16.119
habitual action.

01:37:16.119 --> 01:37:22.120
And they'd go back and they do ibogaine again
after they'd gone back into the addiction

01:37:22.120 --> 01:37:25.280
and then they had all the negative consequences
and they say, "You know what?"

01:37:25.280 --> 01:37:29.850
And they got back to that choice again and
they're like, "No, it's not worth it anymore.

01:37:29.850 --> 01:37:31.320
I'm going to go this way."

01:37:31.320 --> 01:37:34.480
But they were able to pause and make those
decisions.

01:37:34.480 --> 01:37:39.139
And it sounds like from talking to the various
veterans that have gone through our study

01:37:39.139 --> 01:37:44.230
is that they'd approach things like coffee
and they'd be like, "I do this, but do I need

01:37:44.230 --> 01:37:45.370
to do it?"

01:37:45.370 --> 01:37:50.460
Which is really striking actually, it's not
something that people typically tend to do.

01:37:50.460 --> 01:37:56.020
You get into all these habitual actions and
your life is just made up of a lot of habitual

01:37:56.020 --> 01:37:59.869
actions and they were able to reevaluate all
those habitual actions and then establish

01:37:59.869 --> 01:38:01.389
new patterns.

01:38:01.389 --> 01:38:07.270
The drug eventually is going to wear off,
as all drugs eventually do, and you probably

01:38:07.270 --> 01:38:10.020
do lay down a level of habituating again.

01:38:10.020 --> 01:38:17.040
But if you could, during the period of time
when your brain seems to be pretty plastic,

01:38:17.040 --> 01:38:22.449
shift and lay down new patterns that when
the drug wears off assumably, you kind of

01:38:22.449 --> 01:38:24.280
lock into that new set of patterns.

01:38:24.280 --> 01:38:29.500
And I think that's really interesting and
probably a little bit different than the Ozempics

01:38:29.500 --> 01:38:35.530
in the sense that the Ozempics seem to kind
of take away from what folks will say a lot

01:38:35.530 --> 01:38:39.889
of the seeking or rewarding aspects of things
with food or whatever.

01:38:39.889 --> 01:38:41.030
It's just —
Tim Ferriss: Like cravings.

01:38:41.030 --> 01:38:42.030
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, cravings.

01:38:42.030 --> 01:38:45.260
Whereas this seems to introduce a level of
choice.

01:38:45.260 --> 01:38:51.219
What would be very interesting, so you alluded
to this earlier, is you take somebody who

01:38:51.219 --> 01:38:56.520
has a pretty significant addiction like this
and you give them an ibogaine sort of drug

01:38:56.520 --> 01:39:01.410
and then you think about how do you use something
like an Ozempic to kind of follow on with

01:39:01.410 --> 01:39:02.410
that, right?

01:39:02.410 --> 01:39:05.160
Where you're able to gate some of it and it's
way out there.

01:39:05.160 --> 01:39:08.830
And probably not anything anybody's going
to do anytime soon, but it's an interesting

01:39:08.830 --> 01:39:14.860
idea of these are essentially habit-affecting
drugs that we haven't had tools to really

01:39:14.860 --> 01:39:16.940
use before, which I think is pretty cool.

01:39:16.940 --> 01:39:22.690
Tim Ferriss: And folks who want to dive deeper
into this sort of reopening of, let's call

01:39:22.690 --> 01:39:27.340
it critical windows, I mean I'm borrowing
that terminology from Professor Gül Dölen,

01:39:27.340 --> 01:39:34.469
but pretty fun stuff to dig into as well,
if you want to scientifically, at least for

01:39:34.469 --> 01:39:40.280
the time being, reinforce what a lot of experienced
facilitators have been saying for a long time,

01:39:40.280 --> 01:39:42.929
which is the post-period matters a lot.

01:39:42.929 --> 01:39:47.909
If you're going to have knee surgery, make
sure you do your rehab.

01:39:47.909 --> 01:39:54.380
All right, let's hop to a few other questions.

01:39:54.380 --> 01:39:57.230
One is coming back to ibogaine specifically.

01:39:57.230 --> 01:40:01.670
The two things that have been of greatest
interest to me with respect to ibogaine are

01:40:01.670 --> 01:40:08.989
TBI, right, so if someone has no addiction,
no PTSD, the only issue they have is some

01:40:08.989 --> 01:40:13.830
form of TBI, do you think there is a role
for ibogaine so we can place hold that, there's

01:40:13.830 --> 01:40:18.570
part two to this question, which is I've heard
reports and I haven't gone into the literature

01:40:18.570 --> 01:40:19.570
on this site.

01:40:19.570 --> 01:40:22.970
I don't know, maybe this has been explained
somewhere, but I've certainly heard reports

01:40:22.970 --> 01:40:31.820
of opiate addicts who have seemed to indicate
that ibogaine or iboga opens a window after

01:40:31.820 --> 01:40:37.489
treatment during which they do not seem to
experience much in terms of withdrawal symptoms.

01:40:37.489 --> 01:40:41.349
And I want to know what the hell is going
on there if that seems to be observed.

01:40:41.349 --> 01:40:42.840
So those are the two questions.

01:40:42.840 --> 01:40:46.450
Somebody who just has TBI, do you think there
might be a role for an ibogaine or something

01:40:46.450 --> 01:40:52.670
like it, compared to other options, and then
the seeming diminishing or disappearance of

01:40:52.670 --> 01:40:54.320
for a period of time, physical withdrawal
symptoms?

01:40:54.320 --> 01:40:58.260
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it's a little tough
with the cohort that we studied because were,

01:40:58.260 --> 01:40:59.369
it was military TBI.

01:40:59.369 --> 01:41:08.570
While I understand that the PTSD diagnosis
is pretty ubiquitous in the system, it's probably

01:41:08.570 --> 01:41:12.340
true in a fair amount of folks, especially
folks who've been exposed to a lot of combat

01:41:12.340 --> 01:41:17.610
related trauma and earlier life trauma that
in our group, it seemed to be there in most

01:41:17.610 --> 01:41:20.970
of the participants, so was depression and
anxiety.

01:41:20.970 --> 01:41:23.300
And so we haven't studied a pure TBI group.

01:41:23.300 --> 01:41:30.739
And so the confound is without studying a
pure TBI group that somehow had, maybe was

01:41:30.739 --> 01:41:33.520
a competitor in martial arts or something
like that, right?

01:41:33.520 --> 01:41:36.950
Tim Ferriss: Multiple concussions or whatever,
Dr. Nolan Williams: Something like that where

01:41:36.950 --> 01:41:40.450
they intended to potentially get hit in the
head.

01:41:40.450 --> 01:41:43.429
Tim Ferriss: Probably intended to hit other
people in the head.

01:41:43.429 --> 01:41:47.640
Dr. Nolan Williams: Well for sure, but intended
to go into the ring knowing —

01:41:47.640 --> 01:41:48.640
Tim Ferriss: You're going to get hit.

01:41:48.640 --> 01:41:49.719
Dr. Nolan Williams: That you were probably
going to get hit and that it wasn't, because

01:41:49.719 --> 01:41:54.469
the problem is in a lot of these cases, motor
vehicle accidents, there's really high rates

01:41:54.469 --> 01:42:02.170
of PTSD in those because there's not an expectation
that you're about to get hit in the head,

01:42:02.170 --> 01:42:06.610
but there's probably a population you could
study that's pure traumatic brain injury where

01:42:06.610 --> 01:42:07.610
—
Tim Ferriss: Like football players, right?

01:42:07.610 --> 01:42:08.610
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, could be football.

01:42:08.610 --> 01:42:10.909
Tim Ferriss: A lot of reported depression,
who knows?

01:42:10.909 --> 01:42:11.929
There could be a lot of other factors involved,
but —

01:42:11.929 --> 01:42:12.929
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, football players
is another great example.

01:42:12.929 --> 01:42:15.840
Tim Ferriss: Highly correlated, yeah.

01:42:15.840 --> 01:42:23.520
Dr. Nolan Williams: And those guys know they're
going to hit their head in the game probably

01:42:23.520 --> 01:42:24.520
at some point.

01:42:24.520 --> 01:42:25.810
And so that population where it's more of
a pure TBI population, you could ask that

01:42:25.810 --> 01:42:32.570
same question that we asked in this more mixed
population, which is does TBI disability improve?

01:42:32.570 --> 01:42:37.020
We saw a dramatic improvement in TBI disability
at the one month mark, less actually right

01:42:37.020 --> 01:42:39.170
after it really took a while for it to work,
which is —

01:42:39.170 --> 01:42:43.890
Tim Ferriss: If you had to guess, we're two
drinks in, and I'm like, no, just give me

01:42:43.890 --> 01:42:46.920
your wild-ass guess mechanistically, what's
going on here?

01:42:46.920 --> 01:42:48.800
Dr. Nolan Williams: I mean, it's this symphony,
right?

01:42:48.800 --> 01:42:55.130
Which is sounds kind of like maybe even hokey
or a little less direct, but I just think

01:42:55.130 --> 01:43:00.520
that there's something about interacting somehow
with multiple different neurotransmitter systems

01:43:00.520 --> 01:43:03.820
at the same time that must produce this.

01:43:03.820 --> 01:43:07.710
I mean, maybe the GDNF, BDNF alone could do
this.

01:43:07.710 --> 01:43:12.030
I'm not saying it can't, but I suspect that
it's some more complex process.

01:43:12.030 --> 01:43:18.610
And the problem is we don't have great tools
to evaluate that, but sometimes nature trumps

01:43:18.610 --> 01:43:20.679
our human scientific abilities.

01:43:20.679 --> 01:43:27.590
And I think that ibogaine certainly is there
in 2023, it's like the story of scurvy.

01:43:27.590 --> 01:43:33.500
We associated eventually after a bunch of
weird stuff, we eventually associated eating

01:43:33.500 --> 01:43:38.010
oranges with improvement in scurvy, but it
took us another a hundred years to synthesize

01:43:38.010 --> 01:43:42.719
vitamin C. And so I think what's so hard about
this scientifically, and to get the scientific

01:43:42.719 --> 01:43:46.570
community fully on board with these ideas,
is that we're likely going to figure out this

01:43:46.570 --> 01:43:48.520
works before we have any idea on how it works.

01:43:48.520 --> 01:43:49.520
Your second question, which is, I'm going
to answer it the same way, is that we don't,

01:43:49.520 --> 01:43:53.380
but it's very unique to ibogaine, and you
can take somebody who's going through florid

01:43:53.380 --> 01:43:58.909
opioid withdrawals and you can have them not
only have a cessation or stopping of a desire

01:43:58.909 --> 01:44:04.810
to go take more heroin or prescription opiates
or whatever it is, but you can have them basically

01:44:04.810 --> 01:44:11.469
blunt or completely attenuate the physical
withdrawal symptoms of withdrawing from opiate

01:44:11.469 --> 01:44:12.469
use from opiates, right?

01:44:12.469 --> 01:44:16.610
Which is diarrhea, like, headache, sweating,
all this stuff that people will go through

01:44:16.610 --> 01:44:19.409
when they're going into opiate withdrawals.

01:44:19.409 --> 01:44:23.620
And this seems to really knock that out.

01:44:23.620 --> 01:44:30.260
It's likely, again, that because it's interacting
with the opioid receptors, it's interacting

01:44:30.260 --> 01:44:31.880
with the glutamate system, but it is, to my
knowledge, none of the other psychedelics

01:44:31.880 --> 01:44:39.580
can do that, ketamine can't really do that,
so this is a pretty unique thing to ibogaine

01:44:39.580 --> 01:44:43.619
that it can pull this off and why I think
it's such an important drug to understand.

01:44:43.619 --> 01:44:49.739
I mean, I would argue that because of how
broad ibogaine seems to work across now most

01:44:49.739 --> 01:44:56.630
of the major psychiatric diagnoses with the
absence of something like schizophrenia, but

01:44:56.630 --> 01:44:58.110
anxiety, depression.

01:44:58.110 --> 01:45:02.969
There's traumatic brain injury, multiple different
addiction types.

01:45:02.969 --> 01:45:07.350
It's going to behoove the scientific community
to really break down why it does what it does

01:45:07.350 --> 01:45:12.840
over the next couple of decades, I think,
and try to really back engineer what it is,

01:45:12.840 --> 01:45:14.170
but, yeah, we have no idea.

01:45:14.170 --> 01:45:18.130
Tim Ferriss: Outside of the lab and more in
the wild, what are some of the more interesting

01:45:18.130 --> 01:45:20.770
things happening related to ibogaine and iboga?

01:45:20.770 --> 01:45:23.280
I believe there's something happening in Kentucky?

01:45:23.280 --> 01:45:24.280
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:45:24.280 --> 01:45:29.080
Yeah, so I've been down to Kentucky to testify
in front of the Opiate Abatement Commission

01:45:29.080 --> 01:45:30.179
of Kentucky.

01:45:30.179 --> 01:45:37.300
It was myself along with Srini Rao from Atai,
Deborah Mash, who's the CEO of DemeRx, was

01:45:37.300 --> 01:45:42.199
at the University of Miami for a while, and
Ken Alper, who was at NYU and is an academic

01:45:42.199 --> 01:45:43.199
private practice psychiatrist.

01:45:43.199 --> 01:45:46.630
And so there's a guy by the name of Bryan
Hubbard, which, he's a very interesting guy.

01:45:46.630 --> 01:45:50.780
He's an attorney by training who has worked
in a bunch of different domains within Kentucky

01:45:50.780 --> 01:45:52.610
State government and whatnot.

01:45:52.610 --> 01:45:59.139
And he, for one reason or another, has become
completely convinced that the money that ended

01:45:59.139 --> 01:46:06.130
up being some of the lawsuit money for some
of the opiate overprescription that happened,

01:46:06.130 --> 01:46:09.810
particularly in Kentucky and West Virginia,
but all over the country, should be utilized

01:46:09.810 --> 01:46:14.691
for novel therapeutics and not just for more
of the same sort of treatments that we have

01:46:14.691 --> 01:46:15.800
available.

01:46:15.800 --> 01:46:20.040
The conventional treatments that are available
for opiate-use disorder fall under the realm

01:46:20.040 --> 01:46:22.139
of what we think about as replacement therapy.

01:46:22.139 --> 01:46:26.550
You're replacing a higher-risk opiate with
a lower-risk opiate, so that's Suboxone, which

01:46:26.550 --> 01:46:32.199
contains buprenorphine and methadone, or opiate-blocking
drugs like Naltrexone.

01:46:32.199 --> 01:46:35.599
There's a fairly high fail rate on those drugs.

01:46:35.599 --> 01:46:37.060
Part of it is psychosocial.

01:46:37.060 --> 01:46:39.050
We put people in the right environment.

01:46:39.050 --> 01:46:41.020
You could probably drive up the rates of that.

01:46:41.020 --> 01:46:42.650
There's also a prescription issue.

01:46:42.650 --> 01:46:47.080
You used to have to have a special FDA license
to prescribe it.

01:46:47.080 --> 01:46:50.900
I used to have this thing and then recently
that was knocked out.

01:46:50.900 --> 01:46:53.699
So everybody with a medical license can prescribe
Suboxone.

01:46:53.699 --> 01:46:59.300
And so there's kind of a group of drugs that
can do some work on this, but there's certainly

01:46:59.300 --> 01:47:03.000
folks that we would think about as treatment-resistant
opiate-use disorder patients.

01:47:03.000 --> 01:47:07.760
And the numbers I'm not going to get into
because debated or whatever, but whatever

01:47:07.760 --> 01:47:12.830
those numbers are, it's a good chunk of folks
and they don't really have much to offer.

01:47:12.830 --> 01:47:17.610
And so people have thought about lots of different
options for them.

01:47:17.610 --> 01:47:19.940
And one of the options is brain surgery.

01:47:19.940 --> 01:47:25.690
So in West Virginia, a group of neurosurgeons
are actually doing full on, so there's a surgical

01:47:25.690 --> 01:47:30.350
form of neuromodulation called deep brain
stimulation where you can actually put an

01:47:30.350 --> 01:47:33.500
implanted device into the reward system.

01:47:33.500 --> 01:47:40.110
And also kind of similar to some of these
Wegovy kind of drugs, you can drive down some

01:47:40.110 --> 01:47:44.730
of the pleasure around, and this is more theoretical
at this point, but opiate-use, opiates as

01:47:44.730 --> 01:47:50.820
well as there's some data out of a couple
of different studies with even weight loss

01:47:50.820 --> 01:47:52.659
for stimulating in the reward circuitry.

01:47:52.659 --> 01:48:00.150
What's interesting is that there's a one-in-100
risk of a head bleed from that treatment,

01:48:00.150 --> 01:48:04.320
and about a third of them end up, it ends
up being a pretty significant head bleed,

01:48:04.320 --> 01:48:05.320
right?

01:48:05.320 --> 01:48:06.320
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:48:06.320 --> 01:48:10.280
Dr. Nolan Williams: And so what's really interesting
is you have no one in West Virginia organizing

01:48:10.280 --> 01:48:11.660
all these hearings about it.

01:48:11.660 --> 01:48:12.660
It's just happening.

01:48:12.660 --> 01:48:14.261
They're letting people do the science and
all that stuff.

01:48:14.261 --> 01:48:15.680
And I don't disagree with doing that, by the
way.

01:48:15.680 --> 01:48:21.260
I think it's a useful thing to explore, and
it may be a solution for this, and this is,

01:48:21.260 --> 01:48:27.300
as we described earlier, such a high-risk
phenomenon that a one-in-300 risk is not as

01:48:27.300 --> 01:48:28.300
bad.

01:48:28.300 --> 01:48:29.300
Tim Ferriss: Is it one-in-300 or one-in-100?

01:48:29.300 --> 01:48:30.300
Dr. Nolan Williams: One-in-100 risk of a DBS.

01:48:30.300 --> 01:48:31.300
Tim Ferriss: Of a head bleed.

01:48:31.300 --> 01:48:35.199
Dr. Nolan Williams: It can be a trivial, just
blood on the tip of the electrode, which is

01:48:35.199 --> 01:48:36.550
asymptomatic.

01:48:36.550 --> 01:48:40.570
About a third of those people are going to
be in a more, they'll have a complication,

01:48:40.570 --> 01:48:43.930
have a real complication, and then you've
got about a one-in-300 risk of a torsades

01:48:43.930 --> 01:48:44.930
risk with ibogaine.

01:48:44.930 --> 01:48:48.210
And so next door in Kentucky —
Tim Ferriss: Similar odds.

01:48:48.210 --> 01:48:49.210
Yeah.

01:48:49.210 --> 01:48:50.210
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:48:50.210 --> 01:48:55.800
You've got a similar odd concern, both of
which can be kind of dealt with in the hospital.

01:48:55.800 --> 01:49:00.130
I'd argue that the ibogaine risk is probably
a little less actually than the DBS risk if

01:49:00.130 --> 01:49:02.300
you just kind of look at everything.

01:49:02.300 --> 01:49:06.239
And when they're having these significant
hearings, there's a lot of opinions about

01:49:06.239 --> 01:49:08.650
this and a lot of
Tim Ferriss: Shocker!

01:49:08.650 --> 01:49:09.650
Dr. Nolan Williams: Right?

01:49:09.650 --> 01:49:13.599
And I'm one of the few people that does all
this stuff, so I can kind of, and you juxtapose

01:49:13.599 --> 01:49:14.619
this —
Tim Ferriss: That's part of the reason why

01:49:14.619 --> 01:49:18.500
we're having this conversation, because you
bridge a bunch of interesting, often separate

01:49:18.500 --> 01:49:19.500
worlds.

01:49:19.500 --> 01:49:20.500
So anyway.

01:49:20.500 --> 01:49:21.500
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:49:21.500 --> 01:49:22.500
Thanks, man.

01:49:22.500 --> 01:49:23.500
Yeah.

01:49:23.500 --> 01:49:24.500
Yeah.

01:49:24.500 --> 01:49:25.500
So I went down there and there were a million
questions about the cardiac risk and about

01:49:25.500 --> 01:49:28.969
whether or not this should be done and particularly
should state funds be funding this, and there

01:49:28.969 --> 01:49:31.080
are various opinions about that.

01:49:31.080 --> 01:49:34.170
My opinion and the reason why I was willing
to go down there and support the effort is

01:49:34.170 --> 01:49:40.969
there are a subpopulation of people in which
Suboxone, naltrexone, and methadone don't

01:49:40.969 --> 01:49:45.469
work, and we need to spend some money on trying
to help those people.

01:49:45.469 --> 01:49:48.659
Tim Ferriss: Also working is quote-unquote
"working," has different outcomes if you're

01:49:48.659 --> 01:49:50.270
talking about sction therapies as well, right?

01:49:50.270 --> 01:49:51.270
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's right.

01:49:51.270 --> 01:49:52.270
Tim Ferriss: Anyway.

01:49:52.270 --> 01:49:53.270
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, no, I think you're
totally right.

01:49:53.270 --> 01:49:54.270
I think there's, yeah, it's totally, yeah,
is living —

01:49:54.270 --> 01:49:55.270
Tim Ferriss: Replacement.

01:49:55.270 --> 01:49:56.270
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, replacement.

01:49:56.270 --> 01:49:58.820
On opiates, if you're having to do this your
whole life, I mean, it's tricky.

01:49:58.820 --> 01:50:03.290
You lose your prescription in a flight to
wherever and then now you're in a really bad

01:50:03.290 --> 01:50:04.390
place.

01:50:04.390 --> 01:50:09.760
And so this idea of instead of replacement
or substitution or whatever, interruption,

01:50:09.760 --> 01:50:13.780
which is what ibogaine is going to do, and
I think at some level what DBS is going to

01:50:13.780 --> 01:50:18.469
do too, it's going to interrupt that system,
that circuit that's driving the seeking behavior

01:50:18.469 --> 01:50:24.150
and be able to reorganize the brain, such
the person approaches the problem in a different

01:50:24.150 --> 01:50:28.580
way is a promise that I think everybody wants
to see.

01:50:28.580 --> 01:50:35.730
I think the interesting part about this whole
phenomenon down there is from the folks that

01:50:35.730 --> 01:50:41.590
are opposed to this is this view that one,
the current treatments are fine or whatever,

01:50:41.590 --> 01:50:50.821
and then two, that how could it be that this
extract from a root in an African country

01:50:50.821 --> 01:50:56.280
somehow be something that can do what modern
humans and pharma can't do?

01:50:56.280 --> 01:51:00.671
And it goes back to what I think is a pretty
hubristic part of the human psyche, which

01:51:00.671 --> 01:51:05.830
is that I need to start the fire, I need to
hammer the wheel, I need to, and I think at

01:51:05.830 --> 01:51:07.889
some level —
Tim Ferriss: The sort of "not invented here"

01:51:07.889 --> 01:51:08.889
kind of thing.

01:51:08.889 --> 01:51:09.889
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:51:09.889 --> 01:51:10.889
Yeah, right?

01:51:10.889 --> 01:51:16.960
And I think the idea that somehow this just
exists, it goes around the psyche in a way

01:51:16.960 --> 01:51:18.040
that doesn't really work.

01:51:18.040 --> 01:51:19.700
And we saw this with scurvy too, right?

01:51:19.700 --> 01:51:24.270
I've spent a lot of time studying scurvy because
I am very interested in this human phenomenon.

01:51:24.270 --> 01:51:27.570
There were people called anti-fruiters.

01:51:27.570 --> 01:51:29.449
I'm not kidding you, it's a real term.

01:51:29.449 --> 01:51:32.540
And they were in the royal societies in the
1750s.

01:51:32.540 --> 01:51:38.219
And so we knew from 1498 that folks that were
going around the Horn of Africa and going

01:51:38.219 --> 01:51:43.480
to Asia that way, they'd plant citrus trees
all the way there.

01:51:43.480 --> 01:51:48.989
So we were doing this and at some point, scurvy
got worse and all that.

01:51:48.989 --> 01:51:56.200
And people said, "There's literally no way
that these fruits could be the solution for

01:51:56.200 --> 01:51:57.200
this.

01:51:57.200 --> 01:51:58.440
Humans have to solve this.

01:51:58.440 --> 01:52:01.870
And actually the fruits are probably what's
making it worse."

01:52:01.870 --> 01:52:08.590
So James Lind ran the first clinical trial
in the history of humans on scurvy and citrus

01:52:08.590 --> 01:52:14.800
fruit where he gave all these various weird
concoctions like poisons, and they were trying

01:52:14.800 --> 01:52:15.800
to give people cyanide.

01:52:15.800 --> 01:52:17.699
They were trying to give people alcohol mixed
with acid.

01:52:17.699 --> 01:52:24.360
This is what they, and so he randomized people
on a ship with scurvy to these various things

01:52:24.360 --> 01:52:25.360
in citrus fruit.

01:52:25.360 --> 01:52:28.469
And what happened was the citrus fruit receiving
people at the end of the week were taking

01:52:28.469 --> 01:52:31.739
care of everybody else, but it took another
a hundred years.

01:52:31.739 --> 01:52:38.770
And I think this is meta phenomenon of people
need to feel like they made it in that culture

01:52:38.770 --> 01:52:42.369
within that context that this is the latest
and greatest thing.

01:52:42.369 --> 01:52:44.860
It needs to be kind of very proximal in time.

01:52:44.860 --> 01:52:49.320
It needs to be new, it's got to be the new
thing to be the solution.

01:52:49.320 --> 01:52:52.989
And I think that's part of what's going on
in Kentucky from my view of it.

01:52:52.989 --> 01:52:57.969
If you look at West Virginia and you just
look at the actual information of what's going

01:52:57.969 --> 01:53:04.570
on, you'd probably be more likely to cause,
and I don't think that people should, but

01:53:04.570 --> 01:53:09.320
question the brain surgery thing, if you really
get down to it over the ibogaine thing, just

01:53:09.320 --> 01:53:14.030
on the risk portfolio and having to have an
implanted device for the rest of your life.

01:53:14.030 --> 01:53:15.361
But nobody, there's nothing there.

01:53:15.361 --> 01:53:17.510
And it's because we're making this.

01:53:17.510 --> 01:53:23.659
Western society is making this really innovative
new treatment that requires a brain surgery

01:53:23.659 --> 01:53:24.820
and dah, dah, dah.

01:53:24.820 --> 01:53:27.639
And that's totally cool from their perspective.

01:53:27.639 --> 01:53:32.510
And I think that we've got to, as a culture
and as a scientific community, really change

01:53:32.510 --> 01:53:38.260
the way that we think about evaluating tests
and particularly therapies and look at the

01:53:38.260 --> 01:53:46.070
inherent scientific complexity and not the
temporal proximity and the fact that we made

01:53:46.070 --> 01:53:51.550
it or whatever, and those sorts of things
that I think drive people to have these misconceptions.

01:53:51.550 --> 01:53:56.520
So yeah, Kentucky's really interesting, and
it's really an argument about whether or not

01:53:56.520 --> 01:54:01.489
some of this money should be earmarked and
whether or not it should be studied.

01:54:01.489 --> 01:54:05.280
I think both of those things are a, "Yes,
we should be."

01:54:05.280 --> 01:54:09.610
It's the best candidate that we know about
and the risks are mitigatable and similar

01:54:09.610 --> 01:54:10.610
to DBS.

01:54:10.610 --> 01:54:16.179
Tim Ferriss: So many facets to this, like
insurance reimbursement, scaling, therapists

01:54:16.179 --> 01:54:20.639
availability, or I should say more medical
availability for the duration of stay that

01:54:20.639 --> 01:54:25.739
would be required with ibogaine versus, there's
so many facets to all of this in terms of,

01:54:25.739 --> 01:54:34.619
say, getting to patient 10,000, whether it's
this DBS implant surgery or ibogaine, there's

01:54:34.619 --> 01:54:39.969
so many levels of nuance, but at the basic
science level and the further research that

01:54:39.969 --> 01:54:42.929
if, I mean, who am I to say?

01:54:42.929 --> 01:54:48.000
But it seems like the cost and severity and
prevalence of the problem is such that the

01:54:48.000 --> 01:54:49.190
answer is of course, yes.

01:54:49.190 --> 01:54:50.190
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

01:54:50.190 --> 01:54:51.650
Tim Ferriss: Of course it's yes.

01:54:51.650 --> 01:54:57.380
I do want to ask you about synthesis specifically,
really diving in because I'm always fascinated

01:54:57.380 --> 01:55:03.750
by rate-limiting steps and also unintended
consequences of using different compounds

01:55:03.750 --> 01:55:04.800
that occur in nature.

01:55:04.800 --> 01:55:12.369
But before we get there, I want to very selfishly
throw a wild card question around IRBs and

01:55:12.369 --> 01:55:16.790
funding studies and getting ethical approvals
because I would love to see more studies on

01:55:16.790 --> 01:55:22.340
extended fasts in humans, but to my knowledge,
those basically got taken off of the table

01:55:22.340 --> 01:55:28.220
if I wanted to try to make the case and fund
some science related to extended fasts in

01:55:28.220 --> 01:55:29.220
humans.

01:55:29.220 --> 01:55:30.220
Any suggestions?

01:55:30.220 --> 01:55:35.849
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, I mean I think everything's
studiable if the risk benefit ratio is on

01:55:35.849 --> 01:55:36.849
the right side of things.

01:55:36.849 --> 01:55:40.320
I don't think there's any, I mean at least
at Stanford, I think that's the evaluation

01:55:40.320 --> 01:55:46.560
is extended fasts in normal healthy controls.

01:55:46.560 --> 01:55:51.420
That would be, but I think if you can make
an argument for some sort of medical psychiatric,

01:55:51.420 --> 01:55:58.170
whatever it is, condition that you think that
the benefit of doing that significantly outweighs

01:55:58.170 --> 01:55:59.170
the risk.

01:55:59.170 --> 01:56:00.170
Tim Ferriss: I think I can make that case.

01:56:00.170 --> 01:56:01.300
Dr. Nolan Williams: Then I think you can do
it.

01:56:01.300 --> 01:56:03.360
And so it really is just that, right?

01:56:03.360 --> 01:56:06.550
It's this ethical, and I agree with it, right?

01:56:06.550 --> 01:56:11.860
It's this ethical justification that we're
going to be able to do more good by trying

01:56:11.860 --> 01:56:13.739
to do something like this than to do harm.

01:56:13.739 --> 01:56:18.840
I mean, if people, a study that would never
go through any IRB is we're going to randomize

01:56:18.840 --> 01:56:27.139
people to no motorcycle helmet or motorcycle
helmet and have them do laps around the university.

01:56:27.139 --> 01:56:32.680
We pretty much know the answer to that question
and the benefit of knowing in a randomized

01:56:32.680 --> 01:56:37.900
control trial way, the benefit of the answer
to that question does not outweigh the risk

01:56:37.900 --> 01:56:43.090
at the individual patient or participant level
of participating in a trial like that.

01:56:43.090 --> 01:56:44.770
So that trial would never get done.

01:56:44.770 --> 01:56:48.020
And so I think that's the way that we have
to look at it.

01:56:48.020 --> 01:56:52.760
So if you've got a reason, your reason is
you think that there's going to be some effect

01:56:52.760 --> 01:56:55.760
on coronary artery disease or something like
that, or —

01:56:55.760 --> 01:56:59.139
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I think there are a couple
of different approaches I could take just

01:56:59.139 --> 01:57:01.829
in case there's anyone listening who wants
to do this.

01:57:01.829 --> 01:57:07.619
And there are extended people study fasting
in animals all the time, but it's for whatever

01:57:07.619 --> 01:57:11.580
set of reasons at some point it seems to have
been taken off the table for human subjects.

01:57:11.580 --> 01:57:15.400
I mean there is research done, I want to say
up until maybe the '40s and '50s, but then

01:57:15.400 --> 01:57:16.950
it kind of vanished.

01:57:16.950 --> 01:57:21.900
And I'm very interested in if I had to make
the case, I would probably make some type

01:57:21.900 --> 01:57:27.770
of case around what Chris Palmer at Harvard
and other people have called metabolic psychiatry.

01:57:27.770 --> 01:57:35.540
So to look at this almost like the accelerated
TMS equivalent of a ketogenic diet for certain

01:57:35.540 --> 01:57:40.760
psychiatric conditions, because you see some
incredible results, and Chris has been on

01:57:40.760 --> 01:57:46.889
the podcast with, say, ketogenic diet as applied
to conditions like schizophrenia, for instance.

01:57:46.889 --> 01:57:52.909
I mean remarkable transformations where people
get off of half a dozen or dozen medications.

01:57:52.909 --> 01:58:01.810
And you, I am interested in practical solutions
and especially things that are uncrowded from

01:58:01.810 --> 01:58:06.820
the perspective of scientific support, which
for a while has been psychedelics, but certainly

01:58:06.820 --> 01:58:07.820
accelerated TMS.

01:58:07.820 --> 01:58:12.710
I'm agnostic when it comes to the tools and
I think I'd probably make the metabolic psychiatry

01:58:12.710 --> 01:58:17.690
argument, but the fact of the matter is I
also feel like it's been so long since we

01:58:17.690 --> 01:58:24.400
applied modern tools and tracking of biomarkers
and so on, everything that we have at hand

01:58:24.400 --> 01:58:28.650
now to human fasting, that may not be the
argument that I would use, but certainly there

01:58:28.650 --> 01:58:29.650
is that.

01:58:29.650 --> 01:58:30.650
Okay.

01:58:30.650 --> 01:58:31.650
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, absolutely.

01:58:31.650 --> 01:58:34.719
Tim Ferriss: So synthesis, there's a great
piece that came out in National Geographic

01:58:34.719 --> 01:58:39.670
not too long ago by journalist named Rachel
Nuwer, I think I'm getting the last name right?

01:58:39.670 --> 01:58:40.670
N-U-W-E-R.

01:58:40.670 --> 01:58:46.940
She also has a great book on MDMA and MDMA-assisted
psychotherapy, the history and implications

01:58:46.940 --> 01:58:51.750
that recently came out and she traveled to,
I think it was Gabon, although I think there

01:58:51.750 --> 01:58:56.670
is also, I think you can also find iboga in
Cameroon if I'm not mistaken.

01:58:56.670 --> 01:59:02.810
And she went on the ground to look at all
the implications of global demand for ibogaine

01:59:02.810 --> 01:59:08.410
and iboga and it's very, very nuanced, but
it seems clear, and I wrote a piece a long

01:59:08.410 --> 01:59:14.201
time ago on my blog, which was a letter to
users of psychedelics, like a plea for a more

01:59:14.201 --> 01:59:19.690
ethical menu of options, something like that
to just point out some of the diminishing

01:59:19.690 --> 01:59:25.110
natural supplies for say, peyote, right?

01:59:25.110 --> 01:59:29.489
Almost certainly going to go extinct, use
something else, use San Pedro cactus, look

01:59:29.489 --> 01:59:30.670
at the growth cycles.

01:59:30.670 --> 01:59:36.980
Just don't touch it unless you're part of
a culture that has this as an integral part

01:59:36.980 --> 01:59:41.340
of tradition and healing as would be the case
for say, people who are in the Native American

01:59:41.340 --> 01:59:42.340
Church and so on.

01:59:42.340 --> 01:59:47.130
But iboga, ibogaine can be had in, I guess,
a number of different ways.

01:59:47.130 --> 01:59:51.469
Maybe you could speak to the known options
and then looking forward what some of the

01:59:51.469 --> 01:59:56.290
most interesting options are for whether it's
extraction or synthesis.

01:59:56.290 --> 01:59:59.920
Dr. Nolan Williams: And so there's kind of
the straight extract out of the iboga tree

01:59:59.920 --> 02:00:05.540
as you're pointing out, and that one's probably
a pretty big ethical issue because what's

02:00:05.540 --> 02:00:09.150
happening, and I think that that National
Geographic article really reflect this is

02:00:09.150 --> 02:00:13.660
that there's such a global demand that it
drives the prices up in the peoples that take

02:00:13.660 --> 02:00:21.190
ibogaine, iboga and in Gabon in surrounding
countries no longer can access it because

02:00:21.190 --> 02:00:23.710
of the high cost, which is the last thing
that you want to have happen.

02:00:23.710 --> 02:00:27.010
That's the thing that everybody I think should
be trying to avoid first.

02:00:27.010 --> 02:00:29.110
And so what else can you do?

02:00:29.110 --> 02:00:34.140
So another way to do this is that there's
another tree in Ghana and other places in

02:00:34.140 --> 02:00:37.670
Africa that has voacangine, the Voacanga africana
tree, and it has voacangine, which is a very

02:00:37.670 --> 02:00:42.520
similar but not identical alkaloid to ibogaine.

02:00:42.520 --> 02:00:44.170
It's actually not even a controlled substance.

02:00:44.170 --> 02:00:50.079
So voacangine is not on the controlled substances
list, and it's not as much of an issue.

02:00:50.079 --> 02:00:56.230
There's not really a current medicinal usage
of voacangine or utilization of that within

02:00:56.230 --> 02:00:57.920
those cultures that have these trees.

02:00:57.920 --> 02:01:02.210
Tim Ferriss: It's also more commercially cultivated,
if I'm not mistaken, for maybe fragrances

02:01:02.210 --> 02:01:03.210
or something.

02:01:03.210 --> 02:01:04.210
I can't recall the commercial use.

02:01:04.210 --> 02:01:05.530
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, and it's pretty
ubiquitous too.

02:01:05.530 --> 02:01:09.780
I mean, I've heard that it's not just in Africa,
it's in some of Central and South America,

02:01:09.780 --> 02:01:15.510
and so you can extract the voacangine and
then you can do this simple chemical step

02:01:15.510 --> 02:01:16.750
to get it to ibogaine.

02:01:16.750 --> 02:01:24.040
So to do a synthesis pathway of ibogaine from
de novo, it's like 26 plus steps or something

02:01:24.040 --> 02:01:25.040
like that.

02:01:25.040 --> 02:01:31.489
But this is the last step before it's ibogaine
from voacangine, and so it's really pretty

02:01:31.489 --> 02:01:35.230
straightforward to do that, but you're still
talking about a botanical, right?

02:01:35.230 --> 02:01:39.699
You're still talking about essentially growing
a plant to derive a chemical out of the plant.

02:01:39.699 --> 02:01:45.180
So the other way to think about it is you
do a full chemical synthesis and people have

02:01:45.180 --> 02:01:46.240
looked at that and tried to do it.

02:01:46.240 --> 02:01:50.160
It's very hard to though, because ibogaine
has two chiral centers, so it has the potential

02:01:50.160 --> 02:01:54.160
for four stereoisomers, and that chemistry
is complicated.

02:01:54.160 --> 02:01:57.260
And so full synthesis is a tricky process.

02:01:57.260 --> 02:02:05.300
My suspicion is that whether it be biosynthesis
or straight full synthesis, but there's got

02:02:05.300 --> 02:02:10.020
to be a way to make this that avoids trees
eventually.

02:02:10.020 --> 02:02:11.960
I think it's better for the environment.

02:02:11.960 --> 02:02:16.040
It's going to be more scalable, is going to
be something that standard pharma is going

02:02:16.040 --> 02:02:19.840
to want to see happen, to really be able to
use it.

02:02:19.840 --> 02:02:23.900
But there's still some time to get from A
to B as far as that goes.

02:02:23.900 --> 02:02:24.960
So I think it's not a done deal.

02:02:24.960 --> 02:02:26.250
It's not completely figured out yet.

02:02:26.250 --> 02:02:30.449
Tim Ferriss: So bridging from that, I mean,
this is actually a completely separate question,

02:02:30.449 --> 02:02:35.560
but also raises some sustainability, ethical
questions, 5-MeO-DMT.

02:02:35.560 --> 02:02:37.599
So five methoxy DMT.

02:02:37.599 --> 02:02:42.040
So first of all, I would really implore people,
I'll link to this in the show notes as well,

02:02:42.040 --> 02:02:43.630
read the blog post that I put together.

02:02:43.630 --> 02:02:49.520
So the 5-MeO-DMT, I mean it's present in quite
a few different plants, different nuts.

02:02:49.520 --> 02:02:55.410
Most people who have heard it in the zeitgeist
to know it within the context of Bufo alvarius,

02:02:55.410 --> 02:03:00.010
there are other scientific names for this
toad, the Sonoran Desert Toad.

02:03:00.010 --> 02:03:06.989
And that has turned into a huge mess in terms
of cartel harvesting and over harvesting from

02:03:06.989 --> 02:03:08.130
these poor toads.

02:03:08.130 --> 02:03:09.130
It can be synthesized.

02:03:09.130 --> 02:03:10.679
There are ways to synthesize that.

02:03:10.679 --> 02:03:12.380
Hamilton Morris has beat the drum about this.

02:03:12.380 --> 02:03:17.480
To his credit, there is no, people are not
going to like this, but there is no documented

02:03:17.480 --> 02:03:22.989
indigenous use of the Sonoran Desert Toad,
Ken Nelson, look it up, in the '80s, produced

02:03:22.989 --> 02:03:25.420
a pamphlet after testing God knows how many
things.

02:03:25.420 --> 02:03:27.329
Brilliant amateur biochemist.

02:03:27.329 --> 02:03:31.329
But nonetheless, it is very, very, it is interesting
and appealing on a whole number of levels.

02:03:31.329 --> 02:03:35.150
A lot of people are trying to commercialize
it because at least in Earth time, it is a

02:03:35.150 --> 02:03:36.480
short experience.

02:03:36.480 --> 02:03:41.470
So that's going to 10 to 20 minutes, 10 to
15 minutes.

02:03:41.470 --> 02:03:45.480
So from a business model perspective, I understand
the appeal much like I understand the appeal

02:03:45.480 --> 02:03:50.219
of the newly branded psychoplastogens, right?

02:03:50.219 --> 02:03:56.210
Psychedelics with the content/mind-altering
aspects as removed as possible.

02:03:56.210 --> 02:04:00.219
We might come back to that, but my question
related to this is not so much on the production

02:04:00.219 --> 02:04:02.100
side because people can read about that separately.

02:04:02.100 --> 02:04:04.360
And I think it's important for people to read
about.

02:04:04.360 --> 02:04:11.770
It's more of practical use following IGA administration,
I believe I've heard people describe its use

02:04:11.770 --> 02:04:15.100
on what some people call the gray day following
administration.

02:04:15.100 --> 02:04:21.559
Could you speak to this, because I'm trying
to discern for myself how important or critical

02:04:21.559 --> 02:04:26.810
it is from a therapeutic outcomes perspective
versus being a business differentiator?

02:04:26.810 --> 02:04:27.810
Does that make sense?

02:04:27.810 --> 02:04:34.869
This is something we put a nice set of icing
on top of the cake and that includes this

02:04:34.869 --> 02:04:39.340
what can often be a sublime experience, not
always for people, it can be destabilizing

02:04:39.340 --> 02:04:42.270
for some folks, but in the form of 5-MeO-DMT.

02:04:42.270 --> 02:04:43.520
So what's your take on this?

02:04:43.520 --> 02:04:48.501
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, I mean, so we specifically
made sure that folks weren't getting a second

02:04:48.501 --> 02:04:54.469
kind of confounding drug on top of the ibogaine
to figure out what the ibogaine effects were

02:04:54.469 --> 02:04:55.469
in isolation.

02:04:55.469 --> 02:05:02.849
And as I described earlier, we had extremely
remarkable effects in the absence of doing

02:05:02.849 --> 02:05:03.849
the 5-MeO.

02:05:03.849 --> 02:05:06.370
I think what people say about this, the 5-MeO,
is that it just takes the edge, at minimum

02:05:06.370 --> 02:05:12.760
it just seems to take the edge off the gray
day, which is a day that happens for not everybody

02:05:12.760 --> 02:05:17.710
that takes ibogaine, but a fair percentage
of people such that there's a name for it,

02:05:17.710 --> 02:05:23.960
where people for some reason end up having
kind of a bad day in and around like day two,

02:05:23.960 --> 02:05:27.809
three, where they really have a hard time
and then it goes away the next day.

02:05:27.809 --> 02:05:29.889
Tim Ferriss: Hard time meaning depressive
symptoms —

02:05:29.889 --> 02:05:30.889
Dr. Nolan Williams: For some people.

02:05:30.889 --> 02:05:31.889
Tim Ferriss: What is hard?

02:05:31.889 --> 02:05:32.889
Okay, what makes it hard?

02:05:32.889 --> 02:05:33.889
Dr. Nolan Williams: Anxiety.

02:05:33.889 --> 02:05:34.889
Tim Ferriss: Anxiety.

02:05:34.889 --> 02:05:37.880
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, like anhedonia,
low motivation, sadness.

02:05:37.880 --> 02:05:44.580
I think probably what's going on is there
was a serious kind of flooding of your CNS

02:05:44.580 --> 02:05:49.580
with a whole host of effects from this drug,
and then the brain's then kind of reacting

02:05:49.580 --> 02:05:56.190
to that, and then it seems to rebound out
on its own without the 5-MeO.

02:05:56.190 --> 02:06:02.000
But it sounds like, from what I've heard,
the 5-MeO bridges people out of that, so they

02:06:02.000 --> 02:06:05.290
don't have to really have to experience that
feeling.

02:06:05.290 --> 02:06:12.000
Now the question would be, does it do something
else beyond that that's useful?

02:06:12.000 --> 02:06:13.000
And we have no idea.

02:06:13.000 --> 02:06:19.480
I suppose what we could have done, or maybe
what we could do in a subsequent study is

02:06:19.480 --> 02:06:23.050
to just randomize people to getting no 5-MeO
after or 5-MeO.

02:06:23.050 --> 02:06:25.239
And we could actually answer that question.

02:06:25.239 --> 02:06:27.309
Does it have an effect on the long-term?

02:06:27.309 --> 02:06:32.620
It's hard because there's a floor effect of
just the profound improvements that we saw

02:06:32.620 --> 02:06:34.060
just from ibogaine.

02:06:34.060 --> 02:06:39.849
So you'd have to, my guess is that the statistics
would tell you that you have to do a pretty

02:06:39.849 --> 02:06:45.590
large sample to be able to see something if
it's there with 5-MeO just because everybody's

02:06:45.590 --> 02:06:46.590
flooring out just with ibogaine.

02:06:46.590 --> 02:06:50.480
Tim Ferriss: When you say flooring out, does
that mean that the amplitude of the effect

02:06:50.480 --> 02:06:56.580
is so great that, just in terms of seeing
a large percentage improvement over the ibogaine,

02:06:56.580 --> 02:06:58.260
you'd need to see something great?

02:06:58.260 --> 02:06:59.260
Dr. Nolan Williams: Exactly.

02:06:59.260 --> 02:07:00.360
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by flooring
out?

02:07:00.360 --> 02:07:01.360
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, yeah.

02:07:01.360 --> 02:07:03.929
So essentially we're seeing people drop down
within that normal range.

02:07:03.929 --> 02:07:04.929
Tim Ferriss: On the assessments.

02:07:04.929 --> 02:07:05.929
Dr. Nolan Williams: On the assessments.

02:07:05.929 --> 02:07:09.580
So you'd need to see — and people are going
into the range of a score of five, six, something

02:07:09.580 --> 02:07:11.969
like that on various scales.

02:07:11.969 --> 02:07:16.739
The PTSD scales, the depression scales between
the normal range, and so you'd have to zero

02:07:16.739 --> 02:07:17.880
people out with a 5-MeO, essentially.

02:07:17.880 --> 02:07:26.590
Or the other thing that it may do, which nobody
knows probably, is whether or not it makes

02:07:26.590 --> 02:07:29.469
some of these folks who were, and you'll see
in the paper, there are a couple of people

02:07:29.469 --> 02:07:31.280
that relapsed at the month mark.

02:07:31.280 --> 02:07:33.409
Maybe it helps the durability on some people.

02:07:33.409 --> 02:07:34.409
It could do those things.

02:07:34.409 --> 02:07:38.650
So I'm not saying that it doesn't have a benefit.

02:07:38.650 --> 02:07:43.550
It would be a hard study to deal with, and
I think from a purist, I want to see this

02:07:43.550 --> 02:07:49.909
go through the FDA and kind do the FDA things
and see if we can get the first drug through

02:07:49.909 --> 02:07:50.909
the process.

02:07:50.909 --> 02:07:57.190
I don't want to say it's a distraction from
a US scientific regulatory strategy standpoint,

02:07:57.190 --> 02:07:59.640
but it's definitely something that would add
complexity.

02:07:59.640 --> 02:08:05.440
So I think at the level of clinics, they're
doing this in Mexico and they have free-range

02:08:05.440 --> 02:08:06.929
to use these substances.

02:08:06.929 --> 02:08:09.989
I don't think it's, on the face of it, like
a terrible thing to do.

02:08:09.989 --> 02:08:11.710
I think it makes sense why they're doing it.

02:08:11.710 --> 02:08:13.340
Tim Ferriss: Do you really think that?

02:08:13.340 --> 02:08:17.690
I guess just to push on that a little bit,
I'm like, okay, so people have a hard day,

02:08:17.690 --> 02:08:21.710
but it's known that this is a phenomenon so
they could prep people for the possibility

02:08:21.710 --> 02:08:26.489
that they would experience this hard day if
they're not somehow edging into dangerous

02:08:26.489 --> 02:08:28.270
territory where they're likely to self-harm.

02:08:28.270 --> 02:08:31.360
5-MeO-DMT, I have some experience with it.

02:08:31.360 --> 02:08:36.510
I understand the appeal, it's like satcitananda,
et cetera, but it's not risk-free.

02:08:36.510 --> 02:08:40.950
Dr. Nolan Williams: Well, you're strapping
yourself to a rocket.

02:08:40.950 --> 02:08:41.950
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:08:41.950 --> 02:08:42.950
That is a big, big gun, right?

02:08:42.950 --> 02:08:43.950
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

02:08:43.950 --> 02:08:48.099
Tim Ferriss: Which is not to malign anyone
who is using this in a clinical setting, but

02:08:48.099 --> 02:08:49.360
it's not risk free.

02:08:49.360 --> 02:08:52.280
I have friends who are very, very experienced
psychonauts.

02:08:52.280 --> 02:08:57.179
We're talking like a hundred plus reps with
things like ayahuasca who have been pretty

02:08:57.179 --> 02:09:04.929
much even keel with their various experiments
and been knocked pretty sideways for non-trivial

02:09:04.929 --> 02:09:07.530
periods of time by 5-MeO-DMT.

02:09:07.530 --> 02:09:10.010
Maybe that's just the sample set that I'm
dealing with.

02:09:10.010 --> 02:09:11.960
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, it's an interesting
— and I totally agree with you.

02:09:11.960 --> 02:09:18.080
Medicine is a discipline and a profession
of risk mitigation and risk benefit ratios

02:09:18.080 --> 02:09:19.760
and everything.

02:09:19.760 --> 02:09:25.520
Anybody that would proclaim themselves to
be a physician scientist that doesn't believe

02:09:25.520 --> 02:09:28.540
that isn't seeing it as it is.

02:09:28.540 --> 02:09:33.429
Everything that I do is some sort of risk
mitigation exercise where I'm looking at this

02:09:33.429 --> 02:09:38.650
thing has these risks, but this person has
these inherent risks, and how do you square

02:09:38.650 --> 02:09:39.650
all that?

02:09:39.650 --> 02:09:44.610
I think to your point, it would totally make
sense if the person was in such a bad place

02:09:44.610 --> 02:09:47.090
in the grade A that you were worried about
them.

02:09:47.090 --> 02:09:50.810
I think there's a justification there.

02:09:50.810 --> 02:09:57.840
It seems that they do have some general experience
that this was helpful to getting folks out.

02:09:57.840 --> 02:10:03.550
The reality is that we're really not going
to know much of anything, and a lot of this

02:10:03.550 --> 02:10:11.070
is going to be in the realm of anecdote until
we do the trials in the US and really thoroughly

02:10:11.070 --> 02:10:15.010
document everything that happens with this
draw, with ibogaine, with fibromyalgia, which

02:10:15.010 --> 02:10:18.940
as you know, people are trying to put through
trials and commercialize that.

02:10:18.940 --> 02:10:24.820
So I think there's a moment where we'll be
able to rectify all of this and figure it

02:10:24.820 --> 02:10:25.820
out.

02:10:25.820 --> 02:10:30.949
I agree with you too that I think that where
MDMA may be a substance that certainly not

02:10:30.949 --> 02:10:37.771
everybody could use, but a decently broad
population-based drug that a fair amount of

02:10:37.771 --> 02:10:39.710
the population that had PTSD could go after.

02:10:39.710 --> 02:10:47.030
I think that these substances are more constrained
to a smaller population where the risk benefit

02:10:47.030 --> 02:10:48.260
is right for them.

02:10:48.260 --> 02:10:49.949
So absolutely, it's a tricky moment.

02:10:49.949 --> 02:10:55.349
I think we know just enough to be dangerous
in some places, and we got to get through

02:10:55.349 --> 02:11:00.239
this just enough to be dangerous moment to
the we know how to not be dangerous moment

02:11:00.239 --> 02:11:04.650
as a culture and do that with the scientific
process.

02:11:04.650 --> 02:11:10.729
Tim Ferriss: Much earlier you were laying
out psychiatry 1.0, 2.0, 3.0.

02:11:10.729 --> 02:11:13.170
I'd love for you to feel free to speculate,
right?

02:11:13.170 --> 02:11:18.270
It's going to be speculation, but putting
aside the — I know it's hard to do, but

02:11:18.270 --> 02:11:22.719
shepherding stuff in its simplest form, cleanest
form through the FDS, etc.

02:11:22.719 --> 02:11:28.630
But what might psychiatry 4.0 look like in
the sense that — for instance, something

02:11:28.630 --> 02:11:34.079
that's on my mind, and I'll keep it short,
is as I understand it at a very primitive

02:11:34.079 --> 02:11:40.440
level, the way that some of the accelerated
TMS protocols work from a hardware perspective

02:11:40.440 --> 02:11:47.100
is you're kind hitting nodes at the exterior
of the hub and triangulating in a way to hit

02:11:47.100 --> 02:11:48.790
what you're trying to hit.

02:11:48.790 --> 02:11:52.909
But perhaps you could use, for instance, in
my conversation with Nora Volkow of NIDA,

02:11:52.909 --> 02:11:55.190
she's talking about focused ultrasound.

02:11:55.190 --> 02:11:58.559
So maybe you use that to hit the deeper structures
directly.

02:11:58.559 --> 02:12:00.719
You hit the nucleus accumbens or whatever
it might be.

02:12:00.719 --> 02:12:06.159
Then related to that, if we're talking about
Freud and so on, focusing on content, and

02:12:06.159 --> 02:12:11.280
then you've got this sort of neurotransmitter
focus, oh, it's a serotonin issue, and then

02:12:11.280 --> 02:12:18.130
now you have the electroceutical kind of structural
nuance and experimentation.

02:12:18.130 --> 02:12:20.920
But it seems like these things aren't necessarily
wrong.

02:12:20.920 --> 02:12:24.030
They're just, at least if we're looking at
the content, the molecules incomplete.

02:12:24.030 --> 02:12:25.030
Dr. Nolan Williams: It's incomplete.

02:12:25.030 --> 02:12:26.030
That's right.

02:12:26.030 --> 02:12:29.489
Tim Ferriss: But if you talk to a lot of these
guys who go through, and gals, but a lot of

02:12:29.489 --> 02:12:33.070
these operators of men who go through the
ibogaine experience, they will — and maybe

02:12:33.070 --> 02:12:37.070
there's a visibility bias because they can't
see what the hell's going on in their head

02:12:37.070 --> 02:12:40.829
from a chemical perspective, but they can
remember the content, but a lot of people

02:12:40.829 --> 02:12:45.190
attribute the therapeutic effects to much
of the content.

02:12:45.190 --> 02:12:46.901
There's reconciliation and so on.

02:12:46.901 --> 02:12:49.520
So what might psychiatry 4.0 look like?

02:12:49.520 --> 02:12:50.520
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, that's a great question.

02:12:50.520 --> 02:12:53.940
We have a paper coming out soon where we're
actually trying to use neurostimulation to

02:12:53.940 --> 02:12:59.710
change trait hypnotizability to make people
more suggestible transiently, and it's probably

02:12:59.710 --> 02:13:06.730
an ability to zero in on specific content
and manipulate that content through circuit

02:13:06.730 --> 02:13:08.210
based intervention.

02:13:08.210 --> 02:13:14.070
That's a pure speculation, but I'll give you
an anecdotal kind of case report example.

02:13:14.070 --> 02:13:19.170
So there was a patient who got similar sort
of deep brain stimulation approach that I

02:13:19.170 --> 02:13:23.850
was describing earlier that they're looking
at in West Virginia for addiction.

02:13:23.850 --> 02:13:29.110
That individual received deep brain stimulation,
I think in Europe, I think for OCD.

02:13:29.110 --> 02:13:31.960
He had a normal musical taste.

02:13:31.960 --> 02:13:38.659
He listened to whatever the range of standard
artists, and then he got his deep brain stimulation

02:13:38.659 --> 02:13:41.139
and he became obsessed with Johnny Cash.

02:13:41.139 --> 02:13:44.350
Totally sold all of his —
Tim Ferriss: I didn't see that coming.

02:13:44.350 --> 02:13:45.350
All right.

02:13:45.350 --> 02:13:49.760
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, totally sold all
of his other albums and listened to Johnny

02:13:49.760 --> 02:13:50.760
Cash.

02:13:50.760 --> 02:13:55.210
The batteries for the brain stimulators will
wear out after time and you need a new one.

02:13:55.210 --> 02:13:59.360
It's decently common that if the person's
doing pretty well, then they will forget to

02:13:59.360 --> 02:14:02.370
come in and then all of a sudden their battery's
dead and then they'll have a re-emergence

02:14:02.370 --> 02:14:03.370
of symptoms.

02:14:03.370 --> 02:14:04.370
So this gentleman did.

02:14:04.370 --> 02:14:10.329
So I think it was his OCD and his OCD got
worse again, and he fell out of favor with

02:14:10.329 --> 02:14:14.250
Johnny Cash and threw away all of his Johnny
Cash albums and started listening to whatever

02:14:14.250 --> 02:14:17.679
he was listening to before, went in and got
a battery changed.

02:14:17.679 --> 02:14:19.380
The battery was put back in.

02:14:19.380 --> 02:14:20.960
Tim Ferriss: He's like, "For fuck's sake.

02:14:20.960 --> 02:14:23.929
I've got to go back to the shop and buy my
Johnny Cash!"

02:14:23.929 --> 02:14:27.090
Dr. Nolan Williams: That's what he did.

02:14:27.090 --> 02:14:30.239
So just like we don't really understand how
ibogaine works, we really don't understand

02:14:30.239 --> 02:14:31.239
what happened there.

02:14:31.239 --> 02:14:36.360
It was a very illustrative case, and I love
talking about that case because it gets into

02:14:36.360 --> 02:14:42.079
this area that I think people are really worried
about right now, about specific content manipulation.

02:14:42.079 --> 02:14:43.300
I don't think we can do that.

02:14:43.300 --> 02:14:44.639
We don't have the tools to do that.

02:14:44.639 --> 02:14:49.590
We have these broad tools that basically change
the lens of the world that you look through.

02:14:49.590 --> 02:14:54.510
I can change your brain in such a way that,
for some people, they're going to see rose

02:14:54.510 --> 02:14:56.920
color glasses where they saw blue before or
whatever.

02:14:56.920 --> 02:15:00.219
Tim Ferriss: It's like, I can give you a different
set of glasses to watch the movie, but I can't

02:15:00.219 --> 02:15:01.270
change the movie directly.

02:15:01.270 --> 02:15:03.050
Dr. Nolan Williams: You can't change the movie
directly.

02:15:03.050 --> 02:15:10.880
My suspicion is that we're going to edge into
a world where maybe we can change the movie.

02:15:10.880 --> 02:15:16.000
That's where it starts to get both very interesting
and very ethically complicated.

02:15:16.000 --> 02:15:20.440
That story about Johnny Cash, that doctor
had no intention of doing that because nobody

02:15:20.440 --> 02:15:22.300
knows how to do that.

02:15:22.300 --> 02:15:24.840
Maybe they played Johnny Cash in the OR, maybe
not.

02:15:24.840 --> 02:15:29.680
They didn't report that in the case report,
but there's no clear sense of why that happened

02:15:29.680 --> 02:15:30.680
either.

02:15:30.680 --> 02:15:33.710
Is this the first song that was playing in
the hospital and he woke up and his brain,

02:15:33.710 --> 02:15:37.260
the reward system just kind of attached to
it or whatever?

02:15:37.260 --> 02:15:41.449
But it's one of these things where we're going
to get to a place of sophistication.

02:15:41.449 --> 02:15:43.570
I think we're going to be able to do that.

02:15:43.570 --> 02:15:49.280
What happens in medicine is, I see it is we
are always redefining what's illness.

02:15:49.280 --> 02:15:55.630
So it used to be that high blood pressure
was 150 over 100 or something, and then it

02:15:55.630 --> 02:16:00.650
was 140 over 90 and then it's 130 over 80,
and now it's 125 over 75 or whatever it is.

02:16:00.650 --> 02:16:01.969
It's not that the illness has changed.

02:16:01.969 --> 02:16:04.159
It's our definition of illness changed.

02:16:04.159 --> 02:16:11.260
So my suspicion is that as we have enough
tools to be able to knock out these major

02:16:11.260 --> 02:16:16.000
mental illnesses, which were effectively end
states, at the end of the day.

02:16:16.000 --> 02:16:21.260
We let the system go all the way to this kind
of completely semi-functional end state where

02:16:21.260 --> 02:16:25.900
people have semi volition and they're kind
of stuck in these mindsets and these behaviors.

02:16:25.900 --> 02:16:31.949
If we can figure out what that is and we have
ways of intervening sooner in that process,

02:16:31.949 --> 02:16:39.530
and we have emerging tools that can help you
with this sort of content targeting manipulation

02:16:39.530 --> 02:16:44.830
sort of thing, then I think you're going to
be talking about much more specific sort of

02:16:44.830 --> 02:16:45.830
interventions.

02:16:45.830 --> 02:16:48.430
That's very sci-fi though.

02:16:48.430 --> 02:16:51.620
It's not something that I think there's even
a hint of.

02:16:51.620 --> 02:16:54.420
I wouldn't even know how to tell you how to
do that now.

02:16:54.420 --> 02:16:58.090
Our one shot on goal is just to move around
this brain trait.

02:16:58.090 --> 02:17:04.450
We do have a study with Raag Airan where we're
actually packaging ketamine into nano particles,

02:17:04.450 --> 02:17:10.679
infusing them into through an IV into the
bloodstream, and then using ultrasound to

02:17:10.679 --> 02:17:14.970
open the nano particle ketamine and drop ketamine
just in the cingulate, in the area that we

02:17:14.970 --> 02:17:18.280
were talking about earlier in this case, in
pain patients, because it's easy to measure

02:17:18.280 --> 02:17:20.630
pain scales and reactivity.

02:17:20.630 --> 02:17:25.530
But what I think will be really interesting
with that is if you could take that same technology

02:17:25.530 --> 02:17:33.410
and start to drop various psychedelics into
specific brain regions and you can do a behavior

02:17:33.410 --> 02:17:38.680
mapping exercise, what's necessary and sufficient
to produce the clinical improvement, what's

02:17:38.680 --> 02:17:41.310
necessary and sufficient to produce the trip.

02:17:41.310 --> 02:17:45.510
I think that's going to be way more important
than modifying the molecules, because it's

02:17:45.510 --> 02:17:46.510
like a confound.

02:17:46.510 --> 02:17:50.639
We're not really answering the question of
does tripless ibogaine work?

02:17:50.639 --> 02:17:51.900
Because it's not ibogaine.

02:17:51.900 --> 02:17:53.460
It's just some other thing.

02:17:53.460 --> 02:17:54.460
Tim Ferriss: Right.

02:17:54.460 --> 02:17:57.360
Dr. Nolan Williams: But what we really want
to know is does tripless ibogaine work because

02:17:57.360 --> 02:18:03.050
we just put it in the amygdala or just into
the cingulate or something like that?

02:18:03.050 --> 02:18:08.200
That in isolation isn't sufficient to produce
the trip, but it's sufficient to produce a

02:18:08.200 --> 02:18:09.200
therapeutic effect.

02:18:09.200 --> 02:18:12.250
I think if we can pull that off, we're going
to start next year on that, that's going to

02:18:12.250 --> 02:18:17.099
be super cool because it's going to give us
the ability to have more of these questions.

02:18:17.099 --> 02:18:21.340
You could even think about it if you had a
long-acting anesthetic where you had somebody

02:18:21.340 --> 02:18:27.080
with pretty pronounced psychosis, schizophrenia
symptoms coming in, and you're able to shut

02:18:27.080 --> 02:18:31.269
down their amygdala for a couple of days with
an anesthetic just in the amygdala and nowhere

02:18:31.269 --> 02:18:32.269
else.

02:18:32.269 --> 02:18:37.559
They're totally awake, they're still with
you, but their fear response or into the cingulate,

02:18:37.559 --> 02:18:41.240
their kind of salience of the environment
response, goes down because you're able to

02:18:41.240 --> 02:18:45.490
kind of temporarily shut it off, but you're
not having to give a whole body whole brain

02:18:45.490 --> 02:18:49.220
anesthetic where you're putting somebody into
a medical coma or something.

02:18:49.220 --> 02:18:51.469
You're just shutting down this one area.

02:18:51.469 --> 02:18:53.679
So it's an interesting —
Tim Ferriss: Which you could potentially do

02:18:53.679 --> 02:18:55.769
through neuromodulation and not pharmaceutical,
right?

02:18:55.769 --> 02:19:00.439
Dr. Nolan Williams: You can, but even with
— so people have tried to do that with deep

02:19:00.439 --> 02:19:02.819
brain stimulation.

02:19:02.819 --> 02:19:06.349
You can actually do a jamming signal in certain
areas and shut it down too.

02:19:06.349 --> 02:19:09.939
But it ends up being — and that may be the
long-term solution, you're using drugs like

02:19:09.939 --> 02:19:11.689
focal drugs to test it.

02:19:11.689 --> 02:19:12.689
It's a commitment.

02:19:12.689 --> 02:19:17.490
It's not something you do in an emergency,
but you could in theory do this in the ER.

02:19:17.490 --> 02:19:22.660
You could take somebody that was acutely psychotic,
you could put an anesthetic that could shut

02:19:22.660 --> 02:19:27.530
down that system for transiently for a couple
of days, and you could get them more on board

02:19:27.530 --> 02:19:32.410
with thinking about what the long-term solution
is when the fear system isn't in place as

02:19:32.410 --> 02:19:33.490
much.

02:19:33.490 --> 02:19:35.769
All total speculation.

02:19:35.769 --> 02:19:39.160
It's not something I have any direct data
to support, but it's definitely interesting.

02:19:39.160 --> 02:19:43.780
Tim Ferriss: Let's push it into a little bit
more sci-fi because it's fun.

02:19:43.780 --> 02:19:46.990
Also a lot of things that start in sci-fi
end up in sci.

02:19:46.990 --> 02:19:48.500
Dr. Nolan Williams: Right, true.

02:19:48.500 --> 02:19:50.000
Tim Ferriss: So to speak.

02:19:50.000 --> 02:19:53.840
You look at Snow Crash by Neal Stephenson
and he's very good.

02:19:53.840 --> 02:19:58.350
There are many, many examples, but do you
think you could change handedness, hand dominance?

02:19:58.350 --> 02:20:00.140
Dr. Nolan Williams: That one would be hard.

02:20:00.140 --> 02:20:05.470
I think it's possible to do that at childhood.

02:20:05.470 --> 02:20:06.960
We do do this in stroke.

02:20:06.960 --> 02:20:11.850
We do this constraint therapy sort of stuff
where you actually constrain — if you have

02:20:11.850 --> 02:20:16.800
a big stroke where you have on one side of
the brain, you have a pretty devastating stroke

02:20:16.800 --> 02:20:20.840
where people can't move their arm and all
that good stuff, or they have minimal movement

02:20:20.840 --> 02:20:24.140
of their arm and then they have an intact
side, that's totally fine.

02:20:24.140 --> 02:20:30.760
You actually constrain the intact side and
force the person to use the affected side

02:20:30.760 --> 02:20:33.181
to drink water or to write or whatever.

02:20:33.181 --> 02:20:37.109
What you're trying to do is to reorganize
the neural system in such a way to where there's

02:20:37.109 --> 02:20:40.430
more cross-functional attribution of that
side.

02:20:40.430 --> 02:20:42.780
So we have some evidence there's ways to do
that.

02:20:42.780 --> 02:20:46.480
There's kids who need corpus callosotomies
in various different, pretty significant,

02:20:46.480 --> 02:20:50.760
like, say, epilepsy surgeries when they're
really, really young, like one year old, two

02:20:50.760 --> 02:20:52.510
years old, three years old, whatever.

02:20:52.510 --> 02:20:58.030
When you look at those cases, it can lose
a pretty substantial part of their brain from

02:20:58.030 --> 02:21:01.979
whatever surgery they needed to deal with
their problem or if they had a trauma or whatever.

02:21:01.979 --> 02:21:06.930
They can reorganize the system to be able
to reallocate resources to be able to do bilateral

02:21:06.930 --> 02:21:07.930
sort of functions.

02:21:07.930 --> 02:21:10.570
So it's mostly that the brain gets fixed.

02:21:10.570 --> 02:21:15.140
The interesting thing about this idea of critical
periods and whether or not, and maybe what

02:21:15.140 --> 02:21:20.221
ibogaine is doing and whether or not you can
make a more plastic brain is this idea that

02:21:20.221 --> 02:21:25.180
if you could bring the brain to a more juvenile
state, then you could probably neuro-rehab

02:21:25.180 --> 02:21:26.609
it better.

02:21:26.609 --> 02:21:29.290
That's going to be, I think one of the questions
that'll come out of the data that we're going

02:21:29.290 --> 02:21:32.190
to present is how far can this go?

02:21:32.190 --> 02:21:38.340
We saw people go from mild to moderate TBI
disability to not on average, which is awesome

02:21:38.340 --> 02:21:39.590
and never heard of.

02:21:39.590 --> 02:21:43.840
But if you keep pushing on that, how far could
you go with something like that?

02:21:43.840 --> 02:21:48.479
That's going to be a question that I don't
have an answer for, but at least there's a

02:21:48.479 --> 02:21:50.680
signal there to look.

02:21:50.680 --> 02:21:55.569
That's kind of part of what I like about the
general work that I try to do is I like to

02:21:55.569 --> 02:22:01.330
be relatively disrupting and I like to be
in spaces where nobody else is working.

02:22:01.330 --> 02:22:04.370
I start to not like it when everybody's doing
it.

02:22:04.370 --> 02:22:07.990
So I'm like always now, "Where am I going?"

02:22:07.990 --> 02:22:11.890
I'm always looking for the thing where nobody's
really in that space studying it.

02:22:11.890 --> 02:22:16.550
I always like it when people think — if
people think it's really weird, it's a positive

02:22:16.550 --> 02:22:19.040
signal that I need to do it kind of thing.

02:22:19.040 --> 02:22:27.310
So I think certainly this area of using psychedelic
drugs to try to treat neuro deficits is not

02:22:27.310 --> 02:22:30.420
something that a whole lot of people are really
looking into right now.

02:22:30.420 --> 02:22:33.060
So it's pretty curious and hopefully we can
ask some of those questions.

02:22:33.060 --> 02:22:40.310
Tim Ferriss: One aspect of the accelerated
TMS in terms of — case reports may be too

02:22:40.310 --> 02:22:46.700
strong word — anecdotal reports that I found
interesting, is it seems like some folks report

02:22:46.700 --> 02:22:51.780
increased visual acuity or color contrast.

02:22:51.780 --> 02:22:53.399
I found that very interesting for a few reasons.

02:22:53.399 --> 02:22:57.750
Number one is that it's very commonly reported,
say if you're on lower doses or higher doses,

02:22:57.750 --> 02:23:00.689
but let's just say low to moderate doses of
certain psychedelics.

02:23:00.689 --> 02:23:05.900
It's sort of like the dial on your HD visual
perception is set forward a few clicks.

02:23:05.900 --> 02:23:07.540
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yep.

02:23:07.540 --> 02:23:10.250
Tim Ferriss: The flowers sparkle just a little
bit more.

02:23:10.250 --> 02:23:12.960
You notice details you would otherwise not
notice.

02:23:12.960 --> 02:23:15.560
The reason I'm bringing this up, that raises
a lot of questions, but I'm bringing that

02:23:15.560 --> 02:23:21.880
up specifically because there are athletes
who have talked about the performance enhancing

02:23:21.880 --> 02:23:26.569
benefits of some types of psychedelic use.

02:23:26.569 --> 02:23:30.990
I think Aaron Rodgers would be one example
of this, although I don't want to say it's

02:23:30.990 --> 02:23:32.359
sort of in session use.

02:23:32.359 --> 02:23:34.840
It's I think more longer term implications.

02:23:34.840 --> 02:23:38.660
However, there are people, certainly I know
athletes who have used these things to enhance

02:23:38.660 --> 02:23:44.810
their perceptual faculties, which then leads
me to wonder and almost assume that neuromodulation

02:23:44.810 --> 02:23:50.850
will be used as a very hard to detect means
of performance enhancement in sports.

02:23:50.850 --> 02:23:55.050
It's hard for me to see how that would not
be the case with people who are willing to.

02:23:55.050 --> 02:23:57.150
I think there was some type of poll done at
one point.

02:23:57.150 --> 02:24:00.670
It's like, would you be willing to take a
drug that would guarantee you to get a gold

02:24:00.670 --> 02:24:03.390
medal, but it would reduce your lifespan by
five or 10 years?

02:24:03.390 --> 02:24:08.149
The answer for this thing could be all made
up, who knows?

02:24:08.149 --> 02:24:14.100
But I remember the report supposedly indicating
that the yes answer was very, very high percentage

02:24:14.100 --> 02:24:15.100
of respondents.

02:24:15.100 --> 02:24:20.930
So if they're looking at something that has
a lower risk profile and is basically going

02:24:20.930 --> 02:24:27.530
to be impossible for the world anti-doping
association to track, why wouldn't they try

02:24:27.530 --> 02:24:28.530
it?

02:24:28.530 --> 02:24:29.530
Dr. Nolan Williams: Absolutely.

02:24:29.530 --> 02:24:33.120
I'll tell you, we've had a number of patients
who've gone through and they remitted really

02:24:33.120 --> 02:24:35.819
early, so they lost all their symptoms really
early in the week.

02:24:35.819 --> 02:24:37.369
So day one or two.

02:24:37.369 --> 02:24:38.640
Then by day three, they've zeroed out.

02:24:38.640 --> 02:24:41.590
Then Thursday, day four or five, they're coming
in and they're saying, "You know what?"

02:24:41.590 --> 02:24:48.110
I remember this one guy, he was like, "I was
driving by the beach and I saw the sun setting

02:24:48.110 --> 02:24:53.330
or whatever it was, and I wanted to stop and,
for whatever reason, just sit on the beach,

02:24:53.330 --> 02:24:54.880
and I don't normally do that."

02:24:54.880 --> 02:25:01.050
Then he described how he was completely present
in the present moment and able to just be

02:25:01.050 --> 02:25:03.109
there and present and was watching the water
for an hour.

02:25:03.109 --> 02:25:06.460
He said, "I've never been able to do that
before, but I used to do these mindfulness

02:25:06.460 --> 02:25:09.229
courses that I couldn't understand, and it
fell a lot like that.

02:25:09.229 --> 02:25:13.840
I went and found my book and it sounded like
I was having this kind of totally present

02:25:13.840 --> 02:25:15.230
mindful moment."

02:25:15.230 --> 02:25:17.500
I've had a ton of folks come back and tell
me this.

02:25:17.500 --> 02:25:21.640
So if they remit really early and we keep
treating them, we treat them through and they

02:25:21.640 --> 02:25:25.240
get to this kind of more — now that we've
had folks come through for this and folks

02:25:25.240 --> 02:25:30.790
come through for psychedelic treatments, it
looks like day three, four, five out of a

02:25:30.790 --> 02:25:37.100
psilocybin experiment where the person's no
longer having any trip, but they're just calm

02:25:37.100 --> 02:25:43.200
and peaceful and pretty relaxed and present,
and it's very similar to that.

02:25:43.200 --> 02:25:47.189
So I think that you're probably getting a
similar or the same state there.

02:25:47.189 --> 02:25:51.220
I would assume a state where a lot of good
performance can happen from you really are

02:25:51.220 --> 02:25:55.180
truly in this moment not thinking about the
present or the future.

02:25:55.180 --> 02:25:59.390
I've had a lot of folks actually offer, and
at some point we need to do this, but offer

02:25:59.390 --> 02:26:05.720
various philanthropic gifts for me to run
trials on athlete performance.

02:26:05.720 --> 02:26:07.300
Tim Ferriss: I knew it.

02:26:07.300 --> 02:26:08.300
I knew it.

02:26:08.300 --> 02:26:09.300
Of course.

02:26:09.300 --> 02:26:13.650
Dr. Nolan Williams: One of our donors said
to me, "I will give you the money if you will

02:26:13.650 --> 02:26:15.800
take me and all of my group of friends."

02:26:15.800 --> 02:26:19.689
Tim Ferriss: All my post-finance guy triathlete
friends.

02:26:19.689 --> 02:26:21.720
Dr. Nolan Williams: It is basically that.

02:26:21.720 --> 02:26:26.690
We cycle every morning and randomize us to
sham our active stimulation before we get

02:26:26.690 --> 02:26:28.380
on the bikes and look at it.

02:26:28.380 --> 02:26:32.052
He's like, "The reason why this is good is
because we make the same exact times and everybody

02:26:32.052 --> 02:26:36.550
knows their times, and can you change this?"

02:26:36.550 --> 02:26:37.609
There's a little bit of evidence for this.

02:26:37.609 --> 02:26:41.210
It was a paper that was published a couple
of years ago where they took people and taught

02:26:41.210 --> 02:26:48.529
them to do complex motor tasks like hand tasks
where it's like tap digit 1, 3, 5, 1, 3, 5,

02:26:48.529 --> 02:26:54.479
1, 3, 5, and then interspersed it with 2,
4, 1, 3, 5, 2, 4, like that.

02:26:54.479 --> 02:26:57.330
It's like a complex kind of multi-step finger
task.

02:26:57.330 --> 02:27:02.640
If you prime the motor learning area before
you do that, you can cut the speed of acquisition

02:27:02.640 --> 02:27:04.800
in half compared to the people that had sham.

02:27:04.800 --> 02:27:05.960
Tim Ferriss: That's non-trivial.

02:27:05.960 --> 02:27:06.960
Seems non-trivial.

02:27:06.960 --> 02:27:07.960
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah.

02:27:07.960 --> 02:27:09.130
So it's interesting.

02:27:09.130 --> 02:27:15.470
There's some really early preliminary data
to suggest that you could potentially improve

02:27:15.470 --> 02:27:17.010
performance with neurostim.

02:27:17.010 --> 02:27:20.330
The thing about it is that if you could pull
something — if you really could have something

02:27:20.330 --> 02:27:26.819
that people have been thinking about TMS as
a treatment for insomnia, others for acute

02:27:26.819 --> 02:27:27.819
anxiety.

02:27:27.819 --> 02:27:30.980
If you could come up with something that you
could kind of put the brakes on a couple of

02:27:30.980 --> 02:27:34.310
different symptomatologies and you could make
— maybe it's a TMS device, maybe it's another

02:27:34.310 --> 02:27:35.310
technology.

02:27:35.310 --> 02:27:38.380
You can make it something you could bring
home, then you'd have the ability to have

02:27:38.380 --> 02:27:43.320
this kind of full service sort of process
for dealing with things.

02:27:43.320 --> 02:27:47.050
I think trying to treat depression and isolation
or something like that, you're never going

02:27:47.050 --> 02:27:50.560
to be able to scale, I think one treatment
alone.

02:27:50.560 --> 02:27:54.930
But if we get to a place where we can use
this for a lot of different functions and

02:27:54.930 --> 02:27:59.090
actually the hypnotizability stuff, drive
people up to be able to receive information

02:27:59.090 --> 02:28:03.950
better or study better or whatever, do motor
tasks better and then turn it off and flip

02:28:03.950 --> 02:28:05.530
it on to sleep mode.

02:28:05.530 --> 02:28:10.030
You had a level of more control over your
brain than just your own volition.

02:28:10.030 --> 02:28:14.300
It's your volition plus your volition to do
these things, then I think it's very interesting.

02:28:14.300 --> 02:28:16.010
It's also very sci-fi though.

02:28:16.010 --> 02:28:18.220
We are not anywhere close to knowing we can
do that yet.

02:28:18.220 --> 02:28:19.220
Tim Ferriss: Not anywhere close.

02:28:19.220 --> 02:28:20.220
Yeah, not anywhere close yet.

02:28:20.220 --> 02:28:21.600
But fun food for thought at the very least.

02:28:21.600 --> 02:28:22.870
Nolan, one more time.

02:28:22.870 --> 02:28:25.439
Where can people find your lab online?

02:28:25.439 --> 02:28:27.620
Dr. Nolan Williams: So it's bsl.stanford.edu.

02:28:27.620 --> 02:28:31.590
It's the Stanford Brain Stimulation Lab.

02:28:31.590 --> 02:28:33.130
Tim Ferriss: Great.

02:28:33.130 --> 02:28:34.860
Any other websites you'd like to point people
to?

02:28:34.860 --> 02:28:37.450
Dr. Nolan Williams: No, I think that one's
the place to go.

02:28:37.450 --> 02:28:38.500
Tim Ferriss: That's home base.

02:28:38.500 --> 02:28:42.859
Anything else you'd like to say before we
wind to a close?

02:28:42.859 --> 02:28:43.880
Any comments?

02:28:43.880 --> 02:28:44.880
Public complaints?

02:28:44.880 --> 02:28:47.930
Dr. Nolan Williams: No, it's been super fun.

02:28:47.930 --> 02:28:52.689
I think you've definitely gotten yourself
quite up to speed and kind of right in the

02:28:52.689 --> 02:28:56.859
center of a lot of the pulse of this and both
on the neurostim side and the —

02:28:56.859 --> 02:28:58.060
Tim Ferriss: Psychedelic pulse.

02:28:58.060 --> 02:28:59.550
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, yeah.

02:28:59.550 --> 02:29:04.021
So appreciate the knowledge coming in and
your interests, and I appreciate the ability

02:29:04.021 --> 02:29:06.250
to have a conversation around these topics.

02:29:06.250 --> 02:29:09.050
Tim Ferriss: Thank you for saying that.

02:29:09.050 --> 02:29:12.800
I really have enjoyed delving into this field.

02:29:12.800 --> 02:29:18.510
You've been incredibly helpful as a resource
and a sanity check since I get all excited

02:29:18.510 --> 02:29:24.660
about things and sometimes can fly off the
rails, but it's been so much fun to engage

02:29:24.660 --> 02:29:30.640
with this burgeoning, I think hopefully soon
to be dramatically expanded, field of experimentation,

02:29:30.640 --> 02:29:36.180
especially given the remission rates and the
durability.

02:29:36.180 --> 02:29:41.720
The reason I first began exploring this was
I saw a friend's family completely transformed,

02:29:41.720 --> 02:29:44.460
and the before and after was just truly unbelievable.

02:29:44.460 --> 02:29:47.399
One of the most unbelievable transformations
I've ever seen in my life.

02:29:47.399 --> 02:29:48.399
It happened quickly.

02:29:48.399 --> 02:29:50.550
I think it was about day three.

02:29:50.550 --> 02:29:56.910
Many, many failed interventions, really critical
situation, lots of self — harm, and it was

02:29:56.910 --> 02:30:02.180
just like Ctrl Z undo and back to the person
they used to be.

02:30:02.180 --> 02:30:06.340
It's been durable with, I want to say, let's
call them single day boosters, maybe once

02:30:06.340 --> 02:30:08.359
a quarter or once every six months.

02:30:08.359 --> 02:30:12.130
I think it's now been durable, I want to say
probably a year and a half, which is just

02:30:12.130 --> 02:30:13.130
phenomenal.

02:30:13.130 --> 02:30:14.130
Dr. Nolan Williams: Cool.

02:30:14.130 --> 02:30:15.640
Tim Ferriss: So I appreciate the work you
do.

02:30:15.640 --> 02:30:23.510
I appreciate you being the last man standing
on the scientific bachelorette, and I suspect

02:30:23.510 --> 02:30:24.510
that'll happen again.

02:30:24.510 --> 02:30:26.681
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, probably.

02:30:26.681 --> 02:30:29.939
Tim Ferriss: Thanks for taking the time for
the conversation, man.

02:30:29.939 --> 02:30:30.939
Dr. Nolan Williams: Yeah, thank you.

02:30:30.939 --> 02:30:32.500
Tim Ferriss: Look forward to watching what
you do in the future.

02:30:32.500 --> 02:30:36.420
And for everybody listening, we will link
to everything we discussed in the show notes,

02:30:36.420 --> 02:30:38.729
including Nolan's lab at tim.blog/podcast.

02:30:38.729 --> 02:30:45.010
Until next time, be a little bit kinder than
is necessary to others and to yourself, and

02:30:45.010 --> 02:30:46.010
thanks for tuning in.

